This network is centered on Plant A, supported by a Logistics Center, with MetalFab Inc., Memory Star, and GlobalComponents feeding the operation and NXP Semiconductors plus Broadcom Limited buying the finished laptops. The product structure is fairly material-intensive: each lens_assembly uses 1 unit of epoxy_compound, 1 unit of gold_wire, and 3 units of aluminum_sheet, while each laptop requires 3 units of aluminum_sheet, 1 unit of gold_wire, 3 units of epoxy_compound, and 2 units of lens_assembly. On the inbound side, epoxy_compound is supplied in periodic lots of 61317.0 units, with deliveries arriving every 10.0 to 16.0 days, sourced from MetalFab Inc. at $236.599 per unit. MetalFab runs on a fixed 27.0-day lead time, offers 36.0-day payment terms, and can supply up to 718209 units per order; shipping from MetalFab Inc. to Plant A takes between 2.0 and 11.0 days. Gold_wire is also replenished on a repeating schedule, but the batch size is more variable, averaging 22124.0 units with variability of about 5273.0 units, and arrivals come every 8.0 to 9.0 days. Memory Star charges $221.904 per unit, delivers in 11.0 to 28.0 days, allows payment in 9.0 to 83.0 days, and is capped at 580363 units per delivery; the move from Memory Star to Plant A is a consistent 8.0-day transfer. Gold_wire carries a holding cost of $8.553 per unit and is reviewed every 27 days. Aluminum_sheet is handled more reactively, ordered as needed based on demand rather than on a fixed cycle. GlobalComponents supplies it with a reliable 3.0-day lead time at $355.416 per unit, with payment due 57.0 days after receipt and a maximum order size of 881899 units. At Plant A, aluminum_sheet has a holding cost of $9.365 per unit, a shortage penalty of $3.7 per unit, and a 4-day review cycle. The plant also keeps 3104958 units of lens_assembly on hand initially, with holding cost set at $6.063 per unit, shortage cost at $19.56 per unit, and reviews every 1 day. Finished laptop inventory starts at 1551657 units, with a holding charge of $1.16 per unit, shortage cost of $9.73 per unit, and another 1-day review rhythm. Production at Plant A is modeled as an inspection step that consumes aluminum_sheet, gold_wire, epoxy_compound, and lens_assembly to produce laptops, and each cycle takes between 8.0 and 21.0 days. The site also operates a Laser Cutter with capacity for 3 units, a service time averaging 8.718 days, operating cost of $19.351 per day, and reliability behavior that is pretty realistic: uptime averages 459.339 days before a failure and downtime averages 12.692 days when it goes down. Outbound demand is split across two major customers. NXP Semiconductors places laptop orders every 16.0 to 26.0 days, with each order ranging from 568255.0 to 1395792.0 units, expects delivery in 9.0 to 10.0 days, pays $4385.084 per unit, and settles invoices 76.0 days later; if stock is unavailable, the sale is lost. Broadcom Limited orders less regularly, with arrivals averaging 34.0 days, but each order is exactly 4058162.0 units. Its customer lead time is fixed at 26.0 days, the selling price is $2902.678 per unit, and payment comes in 30.0 to 68.0 days; under shortages, available units are shipped and the rest of the demand is lost. Material flow is straightforward: all three suppliers connect into Plant A, and completed laptops move from Plant A to the Logistics Center. The model runs over a 365-day horizon with 1 replication.

