This setup centers on making the drone from a single input, aluminum_sheet, with PrecisionParts feeding the plant and HP Enterprise buying the finished output. At the Fab manufacturing site, 1 unit of aluminum_sheet is converted into 1 unit of drone through the molding step, and that production cycle varies uniformly between 8 hours and 27 hours. The raw aluminum_sheet stock is controlled with a min-max style reorder approach: once inventory drops below 3698.57 units, it is replenished up to 11320.7 units. Keeping that material on hand costs $7.897 per unit, and it is reviewed every 28 hours. PrecisionParts supplies the aluminum_sheet with a lead time ranging from 14.0 hours to 27.0 hours, charges $272.544 per unit, offers payment terms anywhere from 30.0 days to 84.0 days after receipt, and can provide up to 534795 units per order. On the finished-goods side, drone inventory starts at 3870559 units, carries a holding cost of $1.612 per unit, is reviewed every 1 hour, and faces a shortage cost of $35.758 per unit when demand cannot be filled immediately. Output from Fab moves on to the Logistics Center warehouse before serving HP Enterprise. That customer places demand exactly every 36.0 hours, with each order falling between 691884.0 units and 1453214.0 units, and shipments to the customer take exactly 4.0 hours. If stock runs short, available drone units are shipped right away and the remaining balance is backordered. Each drone sells for $4993.431 per unit, and HP Enterprise pays exactly 89.0 days after delivery. The network is straightforward: PrecisionParts sends aluminum_sheet to Fab, Fab produces drone, and the finished units flow to the Logistics Center. The model runs for a horizon of 200 hours with 10 replications.

