 is reviewed every 11 hours. Manufacturing happens at the Production Center in two separate flows. The fabrication step turns silicon_wafer, gold_wire, and epoxy_compound into drone output in exactly 1.0 hour per cycle, and the center manages inventory for drone, gold_wire, epoxy_compound, and silicon_wafer. The cutting step uses epoxy_compound, silicon_wafer, and gold_wire to make router units, with each cycle taking between 9.0 hours and 26.0 hours, and that same Production Center also manages router, silicon_wafer, gold_wire, and epoxy_compound inventory. There is also a Robot Arm available with capacity for 1 unit at a time, a service time ranging from 0.382 hours to 4.501 hours, and an operating cost of $16.214 per hour. On the finished goods side, drone inventory starts at 3446267 units, with a holding cost of $9.567 per unit and review every 1 hour. Router inventory opens at 875730 units. Demand comes from two commercial customers. Apple Supply buys drones, placing orders with exponentially distributed interarrival times averaging 23.0 hours, and each order asks for between 1667615.0 units and 2239988.0 units. Customer lead time is a fixed 29.0 hours, any shortfall is backordered rather than lost, the selling price is $4334.185 per unit, and payment is received 79.0 days after delivery. Schneider Electric purchases routers with order arrivals averaging 50.0 hours, order sizes between 1002752.0 units and 1512880.0 units, and a fixed customer lead time of 18.0 hours. Router shortages are also backordered, the selling price is $1722.222 per unit, and customer payment comes between 18.0 days and 75.0 days after shipment. The network itself is simple: Process Go., GlobalComponents, and FastChip Corp. connect into the Production Center, and the Production Center feeds the Fulfillment Hub for both drone and router distribution. The model runs in hours over a 200-hour horizon, with 1 replication, and results are collected after a 50-hour warm-up period.

