nits, and taking between 8.0 days and 9.0 days to transfer material to the site. Epoxy_compound is controlled separately, with replenishment triggered below 1717.97 units and topped back up to 3107.7 units; its shortage cost is $19.235 per unit and review happens every 15 days. That material comes from MetalFab Inc. at $81.791 per unit, with supply lead time averaging 3.0 days, payment terms ranging from 35.0 days to 87.0 days, and a delivery limit of 6201 units per order. Inventory positions are quite large for the internally handled items: battery_pack starts at 3750449 units with a holding cost of $0.283 per unit, a shortage cost of $23.706 per unit, and daily review every 1 day; FPGA opens at 4803444 units with holding at $8.362 per unit, shortage at $16.171 per unit, and review every 1 day; camera begins at 4802631 units with holding cost of $3.269 per unit, shortage cost of $37.886 per unit, and review every 1 day. Finished FPGA moves from Manufacturing Site to Fulfillment Hub, and camera moves from Pilot Line to Fulfillment Hub. Customer demand is split across two accounts. Lenovo Group buys FPGA, placing orders somewhere between every 7.0 days and 49.0 days, with demand centered around 4670360.0 units and varying by about 133953.0 units. The promised customer lead time is exactly 14.0 days, the selling price is $1003.721 per unit, payment comes 42.0 days after delivery, and if stock is unavailable the sale is lost. Schneider Electric buys camera on a steadier schedule, ordering every 40.0 days for 2766813.0 units, with delivery committed in 29.0 days. Camera sells at $3744.074 per unit, cash is received 54.0 days later, and the policy is partial fulfillment: available units ship, while the remainder is lost. Altogether, the network links BatteryTech, MetalFab Inc., and ElectroParts into Manufacturing Site, Pilot Line, and Fulfillment Hub, with 1 replication used for the run.

