This network revolves around three incoming materials—copper_wire, epoxy_compound, and steel_rod—feeding an Assembly Hub that makes two sellable items, tablet and FPGA, with a Fulfillment Hub handling outbound flow. One in-house built sub-assembly, sensor_array, is also part of the mix: each sensor_array uses 4 units of steel_rod, 4 units of copper_wire, and 1 unit of epoxy_compound. The tablet itself is made from 1 unit of epoxy_compound and 4 units of copper_wire, while each FPGA requires 4 units of steel_rod plus 3 units of sensor_array. Copper_wire is sourced from Memory Star on a demand-driven basis, with supply arriving in a fixed 13.0 days, at a supplier price of $6.432 per unit, and payment due between 18.0 days and 44.0 days after receipt. Internally, copper_wire carries a holding cost of $4.059 per unit, a shortage cost of $12.34 per unit, and is reviewed every 9 days. Epoxy_compound comes from MetalFab Inc. under a min-max style replenishment rule: when stock drops below 890.21 units, it is brought back up to 1208.58 units. MetalFab Inc. delivers in exactly 29.0 days, charges $456.405 per unit, allows 35.0-day to 60.0-day payment terms, and can supply up to 282213 units per order; the transfer from MetalFab Inc. to Assembly Hub takes 1.0 day. Steel_rod is purchased from PolymerSource through scheduled deliveries, with batch sizes varying uniformly between 59151.0 units and 97097.0 units and arrivals every 14.0 to 23.0 days. PolymerSource has a consistent 25.0-day lead time, charges $6.89 per unit, requires payment 23.0 days after delivery, and has a maximum order size of 705593 units; moving steel_rod into Assembly Hub takes 4.0 days. Inside inventory, steel_rod has a holding cost of $4.947 per unit, a shortage penalty of $38.026 per unit, and a 5-day review cycle. The plant starts with 4922023 units of sensor_array on hand, with carrying cost at $7.815 per unit and review every 1 day. Finished goods inventory begins at 1147541 units for tablet and 4252803 units for FPGA. Tablet stock is checked every 1 day and costs $9.04 per unit to hold, while FPGA is also reviewed every 1 day, with holding cost of $0.877 per unit and shortage cost of $49.828 per unit. Production at Assembly Hub happens through 2 manufacturing steps listed under the same site name. The cutting operation converts epoxy_compound and copper_wire into tablet, and that step takes exactly 27.0 days per cycle. The molding operation turns steel_rod and sensor_array into FPGA, and that cycle is a much shorter 7.0 days. There is also a Robot Arm resource in the system with capacity for 4 units, a service time ranging from 4.724 days to 6.882 days, and an operating cost of $5.219 per day. On the demand side, TechCorp buys tablet with orders arriving every 22.0 days, each order fixed at 3830745.0 units. Customer lead time is 12.0 days, the selling price is $2737.878 per unit, and payment is received 9.0 days later. If stock is short, available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered. Schneider Electric purchases FPGA with order arrivals following an exponential pattern averaging 59.0 days. Its order volume is normally centered on 2962298.0 units with variability of 285922.0 units, customer lead time is 16.0 days, the selling price is $14.085 per unit, and cash is collected 27.0 days after delivery. For shortages, the business ships what it has and loses the rest. In terms of physical flow, Memory Star, MetalFab Inc., and PolymerSource all feed the Assembly Hub, and both tablet and FPGA move onward from Assembly Hub to Fulfillment Hub. The model runs for 100 days total, with results taken after a 30-day warm-up period, across 10 replications.

