Raw material purchasing is largely triggered by actual demand rather than fixed replenishment cycles. Carbon_fiber is sourced from NanoMaterials Inc. at $324.482 per unit, with lead times varying uniformly between 9.0 weeks and 17.0 weeks, a delivery cap of 239661 units per order, and payment due exactly 52.0 days after delivery. The shipment from NanoMaterials Inc. to the Integration Center takes 1.0 weeks. Carbon_fiber carrying cost runs at $6.533 per unit, shortage cost is $49.474 per unit, and stock is reviewed every 13 weeks. Silicon_wafer comes from MegaSupply at $303.277 per unit, with an exponential lead time averaging 15.0 weeks, capacity up to 715517 units per delivery, and payment terms ranging from 12.0 days to 68.0 days. Its holding cost is $5.205 per unit and review happens every 2 weeks. Microprocessor supply comes from ElectroParts at $279.12 per unit, with lead time somewhere between 4.0 weeks and 24.0 weeks, a maximum order size of 494848 units, and net 11.0 days payment.

Inside the operation, sensor_array stock starts high at 4702928 units, with a holding cost of $7.61 per unit, a shortage penalty of $38.733 per unit, and review every 1 week. Finished FPGA inventory begins at 2201795 units, carries at $6.82 per unit, faces a shortage cost of $12.149 per unit, and is also checked every 1 week. Assembly is performed at the Integration Center using the Welding Station, which has capacity for 5 units at a time. That resource runs on a fixed 7.857-week service time and costs $18.326 per week to operate. The FPGA assembly step itself takes between 12.0 weeks and 13.0 weeks, using microprocessor, sensor_array, carbon_fiber, and silicon_wafer as inputs to produce FPGA output.

On the demand side, Ross Associates buys FPGA units with order arrivals ranging from 13.0 weeks to 54.0 weeks and order sizes between 1240277.0 units and 1752361.0 units. Customer lead time is a consistent 25.0 weeks, the selling price is $3147.143 per unit, and payment is received between 44.0 days and 72.0 days later. If stock is short, available units are shipped and the rest of the order is lost. Siemens Digital also purchases FPGA units, with arrivals between 31.0 weeks and 54.0 weeks, a fixed demand of 3351482.0 units per order, and a delivery commitment of 24.0 weeks. Its price point is much lower at $111.592 per unit, and cash comes in between 37.0 days and 70.0 days after shipment. For this customer, whatever is available ships immediately and the remaining quantity is backordered.

Overall, the structure is straightforward: 3 suppliers feed 2 facilities, the Integration Center manufactures and hands FPGA units over to the Logistics Center, and the model is evaluated over a 200-week horizon with 10 replications.

