.727 weeks and 2.501 weeks, and an operating cost of $3.158 per week. Finished inventory starts very high, with 3688714 units of SoC on hand and 4503323 units of FPGA. Holding SoC costs $7.68 per unit, shortages cost $18.226 per unit, and stock is reviewed every 1 week; FPGA holding runs at $5.031 per unit with the same 1 week review cycle. Demand comes from 2 customers. TechCorp buys SoC, placing orders every 58.0 weeks, with demand centered around 712187.0 units and varying by about 139095.0 units. Customer delivery for TechCorp takes exactly 18.0 weeks, the selling price is $1392.001 per unit, and cash is collected between 25.0 weeks and 67.0 weeks later. If SoC inventory is short, whatever is available is shipped and the remainder is lost. Infineon Technologies purchases FPGA, with order arrivals anywhere from 9.0 weeks to 41.0 weeks apart and a fixed demand of 2922087.0 units each time. FPGA orders are delivered in exactly 22.0 weeks, sold at $4112.165 per unit, and paid between 23.0 weeks and 77.0 weeks after shipment; any unfilled quantity is backordered rather than lost. In structural terms, the node setup links GlassCore Ltd., PrecisionParts, and Process Go. to the Integration Center and Storage Facility, and both SoC and FPGA flow from the Integration Center into storage. The model runs over a planning horizon of 100 weeks with 1 replication.

