peration named cutting, where frame_structure, gold_wire, epoxy_compound, and plastic_pellet are converted into smartphone output. That operation takes between 3.0 days and 13.0 days per cycle. The shop also uses an Injection Molder with capacity for 3 units at a time; its processing time is exponential with an average of 3.899 days. Batching is turned on, so jobs are grouped into batches of 19 units and can wait up to 9.02 days before release. The machine is not perfectly reliable either: it stays up for 322.48 days, then goes down for 42.149 days, both on a fixed pattern, and it costs $3.345 per day to run. On the demand side, Siemens Digital buys smartphone units with orders arriving somewhere between 26.0 days and 58.0 days apart, and each order is for 4001722.0 units. Customer delivery lead time ranges from 8.0 days to 25.0 days, the selling price is $2868.027 per unit, and payment comes 51.0 days later. If stock is short, available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered. Lenovo Group also purchases smartphone units, but its demand arrives exponentially with a mean of 32.0 days, with each order fixed at 3260036.0 units. Lenovo’s shipments go out in exactly 1.0 day, the price is $1992.619 per unit, and receivables are collected 17.0 days after delivery; in this case, any unfilled demand is fully backordered. The modeled network therefore has 3 suppliers, 2 facilities, 1 manufacturing site, 1 warehouse, and a 730-day planning horizon, with 1 replication in the run.

