t A, silicon_wafer and microprocessor are converted into GPU in exactly 9.0 days. In a separate assembly step at the same plant, display_panel, memory_chip, and microprocessor are turned into industrial_sensor in anywhere from 11.0 days to 15.0 days. Finished goods then move from Plant A to the Distribution Center, which serves as the warehouse in this setup. Customer demand comes from 2 accounts. Samsung Electronics buys GPU, placing orders with exponentially distributed interarrival times averaging 51.0 days. Order size is normally centered around 101653.0 units, varying by about 5357.0 units. Delivery to Samsung takes 17.0 days, the selling price is $2349.495 per unit, and payment arrives 31.0 days later. If stock is short, available units are shipped immediately and the remainder is backordered. Schneider Electric purchases industrial_sensor, ordering every 41.0 days, with demand ranging from 910866.0 units to 1997249.0 units. Customer lead time is 19.0 days, the sales price is $4996.284 per unit, and payment comes between 50.0 days and 65.0 days after delivery. This account follows a full backorder approach, so unmet demand waits until inventory is replenished. Structurally, the network links 3 suppliers—Process Go., ElectroParts, and Memory Star—to 2 internal nodes, Plant A and the Distribution Center. The model runs for 365 days, with results collected after a 50-day warm-up period, across 5 replications.

