cal_device. Holding tablet inventory costs $1.68 per unit with a 1-week review cadence, and shortages carry a penalty of $38.532 per unit; cooling_unit holding cost is $4.82 per unit and is also reviewed every 1 week. Customer demand is split between TechCorp and Apple Supply. TechCorp buys tablet, placing orders every 7.0 weeks for exactly 1492455.0 units, expecting delivery in between 8.0 weeks and 21.0 weeks. Tablets sell at $2801.968 per unit, payment comes 9.0 days after delivery, and if stock is short, available units are shipped and the rest of the demand is lost. Apple Supply purchases medical_device with orders arriving exponentially at an average of 10.0 weeks, in quantities ranging from 1157665.0 units to 3529961.0 units. Its delivery promise is a fixed 25.0 weeks, the selling price is $1899.083 per unit, payment arrives 20.0 days after delivery, and it follows the same partial-fill, lost-remainder policy. In terms of physical flow, SiliconWorks and MegaSupply feed the plants, tablet moves from Advanced Fab to the Distribution Center, and medical_device moves from Plant A to the Distribution Center. The model runs for a 100-week horizon, with results collected after a 30-week warm-up period, across 5 replications.

