h processing time ranging from 8.0 hours to 29.0 hours. A second operation at the same Integration Center, bonding, converts motor_assembly into EV_battery in anywhere from 4.0 hours to 26.0 hours. The Integration Center manages lithium_cell, microprocessor, motor_assembly, and router inventory in one setup, and motor_assembly plus EV_battery in another, while the Fulfillment Hub serves as the warehouse receiving both router and EV_battery from the plant. On the demand side, HP Enterprise buys router on a regular 32.0-hour cycle, requesting exactly 2636390.0 units each time. Customer delivery for that business falls in a 10.0-hour to 26.0-hour range, the selling price is $2080.864 per unit, and payment comes 10.0 hours after delivery; if stock runs short, available units are shipped and the balance is lost. NXP Semiconductors orders EV_battery every 55.0 hours, with demand centered around 216516.0 units and varying by about 9735.0 units. Its delivery promise is a fixed 28.0 hours, the sales price is $500.697 per unit, and customer payment lands between 27.0 hours and 30.0 hours after delivery; if inventory is insufficient, whatever is available ships immediately and the rest is backordered. Structurally, Memory Star, PolymerSource, and MetalFab Inc. all feed the Integration Center, and the Integration Center sends both router and EV_battery onward to the Fulfillment Hub. The model runs in hours over a 100-hour horizon, with 10 replications.

