bon_fiber, battery_pack, and copper_wire for the testing step, where battery_pack, carbon_fiber, and copper_wire are turned into camera in a cycle ranging from 3.0 to 27.0 hours. The same Assembly Hub also manages GPU, plastic_pellet, and copper_wire for coating, and that process takes a fixed 6.0 hours to turn plastic_pellet and copper_wire into GPU. There is also a Welding Station with capacity for 1 unit at a time, a service time between 3.866 and 5.053 hours, batching enabled in lots of 27 units, a maximum wait of 4.316 hours, and an operating cost of $17.365 per hour. On the inventory side, battery_pack starts with 2482151 units on hand, carries a holding cost of $2.785 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 hour. Camera begins with 4178104 units, has a shortage cost of $24.052 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 hour. GPU opens with 2132451 units in stock. Demand for camera comes from Infineon Technologies every 25.0 hours, with order sizes ranging from 204099.0 units to 1695018.0 units. Customers are promised shipment in 26.0 hours, the selling price is $4867.022 per unit, and cash is collected 17.0 days later. If inventory is short, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. Bosch Automotive buys GPU, with orders arriving anywhere from 8.0 to 41.0 hours apart and a fixed demand of 4474777.0 units each time. GPU customer lead time runs from 7.0 to 23.0 hours, the selling price is $1522.366 per unit, and payment comes 41.0 days after delivery; this is also a partial-fill, lost-remainder arrangement. The model runs for a horizon of 365 hours, and results are measured after a warm-up period of 30 hours.

