This network centers on a Pilot Line that turns aluminum_sheet and glass_fiber into two sellable items, camera and medical_device, with a Regional Depot handling finished-goods storage downstream. There is also an in-house built sub-assembly called frame_structure, which uses 5 units of glass_fiber and 5 units of aluminum_sheet for each unit produced. Each camera then requires 1 unit of frame_structure plus another 3 units of aluminum_sheet and 1 unit of glass_fiber, while each medical_device is built from 3 units of glass_fiber and 4 units of aluminum_sheet. On the sourcing side, aluminum_sheet is brought in on an as-needed basis, and it can come from PolymerSource or MegaSupply. PolymerSource ships aluminum_sheet to the Pilot Line with a lead time somewhere between 14.0 hours and 24.0 hours, charges $147.358 per unit, and is paid between 50.0 days and 63.0 days after delivery. MegaSupply also feeds aluminum_sheet into the Pilot Line, but faster, in a 2.0-hour to 11.0-hour range, at $67.57 per unit, with payment terms ranging from 46.0 days to 78.0 days. Glass_fiber is supplied by BatteryTech to the Pilot Line with an exponential lead time averaging 13.0 hours, a unit cost of $170.677, and payment due between 53.0 days and 56.0 days after receipt; BatteryTech can supply up to 234082 units per order. Internally, glass_fiber is managed with a min-max approach that triggers replenishment when stock drops below 758.21 units and restores it to 2626.5 units. Holding glass_fiber costs $2.494 per unit, shortages cost $6.951 per unit, and stock is reviewed every 29 hours. Aluminum_sheet carries a holding cost of $0.191 per unit and is reviewed every 10 hours. The frame_structure stock starts at 1660338 units, with holding cost of $3.883 per unit, shortage cost of $0.766 per unit, and hourly review every 1 hour. Finished inventory begins with 548034 units of camera and 489148 units of medical_device; for medical_device, holding cost is $7.391 per unit and the inventory position is checked every 1 hour. Production happens in the Pilot Line through 2 manufacturing steps recorded under the same site name. The fabrication activity uses frame_structure, aluminum_sheet, and glass_fiber to make camera, and its cycle time varies uniformly between 14.0 hours and 28.0 hours. The welding activity consumes glass_fiber and aluminum_sheet to produce medical_device, and that process takes exactly 22.0 hours each cycle. Once completed, both camera and medical_device move from the Pilot Line to the Regional Depot. On the demand side, Lenovo Group buys camera on a steady rhythm every 7.0 hours, ordering exactly 3756425.0 units each time. Customers are served in 15.0 hours, unmet demand is backordered, the selling price is $2227.056 per unit, and Lenovo Group pays between 23.0 days and 53.0 days later. Bosch Automotive purchases medical_device every 12.0 hours, with order volume ranging from 2511962.0 units to 3761881.0 units, and expects delivery in 27.0 hours. If stock is unavailable, the sale is lost, and the product sells at $3809.412 per unit with customer payment arriving between 46.0 days and 57.0 days. The modeled network explicitly links PolymerSource, BatteryTech, and MegaSupply to the Pilot Line, and then routes both finished products from the Pilot Line to the Regional Depot. The scenario runs for a horizon of 100 hours with 5 replications.

