This network makes cameras and solar panels using memory_chip and resin, with circuit_board serving as a built-up sub-component along the way. The supplier side is straightforward: PolymerSource feeds memory_chip to the Pilot Line on an as-needed basis, with a fixed 24.0 weeks of lead time, a purchase cost of $242.65 per unit, and payment due 31.0 days after delivery. PrecisionParts provides resin to the same Pilot Line, and that material is replenished on a regular schedule every 44316.0 weeks, with deliveries arriving 10.0 weeks later; its lead time averages 13.0 weeks, the resin costs $328.347 per unit, and payment terms run anywhere from 35.0 days to 73.0 days. To build each circuit_board, the operation consumes 2 units of resin and 5 units of memory_chip. Finished camera production is heavier: each camera requires 3 units of memory_chip, 2 units of resin, and 4 units of circuit_board, while each solar_panel uses 2 units of circuit_board. Inventory is substantial, with 196249 units of circuit_board on hand initially, 2000406 units of camera in stock at the start, and 2011807 units of solar_panel available initially. The circuit_board inventory is reviewed every 1 week, with a holding charge of $6.959 per unit and a shortage penalty of $6.315 per unit, while solar_panel stock is also checked every 1 week and carries $8.795 per unit in holding cost plus a $15.852 per unit shortage cost. Manufacturing is split across two plants: the Pilot Line manages circuit_board, resin, memory_chip, and camera, and runs a coating step that turns memory_chip, resin, and circuit_board into camera in anywhere from 7.0 to 14.0 weeks; Advanced Fab handles circuit_board and solar_panel, using a fabrication step that converts circuit_board into solar_panel in exactly 19.0 weeks. A Logistics Center acts as the warehouse receiving camera from the Pilot Line and solar_panel from Advanced Fab. The shared shop-floor resource is a Conveyor Belt with capacity for 2 units, a fixed service time of 3.101 weeks, batching turned on at 63 units with a maximum wait of 4.629 weeks, and a reliability pattern of 412.401 weeks up followed by 45.922 weeks down; it also costs $16.649 per week to operate. On the demand side, Apple Supply buys camera with orders arriving every 51.0 weeks, each for 4770406.0 units, and expects delivery in 13.0 weeks; sales are made at $2597.445 per unit, customer orders are fully backordered if stock is unavailable, and payment comes in 39.0 to 46.0 days after shipment. Sony Electronics buys solar_panel with order arrivals ranging from 34.0 to 44.0 weeks, constant demand of 995200.0 units per order, and a 1.0-week customer lead time; the selling price is $4954.557 per unit, available units ship immediately while the remainder is backordered, and cash is collected in 35.0 to 41.0 days. Overall, the node structure links PolymerSource and PrecisionParts to the Pilot Line, with finished cameras and solar panels moving on to the Logistics Center, and the model runs for 730 weeks with the first 50 weeks treated as warm-up; results are based on 10 replications.

