y Hub and medical_device from the Processing Unit. The shared Assembly Line has capacity for 2 units at a time, works with a service time ranging from 5.432 hours to 6.451 hours, groups work into batches of 95 units, waits up to 14.936 hours to form a batch, and costs $4.542 per hour to run. Finished stock starts very high, with 4374010 units of router inventory and 2500756 units of medical_device inventory on hand. Customer demand is also substantial. HP Enterprise buys router units, placing orders between every 34.0 hours and 54.0 hours, with order sizes ranging from 1571504.0 units to 4760362.0 units. Router shipments are promised in exactly 20.0 hours and sold at $4881.117 per unit. If stock is short, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost, and customer payment comes between 12.0 days and 64.0 days later. NXP Semiconductors purchases medical_device, ordering every 32.0 hours flat, with demand between 2568906.0 units and 3086885.0 units per order. Delivery to that customer takes between 9.0 hours and 19.0 hours, the selling price is $4095.895 per unit, and any uncovered portion is backordered after available units ship immediately. Payment from NXP Semiconductors is received between 9.0 days and 24.0 days after delivery. Overall, the model links 3 suppliers—MetalFab Inc., PrecisionParts, and CarbonSupply Co.—to 3 facilities—Assembly Hub, Processing Unit, and Fulfillment Hub—and it is evaluated over a 365-hour horizon, with results counted after a 30-hour warm-up period and 10 replications.

