y_compound goes to Production Center in exactly 9.0 days; Process Go. also sends lithium_cell to Production Center. At Production Center, the testing step converts power_module and lithium_cell into GPU in a cycle ranging from 3.0 days to 9.0 days, and the site manages lithium_cell, power_module, and GPU inventory. Pilot Line manages epoxy_compound and camera inventory and runs a polishing step that turns epoxy_compound into camera in a fixed 19.0-day cycle. The shop floor also includes an Injection Molder with capacity for 3 units, a fixed service time of 3.741 days, batching enabled in lots of 12 units, a maximum wait of 19.365 days before a batch is released, and an operating cost of $19.397 per day. Once completed, GPU moves from Production Center to Logistics Center, and camera moves from Pilot Line to Logistics Center. Customer demand comes from Apple Supply for GPU and TechCorp for camera. Apple Supply places orders every 34.0 days, with demand varying uniformly between 311734.0 units and 4955804.0 units, expects delivery in 9.0 days, pays between 30.0 days and 54.0 days later, and buys at $3404.815 per unit; if stock is short, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. TechCorp orders camera somewhere between 28.0 days and 31.0 days apart, requests a steady 187109.0 units each time, accepts an 18.0-day customer lead time, pays in 36.0 days, and purchases at $1284.25 per unit; any unfilled demand is backordered. The model runs for 730 days, with results collected after a 50-day warm-up period, using 1 replication.

