ewed every 6 days. On the production side, the Processing Unit runs a molding step that turns resin and copper_wire into laptops in exactly 3.0 days per cycle, using the Welding Station. That resource has capacity for 4 units, a fixed service time of 7.301 days, runs for 327.545 days before failure on average in this setup, and when it goes down the repair time averages 6.066 days; operating it costs $16.295 per day. The same Processing Unit also performs an assembly step that converts heat_sink and lithium_cell into solar_panel, and each assembly cycle takes exactly 11.0 days. Inventory starts quite high for some items: 3205587 units of heat_sink, 482364 units of laptop, and 4286979 units of solar_panel. Heat_sink inventory is reviewed every 1 day, with a holding charge of $1.556 per unit and a shortage cost of $48.926 per unit. Laptop stock is also checked every 1 day and carries a holding cost of $7.119 per unit. Demand comes from 2 customers. Ross Associates buys laptops, placing orders every 31.0 to 32.0 days, with demand centered around 1126905.0 units and varying by 131862.0 units. Laptop orders are promised in exactly 13.0 days, sold at $2015.053 per unit, and customer payment arrives between 30.0 days and 74.0 days; if stock is unavailable, the sale is lost. Lenovo Group buys solar_panels, with order arrivals between 28.0 days and 50.0 days and demand averaging 4175565.0 units with variability of 421803.0 units. Solar_panel orders are delivered in exactly 1.0 day, sold at $2929.298 per unit, and paid between 50.0 days and 79.0 days later, again under a lost-sales policy. The model runs in days over a 200-day horizon, with 10 replications.

