aptop in a cycle that varies uniformly between 1.0 days and 7.0 days. In a separate inspection step, the same Assembly Hub handles microprocessor, camera, and copper_wire, converting copper_wire and microprocessor into camera with a fixed 20.0-day cycle. Finished goods inventory starts very high: 2378500 units of laptop and 3129229 units of camera. Laptop holding cost runs at $8.622 per unit, with a shortage cost of $28.906 per unit and a 1-day review cadence. Camera inventory is reviewed every 1 day as well, with a holding cost of $3.282 per unit. On the demand side, HP Enterprise buys laptop on a steady pattern, placing orders every 51.0 days for 1133352.0 units, with customer delivery fixed at 9.0 days. Laptops sell at $4838.953 per unit, cash is collected 32.0 days later, and if stock is unavailable the sale is simply lost. Samsung Electronics buys camera more frequently, every 20.0 days, with demand fixed at 1200533.0 units per order. Camera shipments reach the customer in between 13.0 days and 28.0 days, the selling price is $4383.041 per unit, and payment comes in 52.0 days after delivery. If camera demand exceeds what is on hand, available units are shipped and the remaining demand is lost.

