This setup revolves around making two finished items, SoC and GPU, using steel_rod and copper_wire, with BatteryTech, PolymerSource, and ChipCo feeding a single Production Center that ships into a Warehouse. The SoC uses 1 unit of copper_wire per unit, while the GPU consumes 3 units of steel_rod per unit. On the inbound side, steel_rod is restocked on a periodic schedule in batches ranging from 7240.0 units to 42157.0 units, with arrivals every 4.0 weeks; it carries a holding cost of $5.631 per unit and is reviewed every 29 weeks. Copper_wire is also supplied periodically, but at a fixed 51183.0 units each time, arriving every 13.0 weeks, with a holding cost of $6.871 per unit and a 9-week review cadence. BatteryTech supplies steel_rod with an exponential lead time averaging 14.0 weeks, charges $319.434 per unit, offers payment terms between 35.0 days and 78.0 days, and can provide up to 88763 units per order; material from BatteryTech then takes 3.0 weeks to reach the Production Center. ChipCo is the second steel_rod source, with lead time varying uniformly between 11.0 weeks and 26.0 weeks, a unit cost of $237.703, net 13.0 days payment, and capacity up to 451144 units. PolymerSource handles copper_wire, delivering in exactly 28.0 weeks at $419.522 per unit with payment due 44.0 days after receipt. Manufacturing is split across two activities at the Production Center. SoC is produced through a welding step that turns copper_wire into finished units in a fixed 11.0 weeks. GPU is created through an inspection step that converts steel_rod into output, with cycle times between 12.0 weeks and 24.0 weeks. The site also uses a Quality Scanner with capacity for 2 units at a time; it runs with a consistent 4.859-week service time, stays up for 291.909 weeks at a stretch, then goes down with an average outage of 22.237 weeks, and costs $19.65 per week to operate. Finished goods are stocked before shipment, with SoC starting at 295060 units and GPU at 3177104 units. SoC inventory is reviewed every 1 week, costs $4.461 per unit to hold, and incurs a shortage cost of $43.086 per unit. GPU is also reviewed every 1 week, with holding cost of $9.336 per unit and shortage cost of $21.684 per unit. Demand comes from two customers. Ross Associates buys SoC, placing orders at intervals somewhere between 24.0 weeks and 57.0 weeks, with demand centered around 3799833.0 units and varying by about 129559.0 units. Shipments to Ross Associates take exactly 5.0 weeks, the selling price is $3766.918 per unit, and payment is received between 24.0 days and 44.0 days; if stock is short, available units are shipped and the rest of the demand is lost. NXP Semiconductors purchases GPU, with order arrivals following an exponential pattern averaging 10.0 weeks and demand around 1880180.0 units with variability of 467334.0 units. Their delivery lead time ranges from 16.0 weeks to 25.0 weeks, the product sells for $2241.675 per unit, and cash comes in 71.0 days after delivery; any available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered. In network terms, all three suppliers connect into the Production Center, and both SoC and GPU move from the Production Center into the Warehouse. The model runs for 500 weeks, with the first 50 weeks treated as warm-up and excluded from the main results.

