s out fabrication, converting lithium_cell and frame_structure into laptop with a cycle time between 2.0 weeks and 15.0 weeks. The Regional Depot acts as the warehouse receiving EV_battery from Plant A and laptop from Main Factory. Stock positions are very large in this setup: frame_structure starts at 4394105 units, with holding cost of $7.909 per unit, shortage cost of $39.204 per unit, and review every 1 week; EV_battery begins at 4475348 units, with a shortage cost of $17.86 per unit and a 1-week review cadence; laptop opens at 2868518 units, carries a holding cost of $9.864 per unit, and is also reviewed every 1 week. A single Quality Scanner supports the operation with capacity for 1 unit at a time, a service time averaging 8.708 weeks, operating expense of $16.479 per week, and reliability behavior that stays up for a consistent 313.694 weeks before failures, then remains down for an average of 17.139 weeks. On the demand side, Samsung Electronics buys EV_battery every 57.0 weeks, with demand centered around 872975.0 units and varying by about 29316.0 units. Customer delivery to Samsung takes between 1.0 weeks and 15.0 weeks, unmet orders are backordered, the selling price is $4665.259 per unit, and cash is collected between 32.0 days and 36.0 days later. HP Enterprise purchases laptop every 55.0 weeks in a fixed quantity of 949417.0 units, expects delivery in 2.0 weeks to 17.0 weeks, and if stock is unavailable the sale is lost. The laptop sells at $4516.457 per unit, with payment arriving exactly 17.0 days after delivery. The model runs for a horizon of 100 weeks, using 1 replication.

