rocess Fab manages lithium_cell, ceramic_substrate, and medical_device stock. Finished goods then move from Fab to the Regional Depot for both camera and medical_device distribution. Starting inventory is very large for both products, with 1380397 units of camera on hand initially and 363212 units of medical_device. Camera inventory carries a holding cost of $4.49 per unit and a shortage cost of $44.719 per unit, with review every 1 week, while medical_device stock costs $9.534 per unit to hold, has a shortage cost of $43.738 per unit, and is also reviewed every 1 week. Customer demand is split across two major accounts. NXP Semiconductors buys camera units, placing orders with exponentially spaced arrivals averaging 53.0 weeks; each order is for a constant 3063066.0 units, delivery to the customer always takes 7.0 weeks, and if stock is short the business ships what it can immediately and backorders the rest. Those camera sales go out at $1482.676 per unit, with customer payment received 50.0 days later. Sony Electronics purchases medical_device units, with order arrivals averaging 8.0 weeks and demand centered on 4093418.0 units with variability of 273223.0 units. Its customer lead time runs between 2.0 weeks and 16.0 weeks, the same partial-ship-and-backorder approach is used for shortages, and the selling price is $4489.273 per unit. Sony pays on uniform terms between 13.0 days and 34.0 days. Overall, the model links NanoMaterials Inc., MegaSupply, and CarbonSupply Co. into Fab and the Regional Depot, and it is evaluated over a 500-week planning horizon with 10 replications.

