the Distribution Center. On the finished goods side, FPGA starts with 3929324 units in stock, incurs a holding cost of $0.003 per unit, a shortage cost of $38.233 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 week. SoC opens with 2913740 units, carries a holding cost of $1.94 per unit, and is also checked every 1 week. Customer demand is substantial and quite variable. Bosch Automotive buys FPGA, placing orders at intervals between 12.0 weeks and 50.0 weeks, with order sizes between 1666970.0 units and 4737861.0 units. Delivery to Bosch Automotive takes between 6.0 weeks and 28.0 weeks, the selling price is $305.352 per unit, and payment comes in between 48.0 days and 58.0 days; if FPGA is unavailable, the sale is lost. Lenovo Group purchases SoC, with order arrivals between 8.0 weeks and 41.0 weeks and demand volumes ranging from 102827.0 units to 4091885.0 units. Their customer lead time runs from 9.0 weeks to 28.0 weeks, the product sells at $2828.679 per unit, and Lenovo Group pays exactly 20.0 days after delivery; any unfilled demand is backordered rather than lost. Structurally, the node setup links FastChip Corp., GlobalComponents, and NanoMaterials Inc. to Plant A and the Distribution Center, with finished FPGA and SoC both moving from Plant A into the warehouse stage. The model runs over a horizon of 730 weeks with 10 replications.

