This setup centers on Main Factory making two finished goods, SoC and tablet, from three purchased inputs: glass_fiber, steel_rod, and aluminum_sheet, with outbound stock also passing through a Warehouse. For SoC, each unit uses 4 units of aluminum_sheet, 2 units of steel_rod, and 3 units of glass_fiber. For tablet, each unit requires 4 units of glass_fiber, 1 unit of steel_rod, and 1 unit of aluminum_sheet. On the inbound side, SiliconWorks supplies glass_fiber at $50.044 per unit, with lead time exponentially distributed at an average of 5.0 days, 87.0-day payment terms, and a maximum order size of 972129 units. That material is managed with a min-max style replenishment rule: orders are triggered when stock drops below 4607.14 units and brought back up to 8450.07 units. NanoMaterials Inc. provides steel_rod at $5.815 per unit, sourced on demand, with supplier lead time averaging 7.0 days, payment due 83.0 days after receipt, and a 5.0-day transfer from the supplier to Main Factory. Steel_rod also carries a shortage cost of $48.868 per unit and is reviewed every 4 days. CarbonSupply Co. handles aluminum_sheet at $271.912 per unit, delivered in a fixed 11.0 days under net 24.0-day payment terms, with supply capped at 198512 units per delivery. Aluminum_sheet follows another reorder-point approach, with replenishment activated below 3072.7 units and raised to 9050.71 units; its holding cost is $0.713 per unit, shortage cost is $33.651 per unit, and the stock position is reviewed every 11 days. Inside Main Factory, one manufacturing step called bonding converts aluminum_sheet, steel_rod, and glass_fiber into SoC in exactly 17.0 days, and the factory manages aluminum_sheet, glass_fiber, SoC, and steel_rod inventory in that flow. A second operation at the same Main Factory, cutting, uses glass_fiber, steel_rod, and aluminum_sheet to produce tablet in a fixed 15.0 days, while managing tablet along with the same raw materials. Finished SoC inventory starts at 69613 units, with a shortage penalty of $39.716 per unit and daily review every 1 day. Tablet begins with 2199953 units on hand, carries a shortage cost of $18.242 per unit, and is also reviewed every 1 day. Demand for SoC comes from Bosch Automotive, with customer orders arriving on an exponential pattern averaging 34.0 days; each order size ranges uniformly between 2820094.0 units and 4890016.0 units, delivery to the customer takes between 3.0 days and 6.0 days, and the sales price is $1743.717 per unit. If Bosch Automotive asks for more than is available, the business ships what it can and loses the remainder, and payment is received 34.0 days after delivery. Tablet demand comes from TechCorp, where orders show up about every 32.0 days on average, with quantities normally centered at 2323428.0 units and varying by about 508900.0 units. Customer delivery for tablet is a consistent 15.0 days, the selling price is $4164.344 per unit, and if stock is short, available units go out immediately while the balance is backordered. TechCorp pays somewhere between 20.0 days and 61.0 days after delivery. The network itself is straightforward: SiliconWorks, NanoMaterials Inc., and CarbonSupply Co. feed Main Factory, and Main Factory sends both SoC and tablet to the Warehouse. The model runs for a horizon of 730 days with 5 replications.

