This network revolves around two semiconductor products, SoC and CPU, built from gold_wire, plastic_pellet, and microprocessor and moved through GlobalComponents, GlassCore Ltd., SiliconWorks, the Assembly Hub, and the Fulfillment Hub. The SoC uses 2 units of plastic_pellet and 1 unit of gold_wire per unit, while the CPU is more material-intensive at 3 units of microprocessor, 2 units of plastic_pellet, and 4 units of gold_wire for each unit produced. At the raw material level, gold_wire is restocked on a periodic schedule with shipment quantities ranging from 5223.0 units to 76881.0 units, and those deliveries arrive every 4.0 to 23.0 hours. Its carrying cost is $7.36 per unit, with stock reviewed every 15 hours. plastic_pellet follows a min-max style replenishment rule, triggering action when inventory falls below 3416.52 units and building back up to 4596.93 units; shortage on that item is valued at $12.347 per unit, and it is reviewed every 6 hours. microprocessor is sourced on demand, with a holding cost of $7.801 per unit and inventory checks every 2 hours. Finished stock starts high, with 993052 units of SoC on hand and a shortage cost of $39.397 per unit, reviewed every 1 hour, while CPU begins with 3730410 units in inventory. Supply terms are quite different by vendor. GlobalComponents provides gold_wire with an exponential lead time averaging 4.0 hours, charges $10.106 per unit, allows payment exactly 71.0 days after delivery, and can supply up to 648537 units per order. GlassCore Ltd. supplies plastic_pellet with a fixed 11.0-hour lead time, a price of $414.195 per unit, payment terms between 14.0 days and 46.0 days, and a delivery limit of 121630 units; the trip from GlassCore Ltd. to the Assembly Hub takes between 2.0 and 13.0 hours. SiliconWorks supports the microprocessor flow with a steady 12.0-hour lead time, a unit cost of $108.468, payment due in 51.0 to 62.0 days, and capacity of 544781 units per order. Manufacturing is split into two activities at the Assembly Hub. In the cutting step, plastic_pellet and gold_wire are converted into SoC in exactly 15.0 hours per cycle. In the bonding step, microprocessor, plastic_pellet, and gold_wire are assembled into CPU, with processing time varying from 12.0 to 22.0 hours. The site also uses a Laser Cutter with capacity for 5 units at a time; it has a constant service time of 5.582 hours, runs in batches of 70 units, waits up to 11.253 hours to form a batch, and costs $0.067 per hour to operate. Once built, both SoC and CPU move from the Assembly Hub to the Fulfillment Hub for downstream delivery. On the demand side, Broadcom Limited buys SoC, placing orders with exponentially spaced arrivals averaging 46.0 hours and a fixed order size of 3515541.0 units. Customer lead time is exactly 15.0 hours, the selling price is $4431.667 per unit, and payment comes in 24.0 to 49.0 days; if stock runs short, available units ship and the remainder of demand is lost. Samsung Electronics purchases CPU on a very regular pattern, with orders every 23.0 hours for 4688688.0 units at a time, shipment lead time of 11.0 hours, a selling price of $289.04 per unit, and customer payment arriving in 35.0 to 59.0 days, again with partial fulfillment and lost remainder. The model runs for a horizon of 100 hours, with the first 50 hours treated as warm-up, and 5 replications are used.

