This network makes two finished goods, camera and CPU, using memory_chip, aluminum_sheet, and plastic_pellet sourced into a Processing Unit and then moved on to a Logistics Center. The camera build uses 3 units of memory_chip, 3 units of aluminum_sheet, and 2 units of plastic_pellet per unit, while each CPU requires 3 units of plastic_pellet and 2 units of memory_chip. On the inbound side, PrecisionParts supplies memory_chip to the Processing Unit with a fixed 25.0 days lead time, at $342.532 per unit, with payment due between 40.0 days and 45.0 days after receipt, and it can provide up to 598275 units per order. Memory chips are managed with a min-max approach: once stock drops below 1144.69 units, replenishment brings it back up to 1380.38 units. The shortage penalty for that item is $37.546 per unit, and it is reviewed every 1 day. MegaSupply feeds aluminum_sheet into the same Processing Unit with a consistent 14.0 days lead time, a purchase cost of $421.938 per unit, net 82.0 days payment, and a delivery limit of 119430 units. Aluminum sheet inventory is also controlled with thresholds, reordering below 2583.19 units and restoring stock to 6435.96 units; its shortage cost is $2.618 per unit and review happens every 4 days. GlassCore Ltd. provides plastic_pellet to the Processing Unit with lead times varying uniformly between 4.0 days and 23.0 days, at $241.535 per unit, with payment due 50.0 days after delivery. Plastic pellets are sourced on demand rather than by stock trigger, with a shortage cost of $45.854 per unit and a review cycle of 4 days. Inside the plant, the Processing Unit runs a cutting step that turns memory_chip, aluminum_sheet, and plastic_pellet into camera, and that activity takes exactly 2.0 days per cycle. The same Processing Unit also runs a polishing step that converts plastic_pellet and memory_chip into CPU, with processing time ranging from 15.0 days to 19.0 days. A Welding Station supports operations with capacity for 5 units, service times between 3.544 days and 8.34 days, batching enabled at 77 units with a maximum wait of 6.895 days, and failure behavior switched on: uptime averages 205.565 days and downtime averages 31.288 days. Running that resource costs $17.003 per day. Finished goods then flow from the Processing Unit to the Logistics Center, with both camera and CPU handled there. On the inventory side, camera starts with 1599580 units on hand, carries a holding cost of $2.386 per unit, a shortage cost of $18.309 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 day. CPU begins with 4785758 units in stock, has a shortage penalty of $37.282 per unit, and is likewise reviewed every 1 day. Demand comes from two major customers. Schneider Electric buys camera, placing orders every 19.0 days with a fixed demand of 2308593.0 units each time. Customer lead time is 11.0 days, unmet demand is backordered, the selling price is $4450.919 per unit, and payment arrives 58.0 days after delivery. Siemens Digital purchases CPU with order arrivals averaging 14.0 days, demand fixed at 2458777.0 units, and delivery promised somewhere between 12.0 days and 17.0 days. For CPU orders, available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered, the sales price is $2117.861 per unit, and customer payment comes in after 18.0 days. The modeled network explicitly links PrecisionParts, MegaSupply, and GlassCore Ltd. to the Processing Unit for inbound raw materials, and routes both camera and CPU from the Processing Unit to the Logistics Center. The scenario runs over a 365 days planning horizon with 1 replication.

