This setup revolves around a Fab that turns plastic_pellet and solder_paste into a manufactured sub-component called power_module, and then uses those materials to produce both laptop and SoC units, with finished goods moving on to the Regional Depot. The bill of material is pretty material-intensive: each power_module consumes 3 units of solder_paste and 2 units of plastic_pellet, each laptop requires 4 units of power_module plus 1 unit of plastic_pellet and 1 unit of solder_paste, and each SoC uses 1 unit of power_module together with 4 units of solder_paste. On the raw material side, plastic_pellet is managed with a min-max style replenishment rule that triggers below 3517.61 units and restores stock to 8121.75 units, with a shortage penalty of $7.571 per unit and inventory reviewed every 12 hours. Solder_paste follows a similar approach, reordering when inventory drops under 4519.48 units and topping back up to 6909.37 units, with a much steeper shortage cost of $44.663 per unit and a 2-hour review cycle. Inside the plant, power_module starts with 1104749 units on hand, carries a shortage cost of $30.256 per unit, and is checked every 1 hour. Finished inventory is also substantial, with 3042963 units of laptop and 2007960 units of SoC initially available; their shortage costs are $46.217 per unit and $26.447 per unit respectively, and both are reviewed every 1 hour. Supply comes from three vendors connected into the same network as Fab and Regional Depot. ChipCo supplies plastic_pellet to Fab with lead times ranging from 10.0 hours to 14.0 hours, charges $295.316 per unit, offers payment due 86.0 days after delivery, and can supply up to 638858 units per order. NanoMaterials Inc. provides solder_paste to Fab in exactly 10.0 hours, at $440.748 per unit, with payment terms somewhere between 10.0 days and 39.0 days, and a maximum order size of 984695 units. ElectroParts is the second source for plastic_pellet, delivering in a fixed 26.0 hours at $454.814 per unit, with invoices paid between 21.0 days and 56.0 days after receipt. Production at Fab uses an Assembly Line with a capacity of 2 units, and that resource has an exponential service time averaging 2.758 hours. It runs with batching switched on, so jobs are grouped into batches of 59 units and can wait up to 5.086 hours before release, while the line itself costs $3.995 per hour to operate. For laptop output, Fab performs a polishing step that takes anywhere from 8.0 hours to 21.0 hours and consumes power_module, plastic_pellet, and solder_paste, with the Assembly Line required for that work. For SoC output, the same Fab carries out welding, using solder_paste and power_module to make SoC in a steady 18.0-hour cycle. On the demand side, Infineon Technologies buys laptop units, placing orders every 24.0 to 30.0 hours with order sizes between 2748551.0 units and 4571760.0 units. Delivery to that customer takes 7.0 to 27.0 hours, the selling price is $448.681 per unit, payment comes in 57.0 days, and if stock is unavailable the sale is simply lost. Bosch Automotive purchases SoC, with demand arriving every 29.0 to 44.0 hours in quantities from 1852209.0 units to 4681693.0 units. Their delivery lead time is exactly 20.0 hours, the sales price is $3729.021 per unit, payment is received after 83.0 days, and any unmet volume is backordered rather than lost. In network terms, ChipCo, NanoMaterials Inc., and ElectroParts feed Fab with raw materials, and Fab sends both laptop and SoC downstream to the Regional Depot. The model runs in hours over a 365-hour horizon, with results collected after a 30-hour warm-up period, and includes 5 replications.

