This network revolves around two finished goods, camera and FPGA, built from plastic_pellet and steel_rod across Fab, Processing Unit, and a downstream Fulfillment Hub, with supplier links from Process Go., MetalFab Inc., and NanoMaterials Inc. The camera uses 2 units of plastic_pellet per unit, while the FPGA uses 1 unit of steel_rod per unit. Plastic_pellet is replenished in very large scheduled lots, consistently 26590.0 weeks apart, with deliveries arriving somewhere between 11.0 weeks and 27.0 weeks; carrying that material costs $7.633 per unit, shortages run $29.569 per unit, and stock is reviewed every 20 weeks. Steel_rod is also supplied on a regular cycle, roughly 44149.0 weeks between replenishments with variability of about 2320.0 weeks, and its arrivals fall between 14.0 weeks and 28.0 weeks; shortage exposure is $1.311 per unit and review happens every 8 weeks. On the sourcing side, Process Go. provides plastic_pellet with an exponential lead time averaging 29.0 weeks, charges $390.911 per unit, allows 21.0 weeks to pay, and can supply up to 799857 units per order. NanoMaterials Inc. is the second plastic_pellet source, shipping in 12.0 to 14.0 weeks at $221.632 per unit with payment due 22.0 weeks after receipt. MetalFab Inc. supplies steel_rod with a very short lead time averaging 3.0 weeks, a cost of $298.503 per unit, 51.0-week payment terms, and a maximum order size of 992013 units. Material movement is straightforward but not identical: steel_rod sent from MetalFab Inc. to Fab takes between 4.0 weeks and 13.0 weeks to transfer, while plastic_pellet from NanoMaterials Inc. to Fab has a fixed 10.0-week transfer. At Fab, the inspection step converts plastic_pellet into camera in a cycle ranging from 17.0 weeks to 27.0 weeks, and that site manages both plastic_pellet and camera inventory. Processing Unit handles steel_rod and FPGA inventory, and its testing operation turns steel_rod into FPGA in anywhere from 8.0 weeks to 22.0 weeks. Finished cameras and FPGAs then move on to Fulfillment Hub. The camera business starts with 1298738 units on hand, has a holding cost of $4.018 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 week. ABB Industrial places camera orders with exponentially spaced arrivals averaging 59.0 weeks, each order calling for between 1887480.0 units and 3740547.0 units, with customer delivery promised in 16.0 to 17.0 weeks. Cameras sell at $4393.113 per unit, cash comes in 18.0 to 51.0 weeks later, and if stock is unavailable the sale is simply lost. FPGA begins with 4565330 units in inventory, carries at $0.634 per unit, faces a shortage cost of $39.513 per unit, and is also reviewed every 1 week. Apple Supply orders FPGA on a 30.0-week to 41.0-week pattern, with demand per order ranging from 1740572.0 units to 3056451.0 units; delivery to that customer is a fixed 12.0 weeks. FPGA sells at $2519.117 per unit, payment is received in 25.0 to 56.0 weeks, and the service rule is partial fulfillment with the remainder backordered. The modeled system includes all 3 suppliers and all 3 facilities in one network, and it is evaluated over a 100-week horizon with 10 replications.

