tion step converts steel_rod and silicon_wafer into EV_battery in a fixed 7.0-week cycle and depends on 1 unit of Robot Arm capacity. The Robot Arm works with a constant service time of 0.401 weeks, has failures enabled, runs for an average of 305.552 weeks between breakdowns, stays down for an average of 40.881 weeks when it fails, and costs $17.527 per week to operate. Fab is the other manufacturing site, handling solar_panel, memory_chip, cooling_unit, and silicon_wafer inventory. Its inspection activity turns cooling_unit, memory_chip, and silicon_wafer into solar_panel, and the cycle varies uniformly between 7.0 weeks and 28.0 weeks. Inventory positions are very large in this setup: cooling_unit starts at 139064 units, with a holding cost of $6.937 per unit, a shortage penalty of $45.44 per unit, and a 1-week review cadence. EV_battery begins with 3473599 units on hand, with holding at $4.695 per unit, shortage cost at $2.778 per unit, and review every 1 week. Solar_panel opens at 4280645 units, with holding cost of $0.793 per unit, shortage cost of $4.886 per unit, and a 1-week review interval as well. On the demand side, Samsung Electronics buys EV_battery, placing orders every 49.0 weeks with demand centered around 3164348.0 units and varying by about 454926.0 units. Customer deliveries to Samsung take between 9.0 weeks and 14.0 weeks, unfulfilled demand is backordered, the selling price is $4586.517 per unit, and payment comes 7.0 days after delivery. Schneider Electric purchases solar_panel with order arrivals averaging 24.0 weeks, a fixed demand of 2375052.0 units per order, and a customer lead time of exactly 11.0 weeks. For that account, unmet demand is lost, the price is $486.396 per unit, and customer payment lands 72.0 days after delivery. Finished EV_battery moves from Processing Unit to Distribution Center, and solar_panel moves from Fab to Distribution Center. The model runs for a 500-week horizon with 1 replication.

