This network makes routers using three purchased inputs—epoxy_compound, memory_chip, and gold_wire—and it is organized around BatteryTech, GlassCore Ltd., and NanoMaterials Inc. feeding the Fab, with finished routers moving on to the Warehouse. The build starts with a fabricated component called frame_structure, which uses 1 unit of epoxy_compound, 1 unit of gold_wire, and 1 unit of memory_chip. Each router then requires 1 unit of frame_structure plus another 3 units of gold_wire, 3 units of epoxy_compound, and 4 units of memory_chip. On the inbound side, epoxy_compound is replenished in fixed lots of 67317.0 units, with deliveries arriving on a regular schedule somewhere between 12.0 hours and 24.0 hours; it carries a holding cost of $4.512 per unit, a shortage cost of $45.594 per unit, and is reviewed every 8 hours. BatteryTech supplies that material with an exponential lead time averaging 8.0 hours, charges $152.269 per unit, allows 80.0 days to pay, and the transfer from BatteryTech to the Fab takes exactly 2.0 hours. Memory_chip is supplied in batches of 8083.0 units, arriving every 8.0 hours, and GlassCore Ltd. ships it with a lead time varying uniformly between 6.0 hours and 18.0 hours at a cost of $262.031 per unit; payment to GlassCore Ltd. is due between 20.0 days and 76.0 days after receipt. Gold_wire comes in fixed deliveries of 99320.0 units with arrivals ranging from 2.0 hours to 6.0 hours. NanoMaterials Inc. provides it with an exponential lead time with a mean of 13.0 hours, charges $307.635 per unit, offers 86.0-day payment terms, and can supply up to 438541 units per order. Inside the operation, the Fab manages inventory for gold_wire, epoxy_compound, frame_structure, memory_chip, and router, while the Warehouse serves as the downstream storage point for the finished product. There is an opening stock of 3421027 units of frame_structure, with a shortage cost of $34.391 per unit and a 1-hour review cycle, and 997524 units of router on hand initially, with a holding cost of $5.193 per unit, a shortage cost of $44.027 per unit, and review every 1 hour. The conversion step at the Fab is an inspection operation that takes frame_structure, gold_wire, epoxy_compound, and memory_chip as inputs and produces router output, with processing time varying from 3.0 hours to 18.0 hours. A Robot Arm supports the work with capacity for 1 unit at a time, a service time between 4.178 hours and 5.728 hours, batching enabled at 75 units, a maximum wait of 4.962 hours before a batch is released, and an operating cost of $7.549 per hour. Demand comes from two customers. ABB Industrial orders router units with arrivals between 15.0 hours and 59.0 hours, requests a constant 2723154.0 units per order, expects delivery in 13.0 hours to 27.0 hours, and if stock is unavailable the sale is lost; routers sold to ABB Industrial are priced at $2552.867 per unit and payment is received 72.0 days later. Samsung Electronics also buys router units, placing orders between 25.0 hours and 35.0 hours apart, with demand centered around 2452234.0 units and varying by about 492427.0 units, and expects delivery within 4.0 hours to 11.0 hours. For Samsung Electronics, available stock is shipped and the rest is lost, the selling price is $3342.94 per unit, and customer payment comes between 30.0 days and 59.0 days after shipment. The model runs in hours over a 100-hour horizon, with results collected after a warm-up period of 50 hours, and 1 replication is used.

