ock is very high for both end items, with 2804747 units of camera on hand initially and 4757298 units of smartphone. Camera inventory is reviewed every 1 hour and has a shortage penalty of $6.662 per unit, while smartphone is also reviewed every 1 hour but with a much steeper shortage cost of $33.445 per unit. On the demand side, Apple Supply buys camera, placing orders at exponentially spaced intervals averaging 45.0 hours, with each order fixed at 494809.0 units. Delivery to that customer takes between 12.0 hours and 17.0 hours, the selling price is $3204.676 per unit, and payment comes in between 27.0 hours and 32.0 hours; if stock is short, available units are shipped immediately and the remainder is backordered. Schneider Electric purchases smartphone on a regular 39.0-hour cycle, with demand centered around 3276561.0 units and varying by about 291011.0 units. Its customer lead time is exactly 10.0 hours, the selling price is $264.531 per unit, and payment is received 26.0 hours later; unmet demand is fully backordered. Overall, the modeled network includes the three suppliers—GlassCore Ltd., PrecisionParts, and ElectroParts—feeding the three facilities, Production Center, Assembly Hub, and Logistics Center, and the scenario runs for a horizon of 365 hours with 5 replications.

