es to the Storage Facility. Starting stock is very large: 4603818 units of solar_panel and 1850406 units of medical_device. Holding and shortage costs for solar_panel are $1.987 per unit and $4.636 per unit, with review every 1 hour, while medical_device carries $0.677 per unit in holding cost and a much steeper $21.124 per unit shortage cost, also reviewed every 1 hour. Customer demand comes from 2 accounts. Schneider Electric buys solar_panel, placing orders between every 30.0 hours and 53.0 hours, with order sizes ranging from 1662348.0 units to 2246081.0 units. Delivery to Schneider Electric takes between 7.0 hours and 19.0 hours, the selling price is $1273.822 per unit, and cash is collected between 23.0 days and 50.0 days later. If solar_panel inventory is short, available units are shipped and the rest of the demand is lost. Siemens Digital buys medical_device, with orders arriving between every 16.0 hours and 52.0 hours in a fixed quantity of 4342340.0 units. Its delivery time is exactly 16.0 hours, the sales price is $605.982 per unit, and payment comes 85.0 days after delivery. Any unmet medical_device demand is backordered rather than lost. Network-wise, the 3 suppliers—Process Go., Memory Star, and BatteryTech—feed the 3 internal sites: Pilot Line, Integration Center, and Storage Facility. Material flows are straightforward: epoxy_compound from Process Go. to Pilot Line, resin from Memory Star to Pilot Line, carbon_fiber from BatteryTech to Pilot Line, then solar_panel from Pilot Line to Storage Facility and medical_device from Integration Center to Storage Facility. The model runs in hours over a 100-hour horizon with 1 replication.

