en 1.0 weeks and 6.0 weeks. In the polishing step, solder_paste, cooling_unit, plastic_pellet, and resin are processed into SoC, taking between 17.0 weeks and 20.0 weeks. A Robot Arm supports the operation with capacity for 4 units at a time, a service time between 3.609 weeks and 8.449 weeks, batch processing turned on at 100 units per batch, and a maximum wait of 9.179 weeks before a batch is released; it also costs $16.058 per week to run. Plant A manages cooling_unit, CPU, resin, solder_paste, and plastic_pellet in one manufacturing flow, and cooling_unit, solder_paste, resin, SoC, and plastic_pellet in the other, with finished CPU and SoC then moving from Plant A to the Storage Facility warehouse. On the demand side, Ross Associates buys CPU with orders arriving every 58.0 weeks, each for 2493276.0 units, and expects delivery in 2.0 to 6.0 weeks. CPU sells at $4329.032 per unit, customers pay 38.0 days after delivery, and if inventory is unavailable the sale is simply lost. Siemens Digital purchases SoC with order arrivals averaging 33.0 weeks, demand centered around 2064376.0 units with variability of 528109.0 units, and a fixed customer lead time of 27.0 weeks. SoC is sold at $4042.672 per unit, payment comes anywhere from 44.0 days to 88.0 days later, and any unmet demand is backordered rather than lost. The network itself is straightforward: ElectroParts, Memory Star, and Process Go. feed Plant A, while Plant A ships CPU and SoC onward to the Storage Facility. The model runs over a 100-week horizon with 10 replications.

