This network makes power_module sub-assemblies and then turns materials into two finished goods, medical_device and tablet, using BatteryTech, MegaSupply, and GlobalComponents as the inbound suppliers, with production centered at the Pilot Line and finished items moving on to the Distribution Center. The power_module itself is built from 4 units of plastic_pellet, 1 unit of steel_rod, and 5 units of solder_paste. The medical_device is more complex, requiring 3 units of plastic_pellet, 3 units of power_module, 1 unit of solder_paste, and 4 units of steel_rod per unit, while each tablet uses 4 units of solder_paste and 1 unit of steel_rod. On the raw material side, solder_paste is sourced on demand from BatteryTech, with supplier lead time averaging 17.0 weeks, a purchase cost of $59.718 per unit, payment due 69.0 days after delivery, and a maximum supply of 763524 units per order. Plastic_pellet comes from MegaSupply in periodically scheduled lots ranging from 3953.0 units to 47129.0 units, arriving every 3.0 to 21.0 weeks; MegaSupply’s own lead time is exactly 19.0 weeks, the cost is $461.802 per unit, payment terms run between 28.0 days and 44.0 days, and transfer from MegaSupply to the Pilot Line takes between 1.0 weeks and 3.0 weeks. Steel_rod is also bought as needed, this time from GlobalComponents, with an exponential lead time averaging 11.0 weeks, a unit cost of $160.464, and supplier payment falling between 20.0 days and 84.0 days. Inventory is substantial across the system: there are 4186293 units of power_module on hand initially, 4625476 units of medical_device at the start, and 3066216 units of tablet inventory. Medical_device stock is reviewed every 1 week, and shortages carry a penalty of $0.054 per unit. Tablet inventory is likewise reviewed every 1 week, with a holding cost of $6.378 per unit and a shortage cost of $28.653 per unit. At the Pilot Line, one manufacturing flow is an inspection step that consumes plastic_pellet, power_module, solder_paste, and steel_rod to produce medical_device, and that operation always takes 29.0 weeks. It requires the Injection Molder, which has a capacity of 4 units, a service time anywhere from 0.275 weeks to 9.581 weeks, and batching turned on in lots of 63 units with a maximum wait of 6.28 weeks. The same resource is not needed for tablet assembly, which also happens at the Pilot Line and converts solder_paste plus steel_rod into tablet in a fixed 12.0-week cycle. The Injection Molder is not perfectly reliable either: uptime averages 152.986 weeks, downtime averages 35.379 weeks, and it costs $12.211 per week to operate. In terms of site responsibilities, the Pilot Line manages steel_rod, solder_paste, power_module, plastic_pellet, and medical_device in one manufacturing setup, and tablet, solder_paste, and steel_rod in the other, while the Distribution Center serves as the warehouse downstream. Customer demand is split between Dell Technologies and Bosch Automotive. Dell Technologies buys medical_device, placing orders every 19.0 to 40.0 weeks for between 2746555.0 units and 3414578.0 units at a selling price of $705.973 per unit. Delivery to Dell takes exactly 12.0 weeks, and under the partial shipment policy available stock is sent while any remaining demand is lost; customer payment arrives between 19.0 days and 89.0 days later. Bosch Automotive purchases tablet, with orders arriving every 23.0 to 51.0 weeks and demand typically around 40488.0 units with variability of about 9965.0 units. Tablet shipments reach Bosch in 10.0 to 16.0 weeks, sell at $4303.428 per unit, and follow the same partial-fulfillment, lost-remainder approach, with payment received in 30.0 to 59.0 days. Finished medical_device and tablet units both move from the Pilot Line to the Distribution Center, and the full model is evaluated over a 500-week horizon.

