This network makes routers using glass_fiber, gold_wire, and a fabricated component called cooling_unit. Each cooling_unit is built from 3 units of glass_fiber and 4 units of gold_wire, and each router then requires 3 units of glass_fiber, 2 units of cooling_unit, and 1 unit of gold_wire. The supplier base includes PolymerSource, Process Go., and FastChip Corp., all feeding the Assembly Hub, with finished routers then moving from the Assembly Hub to the Warehouse. Glass_fiber is managed with a min-max style replenishment rule: when stock drops below 3457.31 units, it is brought back up to 4345.98 units. Holding that material costs $5.146 per unit, shortages run $12.422 per unit, and inventory is reviewed every 22 weeks. Gold_wire is also reordered by threshold, with replenishment triggered below 2470.33 units and raised to 6235.48 units. PolymerSource supplies glass_fiber with a fixed 16.0 weeks lead time, charges $9.791 per unit, offers payment due between 37.0 weeks and 38.0 weeks after receipt, and can supply up to 958417 units per order. FastChip Corp. is the second glass_fiber source, delivering in exactly 19.0 weeks at $24.69 per unit with 12.0 weeks payment terms, and it is capped at 502660 units per delivery. Process Go. provides gold_wire with an exponential lead time averaging 8.0 weeks, priced at $83.784 per unit, with payment due between 42.0 weeks and 70.0 weeks, and shipments from Process Go. to the Assembly Hub take 4.0 weeks to transfer. Inside the system, cooling_unit stock starts at 2234538 units, with shortage costs of $36.374 per unit and a 1 week review cycle, while router inventory starts at 2339472 units, carries a shortage penalty of $35.542 per unit, and is also reviewed every 1 week. Production is centered at the manufacturing site called Assembly Hub, which manages router, glass_fiber, cooling_unit, and gold_wire inventory, while the Warehouse serves as the storage point downstream. The Assembly Hub runs a testing operation that takes glass_fiber, cooling_unit, and gold_wire as inputs and turns them into router output in a cycle that ranges from 10.0 weeks to 15.0 weeks. The operation is supported by a CNC Machine resource with capacity for 2 units at a time, an exponential service time averaging 2.678 weeks, and an operating cost of $10.875 per week. On the demand side, TechCorp places router orders every 40.0 weeks, each for 2897359.0 units, expects delivery in 7.0 weeks, pays $1582.476 per unit, and settles invoices 15.0 weeks after delivery. Infineon Technologies orders the same product with arrivals averaging 8.0 weeks, demand fixed at 616092.0 units, delivery promised in 29.0 weeks, a selling price of $3334.056 per unit, and payment terms ranging from 29.0 weeks to 35.0 weeks. For both customers, available router stock ships immediately and any remaining requirement is backordered rather than lost. The model runs over a 100 week horizon, with results collected after a 50 week warm-up period, across 10 replications.

