This network makes smartphone and tablet products from three purchased inputs: microprocessor, steel_rod, and gold_wire. Each smartphone uses 3 units of microprocessor, 2 units of gold_wire, and 1 unit of steel_rod, while each tablet consumes 4 units of microprocessor, 4 units of gold_wire, and 1 unit of steel_rod. Supply is coordinated through MetalFab Inc., MegaSupply, and NanoMaterials Inc., all feeding a Processing Unit, with finished smartphones and tablets then moving on to a Regional Depot. For microprocessor replenishment, the business receives scheduled lots of 24558.0 units every 5.0 days, with inventory reviewed every 29 days, a holding charge of $0.609 per unit, and a shortage penalty of $47.857 per unit. MetalFab Inc. supports that flow with a supplier lead time averaging 25.0 days, a purchase cost of $383.373 per unit, payment terms somewhere between 13.0 days and 72.0 days after receipt, and a maximum order size of 894235 units; the trip from MetalFab Inc. to the Processing Unit takes between 4.0 days and 10.0 days. Steel_rod is sourced on demand from MegaSupply rather than on a fixed schedule. MegaSupply delivers in exactly 23.0 days, charges $374.503 per unit, allows a fixed 57.0-day payment window, and can supply up to 941296 units per order. Gold_wire, by contrast, comes in periodic shipments ranging from 40125.0 units to 89571.0 units, arriving somewhere between 17.0 days and 29.0 days, with stock reviewed every 14 days; its holding cost is $7.386 per unit and its shortage cost is $2.797 per unit. NanoMaterials Inc. provides gold_wire with an exponential lead time averaging 16.0 days, a unit cost of $165.098, and payment due in 8.0 days. Inside the Processing Unit, smartphone output is created through an inspection step that consumes microprocessor, gold_wire, and steel_rod and takes between 14.0 days and 23.0 days per cycle. Tablet production at the same Processing Unit uses a molding step, again drawing on steel_rod, microprocessor, and gold_wire, and this cycle is a consistent 3.0 days. The operation also has access to an Injection Molder with capacity for 3 units, a service time ranging from 5.247 days to 8.353 days, and an operating cost of $11.639 per day. On the finished goods side, smartphone stock starts at 3710141 units, carries a shortage cost of $1.461 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 day, while tablet inventory starts at 1875916 units. Customer demand comes from Schneider Electric for smartphones and ABB Industrial for tablets. Schneider Electric places orders with an exponential interarrival pattern averaging 29.0 days, with demand typically 3333878.0 units and variability of about 888226.0 units, and expects delivery in 5.0 days to 17.0 days. Any smartphone shortfall is backordered, the selling price is $1414.351 per unit, and payment is received 30.0 days later. ABB Industrial orders tablets with arrivals averaging 44.0 days, volumes anywhere from 1569.0 units to 142426.0 units, and delivery lead times between 16.0 days and 28.0 days. If tablets are unavailable, the sale is lost, and they are sold at $4713.541 per unit with customer payment collected between 22.0 days and 69.0 days. The model runs over a 200-day horizon, with results measured after a warm-up period of 50 days, and 10 replications are used.

