This setup revolves around making two finished goods, industrial_sensor and camera, using three incoming materials: copper_wire, silicon_wafer, and aluminum_sheet. The network links Process Go., SiliconWorks, and NanoMaterials Inc. to the Processing Unit, and then sends both industrial_sensor and camera from the Processing Unit on to the Logistics Center. For industrial_sensor, each unit consumes 2 units of silicon_wafer and 1 unit of copper_wire, while each camera requires 3 units of aluminum_sheet. Copper_wire is supplied on a periodic schedule in quantities ranging from 47112.0 units to 84129.0 units, with deliveries arriving every 7.0 days. Its inbound supply from Process Go. takes exactly 5.0 days, costs $82.787 per unit, is paid 9.0 days after delivery, and Process Go. can supply up to 71159 units per order. Copper_wire also carries a shortage cost of $22.182 per unit, with stock reviewed every 1 day. Silicon_wafer is sourced on demand from SiliconWorks, with lead times varying uniformly between 8.0 days and 12.0 days. The purchase cost is $200.504 per unit, payment terms run between 9.0 days and 16.0 days, and SiliconWorks can supply up to 461916 units per order. Its shortage cost is $15.3 per unit, and inventory is reviewed every 12 days. Aluminum_sheet is also ordered as needed, coming from NanoMaterials Inc. with an exponential lead time averaging 10.0 days. It costs $444.006 per unit, payment is due between 35.0 days and 78.0 days after receipt, and the material incurs a holding cost of $4.457 per unit plus a shortage penalty of $10.089 per unit, with review every 26 days. Manufacturing is handled at the Processing Unit in two separate flows. The fabrication step turns silicon_wafer and copper_wire into industrial_sensor, with each run taking between 7.0 days and 21.0 days and requiring the Robot Arm. That Robot Arm has a capacity of 2 units, a service time with an average of 0.662 days, and it works in batches of 40 units with a maximum wait of 8.414 days before a batch is released. It is also subject to failures: uptime averages 461.834 days, downtime is fixed at 44.129 days, and operating it costs $11.165 per day. The second flow is welding, which converts aluminum_sheet into camera in a fixed 17.0-day cycle at the same Processing Unit. On the inventory side, industrial_sensor starts with 3345780 units on hand and has a holding cost of $0.182 per unit, reviewed every 1 day, while camera begins with 4022685 units in stock. Customer demand comes from two buyers. Infineon Technologies purchases industrial_sensor, placing orders between every 14.0 days and 22.0 days, with demand centered around 373563.0 units and varying by about 40578.0 units. Customer deliveries take between 11.0 days and 15.0 days, any unfilled demand is backordered, the selling price is $1341.78 per unit, and payment is received 45.0 days after delivery. TechCorp buys camera on a steady 31.0-day cycle, with demand averaging 3154313.0 units and varying by about 489060.0 units. Camera orders are delivered in 26.0 days, available stock is shipped immediately and the remainder is backordered, the sales price is $1419.026 per unit, and customer payment comes 76.0 days later. The model runs for a 500-day horizon with 10 replications.

