nt. That resource has capacity for 3 units at a time, works with service times ranging from 4.533 to 9.021 hours, and costs $18.377 per hour to operate. A separate welding step at the same Plant A turns battery_pack, memory_chip, silicon_wafer, and microprocessor into router units, with each cycle taking between 5.0 and 28.0 hours and no named equipment requirement. Plant A manages microprocessor, EV_battery, and silicon_wafer for the fabrication side, and also manages microprocessor, battery_pack, memory_chip, silicon_wafer, and router for the welding side. Starting inventory is quite high: 1162428 units of battery_pack, 2448231 units of EV_battery, and 1862370 units of router. Their holding costs are $9.281 per unit for battery_pack, $0.661 per unit for EV_battery, and $2.684 per unit for router, with all three reviewed every 1 hour; router stock also carries a shortage cost of $31.849 per unit. Finished goods move from Plant A to Fulfillment Hub for both EV_battery and router. On the demand side, Ross Associates buys EV_battery units, placing orders somewhere between every 20.0 and 41.0 hours for a fixed 2574514.0 units, expecting delivery in 14.0 to 26.0 hours. EV_battery sells at $2479.872 per unit, Ross Associates pays in 15.0 days, and if stock is unavailable the sale is lost. TechCorp purchases router units, with order arrivals between 35.0 and 59.0 hours and demand centered on 570010.0 units with variability of 113619.0 units. Router orders are promised in exactly 8.0 hours, sold at $1846.622 per unit, and customer payment comes between 19.0 and 66.0 days; available units ship right away and the remaining balance is backordered. The modeled network explicitly links MetalFab Inc., PrecisionParts, and NanoMaterials Inc. to Plant A, and Plant A to Fulfillment Hub. The scenario runs for 200 hours, with results collected after a 50-hour warm-up period.

