capacity for 1 job at a time, a service time averaging 6.401 days, works in batches of 82 units when batching is enabled, waits up to 7.179 days to form a batch, and costs $11.916 per day to run. The assembly step for industrial_sensor uses plastic_pellet and steel_rod and takes a fixed 11 days per cycle. Inventory is already substantial: sensor_array starts at 799107 units and incurs $3.11 per unit in holding cost with review every 1 day, SoC begins with 4826654 units and carries $0.285 per unit in holding cost plus a shortage cost of $46.917 per unit, also reviewed every 1 day, and industrial_sensor opens with 4744470 units. Customer demand comes from two major accounts. Sony Electronics buys SoC, placing orders with exponential interarrival times averaging 45 days; each order is typically 2485083.0 units with variability of about 468297.0 units. Delivery to Sony takes between 5 days and 9 days, the selling price is $1571.181 per unit, and payment comes in 22 days to 30 days after shipment. If stock is short, available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered. Dell Technologies purchases industrial_sensor, with orders arriving somewhere between 30 days and 55 days apart and each order fixed at 3800351.0 units. Dell’s delivery lead time is 1 day flat, the sales price is $931.47 per unit, and customer payment lands between 44 days and 68 days later. Here the business follows a lost-sales approach, so if product is unavailable, the missed demand is not recovered. Structurally, MegaSupply, ChipCo, and FastChip Corp. all feed the Integration Center, and the Integration Center sends both SoC and industrial_sensor to the Storage Facility. The model runs for a 100-day horizon, with results collected after a 30-day warm-up period, and includes 10 replications.

