This network makes FPGA units using three purchased inputs: steel_rod, ceramic_substrate, and memory_chip. The flow is straightforward: MetalFab Inc., ElectroParts, and MegaSupply feed the Pilot Line, and finished FPGA output then moves on to the Distribution Center. Each FPGA requires 1 unit of ceramic_substrate, 3 units of memory_chip, and 3 units of steel_rod. On the sourcing side, steel_rod is bought on an as-needed basis, with inventory carrying cost of $7.742 per unit and a review cadence of 13 weeks. MetalFab Inc. supplies that item with an exponential lead time averaging 12.0 weeks, at a purchase cost of $171.036 per unit, and payment is made somewhere between 49.0 and 62.0 days after receipt. Ceramic_substrate is managed with a min-max style rule: when stock falls below 258.23 units, it is replenished up to 390.46 units. Its shortage cost is $33.076 per unit, and the stock position is reviewed every 28 weeks. ElectroParts delivers ceramic_substrate in a 13.0 to 17.0 week range, charging $182.908 per unit, with payment terms running between 13.0 and 37.0 days. Memory_chip is also controlled by an inventory threshold policy, triggering replenishment below 1374.21 units and restoring inventory to 4108.16 units. The shortage penalty is $35.153 per unit, with reviews every 7 weeks. MegaSupply provides memory_chip at $56.589 per unit, with a fixed supplier lead time of exactly 19.0 weeks, payment due in 39.0 to 40.0 days, and a maximum order size of 881696 units per delivery; shipment from MegaSupply to the Pilot Line takes between 5.0 and 7.0 weeks. Manufacturing is done at the Pilot Line, where the assembly step converts ceramic_substrate, memory_chip, and steel_rod into FPGA. The operation cycle varies uniformly between 12.0 and 18.0 weeks. The site also holds the inventories for FPGA, memory_chip, ceramic_substrate, and steel_rod. Finished goods start with 2548715 units of FPGA on hand, with a holding cost of $7.873 per unit and daily review effectively every 1 week. Capacity on the shop floor is supported by a Robot Arm resource with capacity for 5 units at a time. Its processing time ranges from 4.24 to 5.659 weeks, it runs for a consistent 176.197 weeks before failing, and when it does go down, repair time is exponential with an average of 18.011 weeks. Operating the Robot Arm costs $9.684 per week. Demand comes from HP Enterprise, which buys FPGA at $2856.935 per unit. Customer orders arrive exponentially with an average spacing of 27.0 weeks, and order sizes range from 1848547.0 to 4444781.0 units. Delivery to the customer takes between 16.0 and 23.0 weeks, and if stock is short, demand is backordered rather than lost. HP Enterprise pays on a fixed 53.0-day term. The model runs for a horizon of 365 weeks, with 10 replications.

