This network makes industrial_sensor units using three purchased inputs—lithium_cell, plastic_pellet, and steel_rod—and a fabricated sub-assembly called frame_structure. Each frame_structure uses 4 units of plastic_pellet, 2 units of lithium_cell, and 3 units of steel_rod, while each industrial_sensor is built from 2 units of lithium_cell, 2 units of frame_structure, 2 units of plastic_pellet, and 4 units of steel_rod. The operating footprint is simple: Process Go., GlobalComponents, and GlassCore Ltd. feed Main Factory, and finished industrial_sensor units then move on to the Fulfillment Hub. Main Factory manages frame_structure, steel_rod, lithium_cell, industrial_sensor, and plastic_pellet inventory, and its welding step converts lithium_cell, frame_structure, plastic_pellet, and steel_rod into industrial_sensor on a fixed 17.0-hour cycle. On the materials side, lithium_cell is sourced from Process Go. with a reliable 9.0-hour lead time at $89.483 per unit, and payment is made 26.0 to 28.0 days after delivery; this item follows a min-max style replenishment rule, with reordering triggered below 1353.46 units and stock raised to 3151.27 units, and its shortage penalty is $46.753 per unit with reviews every 12 hours. Plastic_pellet comes from GlobalComponents at $116.906 per unit, with supplier lead time averaging 16.0 hours, payment due in 31.0 to 32.0 days, and a supplier limit of 884926 units per delivery; it is supplied in fixed lots of 87417.0 units, with arrivals scheduled anywhere from 14.0 to 24.0 hours, plus a 10.0-hour transfer from GlobalComponents to Main Factory. Holding plastic_pellet costs $5.867 per unit, shortages run $19.964 per unit, and the stock position is reviewed every 23 hours. Steel_rod is purchased from GlassCore Ltd. with a constant 20.0-hour lead time at $198.023 per unit, with invoices settled 35.0 to 82.0 days after receipt; replenishment kicks in when inventory drops below 3144.61 units and brings stock back to 6648.64 units. Its holding cost is $4.892 per unit, shortage cost is $22.516 per unit, and reviews happen every 27 hours. Inside the operation, frame_structure starts with 666381 units on hand and carries a holding cost of $7.464 per unit, reviewed every 1 hour, while finished industrial_sensor inventory begins at 3651264 units with a holding charge of $5.17 per unit and the same 1-hour review rhythm. Demand comes from two customers. Ross Associates places orders every 38.0 hours, with each request ranging from 1839187.0 to 2498709.0 units, expects delivery in exactly 26.0 hours, pays $3428.96 per unit, and settles invoices in 46.0 to 55.0 days; if stock is unavailable, the sale is lost. Schneider Electric orders industrial_sensor every 23.0 hours, with demand varying between 2849345.0 and 4173734.0 units, accepts delivery in 1.0 to 21.0 hours, buys at $542.032 per unit, and pays exactly 55.0 days later, also under a lost-sales arrangement. The model runs in hours over a 500-hour horizon, with the first 50 hours treated as warm-up, and 5 replications are used.

