This setup revolves around two finished electronics products, GPU and CPU, built from three purchased inputs: copper_wire, gold_wire, and microprocessor. At the Integration Center, GPUs are created through an inspection step that consumes 1 unit of copper_wire and 4 units of microprocessor for each GPU, with processing taking between 12 days and 23 days. The same Integration Center also makes CPUs through a welding operation that uses 2 units of gold_wire per CPU and runs in a 14-day to 25-day cycle. The site manages microprocessor, GPU, and copper_wire inventory in one flow, while CPU and gold_wire are handled in another, and finished goods are then sent on to the Fulfillment Hub. Copper_wire is sourced from GlobalComponents, with replenishment arriving in batches every 11 days and order quantities varying between 43232.0 units and 55224.0 units. GlobalComponents typically delivers in 7.0 to 22.0 days, charges $45.503 per unit, allows payment in 17.0 to 85.0 days, and can supply up to 606784 units per order; the move from GlobalComponents to the Integration Center takes between 3.0 days and 13.0 days. Gold_wire comes from PrecisionParts, supplied periodically in quantities ranging from 55961.0 units to 65660.0 units, with shipment arrivals anywhere from 15.0 days to 28.0 days. PrecisionParts itself has a supplier lead time of 9.0 to 12.0 days, charges $311.647 per unit, offers 88.0-day payment terms, and the transfer into the Integration Center is a fixed 12.0 days. Microprocessor supply is handled by ElectroParts, with replenishment centered around 42015.0 units and varying by about 11359.0 units, while receipts occur exactly 1.0 day after the scheduled arrival trigger. ElectroParts has a consistent 27.0-day lead time, charges $484.2 per unit, and is paid 40.0 to 51.0 days after delivery. On the inventory side, copper_wire carries a holding cost of $8.629 per unit, a shortage cost of $36.616 per unit, and is reviewed every 18 days. Gold_wire is reviewed every 25 days, with holding at $9.667 per unit and shortages costing $8.352 per unit. Microprocessor inventory is checked every 29 days, with a relatively light holding cost of $0.91 per unit but a steep shortage penalty of $49.794 per unit. Finished goods are stocked as well: GPU begins with 868012 units on hand, is reviewed every 1 day, and faces a shortage cost of $24.064 per unit, while CPU starts much higher at 3974592 units, is also reviewed every 1 day, has a holding cost of $9.865 per unit, and incurs $35.217 per unit in shortages. Demand comes from two major customers. Lenovo Group buys GPU, with orders arriving on an exponential pattern averaging 59.0 days, and each order falls between 1324957.0 units and 2776795.0 units. Delivery to Lenovo takes exactly 11.0 days, GPUs sell at $1669.234 per unit, and Lenovo pays 35.0 days after receipt; if stock is short, available units are shipped and the rest of the demand is lost. Broadcom Limited purchases CPU, with customer orders arriving on average every 30.0 days and each order fixed at 4851136.0 units. Its delivery window is between 17.0 days and 19.0 days, the selling price is $4648.406 per unit, and payment comes in exactly 31.0 days; this relationship follows a pure lost-sales approach, so unmet demand is not backordered. The network itself links GlobalComponents, PrecisionParts, and ElectroParts to the Integration Center, and from there both GPU and CPU move to the Fulfillment Hub before serving the market. The model runs over a 365-day horizon, with results collected after a 50-day warm-up period, and it is replicated 10 times.

