This network makes industrial_sensor units using aluminum_sheet, gold_wire, and microprocessor inputs, with ChipCo, Process Go., and Memory Star feeding the Fab plant and finished industrial_sensor units then moving on to the Logistics Center. At the material level, a circuit_board sub-assembly is built from 2 units of aluminum_sheet, 1 unit of microprocessor, and 3 units of gold_wire, and each industrial_sensor in turn requires 1 unit of microprocessor, 3 units of circuit_board, 2 units of gold_wire, and 1 unit of aluminum_sheet. Sourcing is mixed: aluminum_sheet is bought as needed, with a shortage impact of $43.834 per unit and a review cadence of 9 weeks; ChipCo supplies it with lead times ranging from 12.0 weeks to 27.0 weeks at $155.421 per unit, and payment falls between 51.0 weeks and 87.0 weeks after receipt. Gold_wire is also sourced on demand, carrying a holding cost of $7.194 per unit, a shortage cost of $10.877 per unit, and a 26-week review cycle; Process Go. delivers it in anywhere from 16.0 weeks to 19.0 weeks at $47.278 per unit, with payment terms between 9.0 weeks and 38.0 weeks. Microprocessor supply is managed with a min-max style policy that triggers replenishment below 4439.35 units and restores stock to 13531.83 units, with holding cost at $2.456 per unit, shortage cost at $1.579 per unit, and reviews every 24 weeks. Memory Star provides those microprocessor units with an exponential lead time averaging 23.0 weeks, charges $89.284 per unit, and is paid exactly 81.0 weeks after delivery; the transfer from Memory Star to Fab adds another 4.0 weeks to 10.0 weeks. Inside Fab, inventory is managed for aluminum_sheet, microprocessor, circuit_board, gold_wire, and industrial_sensor. The operation there is called bonding, and it consumes microprocessor, circuit_board, gold_wire, and aluminum_sheet to produce industrial_sensor, running on a fixed 12.0-week cycle and requiring the Welding Station. That Welding Station has capacity for 2 units, a service time fixed at 7.693 weeks, batching turned on for 93 units at a time, a maximum wait of 13.074 weeks before a batch is released, and an operating cost of $17.735 per week. On-hand stock starts very high, with 4905622 units of circuit_board already available and 4016916 units of industrial_sensor in finished inventory; the finished goods holding cost is $3.823 per unit with a 1-week review interval. Demand comes from two customers. Apple Supply places orders for industrial_sensor at intervals between 13.0 weeks and 39.0 weeks, with demand typically 4329146.0 units and variability of about 14040.0 units; shipments to Apple Supply take exactly 17.0 weeks, missed sales are lost rather than backordered, the selling price is $1127.337 per unit, and payment arrives 73.0 weeks later. Infineon Technologies orders the same product on an exponential pattern averaging 29.0 weeks, with order sizes ranging from 2439956.0 units to 4353158.0 units; delivery takes between 4.0 weeks and 28.0 weeks, unmet demand is also lost, the sales price is $1014.206 per unit, and customer payment comes in 14.0 weeks to 66.0 weeks. The model runs over a 200-week horizon with 10 replications.

