This network makes two finished goods at Plant A: routers and smartphones, with the Fulfillment Hub handling outbound product flow. The raw inputs are silicon_wafer, steel_rod, and microprocessor. A router is built from 3 units of microprocessor and 2 units of steel_rod, while a smartphone consumes 4 units of silicon_wafer. Silicon_wafer is sourced from NanoMaterials Inc. on an as-needed basis, with a holding cost of $6.93 per unit and inventory reviewed every 15 weeks. NanoMaterials Inc. delivers in exactly 12.0 weeks, charges $134.325 per unit, offers 16.0-day payment terms, and can supply up to 190719 units per order. Steel_rod comes from FastChip Corp. through a scheduled replenishment pattern of 65855.0 weeks flat, with arrivals occurring anywhere from 1.0 to 21.0 weeks; FastChip Corp. has an exponential lead time averaging 7.0 weeks, charges $258.87 per unit, and payment is due between 39.0 days and 68.0 days after receipt. The transfer from FastChip Corp. to Plant A takes between 7.0 and 12.0 weeks. Microprocessor supply is managed with a min-max style policy: when stock drops below 4094.09 units, it is brought back up to 12443.94 units. This item carries a holding cost of $6.807 per unit, a shortage cost of $17.033 per unit, and a 14-week review cadence. GlobalComponents supplies the microprocessor with lead times averaging 7.0 weeks, a unit cost of $232.248, payment terms ranging from 11.0 days to 80.0 days, and a maximum order size of 531895 units. Inside Plant A, the polishing step converts microprocessor and steel_rod into router output in a fixed 10.0 weeks per cycle, and the coating step turns silicon_wafer into smartphone output in a 4.0 to 11.0 week range. The site manages microprocessor, router, and steel_rod in one manufacturing flow, and smartphone plus silicon_wafer in another, all under the same Plant A name. A Packaging Machine supports operations with capacity for 3 units, service times between 5.89 and 8.851 weeks, batching enabled at 16 units, a maximum wait of 11.574 weeks, and an operating cost of $19.132 per week. Finished inventory starts very high, with 4150970 units of router on hand and 3956342 units of smartphone. Router stock carries $2.15 per unit in holding cost, $10.429 per unit in shortage cost, and is reviewed every 1 week. Smartphone inventory is reviewed every 1 week as well, with holding cost at $7.259 per unit and shortage cost at $6.421 per unit. On the demand side, Siemens Digital buys router units, placing demand every 25.0 weeks for 1575689.0 units, expecting delivery in exactly 28.0 weeks. Routers sell at $4246.928 per unit, customer payment arrives between 36.0 days and 47.0 days, and if product is unavailable the sale is lost. Samsung Electronics buys smartphones, with orders showing up between 17.0 and 24.0 weeks; demand is roughly 924274.0 units on average, varying by about 96613.0 units, and delivery to the customer takes 13.0 to 15.0 weeks. Smartphones sell at $749.952 per unit, payment comes exactly 41.0 days later, and the policy is partial fulfillment — available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. Structurally, NanoMaterials Inc., FastChip Corp., and GlobalComponents all feed Plant A, and Plant A then sends both router and smartphone output to the Fulfillment Hub. The model runs in weeks over a 200-week horizon with 10 replications.

