 Pilot Line, the polishing step converts glass_fiber into tablet, takes exactly 11.0 weeks per cycle, and requires the CNC Machine. Pilot Line manages both tablet and glass_fiber inventory. At Manufacturing Site, another polishing operation turns silicon_wafer into laptop in a fixed 26.0-week cycle, and this site manages laptop and silicon_wafer stock. The Distribution Center acts as the warehouse receiving tablet from Pilot Line and laptop from Manufacturing Site. Material flow is a bit uneven by design: glass_fiber from FastChip Corp. reaches Pilot Line in anywhere from 6.0 weeks to 11.0 weeks, silicon_wafer from Memory Star is delivered to Pilot Line in 13.0 weeks, and glass_fiber from GlassCore Ltd. also feeds Pilot Line. Outbound finished goods move from Pilot Line to the Distribution Center for tablet and from Manufacturing Site to the Distribution Center for laptop. On the demand side, Ross Associates buys tablet, placing orders somewhere between 28.0 weeks and 55.0 weeks apart, with demand fixed at 1924561.0 units per order. Customer deliveries take exactly 1.0 week, any shortfall is backordered, the selling price is $2922.757 per unit, and payment comes in between 27.0 days and 89.0 days. TechCorp purchases laptop every 31.0 weeks, ordering 3290375.0 units each time. Delivery to that customer takes between 15.0 weeks and 28.0 weeks, unmet demand is also backordered, the sales price is $2094.483 per unit, and payment is received 11.0 days after delivery. The model runs over a 500-week horizon with 5 replications.

