ays. Integration Center manages power_module, CPU, and glass_fiber, and its testing step turns power_module and glass_fiber into CPU in 10.0 days. Starting stock is substantial: 3744097 units of power_module, 872032 units of laptop, and 3351253 units of CPU. Laptop inventory costs include $2.913 per unit in holding, $35.833 per unit in shortage, and review every 1 day. CPU inventory is also checked every 1 day, with a holding cost of $2.991 per unit. The operation is supported by an Injection Molder with capacity for 1 unit at a time. It works with an exponential service time averaging 4.602 days, uses batching with 33 units per batch and a maximum wait of 15.664 days, and it is not perfectly reliable: uptime averages 131.787 days before a failure, and each outage lasts a fixed 14.895 days. Running this resource costs $1.647 per day. Material flow is straightforward: GlassCore Ltd., PolymerSource, and NanoMaterials Inc. ship raw inputs to Main Factory; finished laptops move from Main Factory to the Distribution Center; and CPUs move from Integration Center to the Distribution Center. On the demand side, TechCorp orders laptop units every 20.0 to 50.0 days, with a fixed demand of 3395775.0 units per order. Customer lead time is 6.0 days, the selling price is $624.767 per unit, payment arrives between 46.0 days and 77.0 days, and any shortfall is backordered rather than lost. Lenovo Group buys CPU units every 30.0 days, with demand ranging from 2217901.0 units to 4721606.0 units. Those orders are delivered in 15.0 to 26.0 days, sold at $4769.151 per unit, and paid in 80.0 days, again with a backorder policy. The scenario runs over a 200-day horizon, with results collected after a 30-day warm-up period, across 5 replications.

