the beginning. Medical_device inventory also carries a holding cost of $1.841 per unit, a shortage cost of $8.011 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 day. Demand comes from 2 major customers. Samsung Electronics buys solar_panel, placing orders every 51.0 days, with order sizes between 2655164.0 units and 3935092.0 units, expecting delivery in 11.0 to 17.0 days. Solar_panel sells at $2583.622 per unit, customer payment arrives between 10.0 days and 52.0 days, and if stock is unavailable, the sale is lost. Lenovo Group purchases medical_device with arrivals ranging from 24.0 days to 59.0 days, demand centered around 2172188.0 units with variability of 43350.0 units, and customer lead times between 9.0 days and 12.0 days. Medical_device is sold at $4923.463 per unit, Lenovo pays exactly 77.0 days after delivery, and any unmet demand is backordered rather than lost. The network itself is straightforward: ChipCo, CarbonSupply Co., and PrecisionParts supply Plant A, and Plant A sends both solar_panel and medical_device to the Storage Facility. The model runs over a 730-day horizon, with results collected after a 30-day warm-up period and 1 replication.

