n called cutting consumes glass_fiber and aluminum_sheet to produce EV_battery, and each run takes a consistent 21 hours. Another operation at the same Production Center, testing, uses carbon_fiber, motor_assembly, and glass_fiber to produce GPU, with cycle times varying uniformly between 3 hours and 15 hours. The site manages aluminum_sheet, glass_fiber, and EV_battery in one manufacturing flow, and motor_assembly, glass_fiber, carbon_fiber, and GPU in the other. Starting inventory is substantial: 2530760 units of motor_assembly, 746651 units of EV_battery, and 3742358 units of GPU. Holding motor_assembly costs $3.562 per unit, with review every 1 hour, while EV_battery carries a shortage cost of $39.654 per unit and is also reviewed every 1 hour. On the demand side, Siemens Digital buys EV_battery, with customer orders arriving on an exponential pattern averaging 10 hours and order sizes ranging from 446173.0 units to 2999007.0 units. Delivery to that customer takes exactly 19 hours, unmet demand is backordered, the selling price is $3027.572 per unit, and payment comes in 53 hours to 83 hours later. Ross Associates purchases GPU on a fixed 22-hour ordering rhythm, with demand between 511653.0 units and 3416152.0 units per order. GPU shipments also take 19 hours to reach the customer, available stock is shipped immediately and the remainder is backordered, the sales price is $4490.76 per unit, and customer payment lands between 39 hours and 48 hours. In network terms, PrecisionParts, SiliconWorks, and Memory Star feed the Production Center, and the Production Center then sends both EV_battery and GPU on to the Regional Depot. The model runs over a 100-hour horizon, with 5 replications, and results are collected after a 50-hour warm-up period.

