This network centers on Advanced Fab, which turns solder_paste, memory_chip, and resin into a fabricated component called circuit_board and then into two finished items, SoC and GPU, with Regional Depot acting as the downstream warehouse. The material flow is straightforward: PolymerSource sends solder_paste to Advanced Fab, FastChip Corp. supplies memory_chip to the same plant, and Memory Star provides resin there as well; from Advanced Fab, both SoC and GPU move on to Regional Depot. To build 1 unit of circuit_board, the plant consumes 5 units of resin, 4 units of solder_paste, and 1 unit of memory_chip. The SoC recipe is heavier, requiring 4 units of resin, 2 units of solder_paste, 4 units of memory_chip, and 3 units of circuit_board per unit, while each GPU uses 4 units of circuit_board and 1 unit of memory_chip. Solder_paste is managed with a min-max style replenishment rule, reordering when stock drops below 1559.67 units and topping back up to 4169.37 units; its carrying cost is $4.911 per unit, and inventory is reviewed every 12 days. PolymerSource delivers solder_paste in 6.0 to 13.0 days, charges $201.768 per unit, allows payment in 39.0 to 76.0 days, and can supply up to 284847 units per order. Memory_chip comes in on a scheduled supply pattern, with batch sizes centered around 71862.0 units and varying by about 4336.0 units, and those deliveries arrive every 6.0 to 17.0 days. FastChip Corp. takes 16.0 to 22.0 days to deliver, charges $426.334 per unit, offers net 7.0 days payment terms, and is capped at 7265 units per delivery. Resin is also controlled by an inventory threshold, with replenishment triggered below 948.09 units and raised to 1200.84 units. Holding resin costs $5.657 per unit, shortages cost $38.851 per unit, and the stock position is reviewed every 22 days. Memory Star supplies resin in exactly 27.0 days at $153.375 per unit, with payment due 33.0 to 63.0 days after receipt. Inside Advanced Fab, circuit_board, memory_chip, solder_paste, resin, and SoC are all managed inventories for the SoC side of the operation, while circuit_board, memory_chip, and GPU are managed for the GPU line. The SoC manufacturing step is called polishing; it takes exactly 26.0 days per cycle and consumes resin, solder_paste, memory_chip, and circuit_board to produce SoC. The GPU step is coating, running at a fixed 8.0 days per cycle and converting circuit_board plus memory_chip into GPU. There is also a Packaging Machine available with capacity for 4 units at a time, a service time ranging from 5.591 to 5.741 days, and an operating cost of $17.939 per day. Inventory levels start high: 3056394 units of circuit_board, 4989953 units of SoC, and 1549293 units of GPU. Carrying circuit_board costs $3.817 per unit, shortages cost $43.043 per unit, and it is checked every 1 day. SoC inventory costs $7.172 per unit to hold with a 1 day review cycle, while GPU holding cost is $5.803 per unit with reviews every 1 day as well. On the demand side, Apple Supply buys SoC on a steady 56.0 day ordering rhythm, with order volume averaging 1023569.0 units and varying by about 55048.0 units. Shipments to Apple Supply take exactly 28.0 days, the product sells at $270.023 per unit, and payment comes back in 52.0 to 60.0 days. If SoC stock is short, available units are shipped immediately and the remainder is backordered. Sony Electronics purchases GPU with arrivals averaging 31.0 days, and its demand is roughly 85928.0 units with variability of 17135.0 units. GPU orders are delivered in 15.0 to 16.0 days, sold at $1204.684 per unit, and paid in 45.0 to 48.0 days, with a full backorder policy when supply is not immediately available. The whole setup is evaluated over a 500 day horizon with 10 replications.

