This setup centers on making cameras with three incoming materials at Plant A and then moving finished goods to the Fulfillment Hub. Each camera uses 4 units of aluminum_sheet, 2 units of gold_wire, and 3 units of copper_wire. Copper_wire is sourced from MetalFab Inc. on an as-needed basis, with supplier lead time varying uniformly between 17.0 days and 20.0 days, a purchase cost of $218.725 per unit, and payment due between 36.0 days and 55.0 days after delivery. At the plant, copper_wire carries a shortage cost of $30.763 per unit and is reviewed every 28 days. Gold_wire comes from Memory Star in periodic deliveries, with shipment sizes typically around 18314.0 units and varying by about 5336.0 units, arriving somewhere between 4.0 days and 5.0 days apart. Memory Star’s lead time is exponentially distributed with a mean of 14.0 days, the material costs $443.824 per unit, and payment terms are a fixed 32.0 days. There is also a 10.0-day transfer from Memory Star to Plant A. Gold_wire inventory is reviewed every 9 days and held at $2.37 per unit. Aluminum_sheet is supplied by GlobalComponents in fixed lots of 43018.0 units, delivered periodically every 14.0 days. GlobalComponents typically ships with an average lead time of 24.0 days, charges $316.823 per unit, offers net 51.0-day payment, and can supply up to 107739 units per order. At Plant A, aluminum_sheet is reviewed every 17 days and has a shortage cost of $33.534 per unit. Manufacturing happens at Plant A through a bonding step that converts aluminum_sheet, gold_wire, and copper_wire into camera units. The operation cycle ranges from 5.0 days to 19.0 days. Processing is supported by 1 unit of CNC Machine capacity, and that machine has an exponential service time averaging 0.83 days. Batching is turned on, so work is grouped into batches of 14 units with a maximum wait of 1.982 days before release, and the machine runs at an operating cost of $13.053 per day. Finished camera inventory starts at 415218 units, is reviewed every 1 day, and incurs a holding cost of $2.346 per unit. On the demand side, Broadcom Limited places camera orders every 46.0 days for exactly 2832298.0 units, expects delivery in 16.0 days, pays $4610.811 per unit, and settles invoices 59.0 days after receipt. If stock is short, available units are shipped immediately and the remainder is backordered. HP Enterprise orders every 27.0 days flat, with demand centered around 1106907.0 units and varying by about 290752.0 units. Its delivery promise is between 8.0 days and 13.0 days, the selling price is $4410.535 per unit, and customer payment arrives after 64.0 days. For HP Enterprise, unfulfilled demand is fully backordered. Across the network, MetalFab Inc., Memory Star, and GlobalComponents feed Plant A, and Plant A sends cameras to the Fulfillment Hub. The model runs for 500 days, with results collected after a warm-up period of 30 days, across 5 replications.

