t and a shortage cost of $39.387 per unit. Camera production at the Processing Unit is tied to a welding step that takes between 14.0 and 18.0 days per cycle, and the finished cameras then move on to the Logistics Center. The site also has a Laser Cutter resource with capacity for 3 units at a time; its service time ranges from 5.966 to 7.581 days, and it costs $8.03 per day to run. Customer demand comes from two major accounts. Sony Electronics places camera orders at intervals between 33.0 and 58.0 days, with order sizes ranging from 2756464.0 to 3244321.0 units, expects delivery in 5.0 to 18.0 days, pays between 48.0 and 64.0 days after receipt, and buys at $4489.421 per unit. Lenovo Group orders more frequently, averaging 14.0 days between arrivals, with demand centered around 194554.0 units and varying by about 48791.0 units; its delivery time is fixed at 25.0 days, payment is made 57.0 days after delivery, and the selling price is $3256.35 per unit. For both Sony Electronics and Lenovo Group, available camera stock is shipped immediately and any remainder is backordered. The model runs over a 500-day horizon with 10 replications.

