oduction happens in 2 streams at the Manufacturing Site. In the fabrication step, resin, ceramic_substrate, and heat_sink are processed into medical_device, and the cycle time varies uniformly between 11.0 hours and 13.0 hours. In the cutting step, heat_sink is converted into FPGA, with processing time ranging from 16.0 hours to 22.0 hours. On the demand side, HP Enterprise buys medical_device, placing orders with exponentially distributed interarrival times averaging 19.0 hours. Order size is normally centered on 2758023.0 units with variability of 465033.0 units, shipments are promised in exactly 13.0 hours, and the business follows a partial-fill lost-sales approach, meaning available units go out and the remainder is lost. The selling price is $3550.792 per unit, and payment is received 50.0 days after delivery. Infineon Technologies purchases FPGA on a fixed 12.0-hour ordering rhythm, with demand averaging 3024083.0 units and varying by about 48488.0 units. Customer lead time is exactly 20.0 hours, any shortfall is backordered, the sales price is $4050.238 per unit, and cash is collected 67.0 days later. The modeled network links FastChip Corp., MetalFab Inc., and ChipCo into the Manufacturing Site, then routes medical_device and FPGA from the Manufacturing Site to the Warehouse. The scenario runs for a 200-hour horizon, with results collected after a 30-hour warm-up period, using 1 replication.

