strial_sensor output moves from Main Factory to the Fulfillment Hub, and laptops flow from Processing Unit to the Fulfillment Hub. On the equipment side, there is a Welding Station with capacity for 4 units at a time, an exponential service time averaging 2.656 weeks, and operating expense of $8.984 per week. It is fairly reliable but not perfect: it stays up for a consistent 447.543 weeks, then goes down for 3.168 weeks before returning to service. Inventory on the finished goods side is substantial. The business starts with 767723 units of industrial_sensor on hand, with a holding cost of $3.557 per unit and a 1-week review cycle. Laptop inventory opens at 50350 units, carries a shortage penalty of $16.952 per unit, and is also reviewed every 1 week. Demand comes from two major customers. Bosch Automotive buys industrial_sensor, placing orders at intervals between 29 and 39 weeks, with demand roughly 4189929.0 units on average and variability of 542678.0 units. Customer deliveries take between 11 and 25 weeks, unmet demand is backordered, the selling price is $1564.808 per unit, and payment is received 46 days after delivery. Samsung Electronics purchases laptop, with order arrivals averaging 34 weeks, order sizes ranging from 2196091.0 to 2250558.0 units, and customer lead times between 12 and 21 weeks. For laptops, if stock is unavailable the sale is lost, pricing is set at $1519.675 per unit, and customer payment comes 49 days after delivery. Overall, the structure is straightforward: suppliers feed Main Factory with ceramic_substrate and silicon_wafer, Main Factory makes industrial_sensor, Processing Unit makes laptop, and both products are forwarded to the Fulfillment Hub for downstream fulfillment.

