This network revolves around Plant A, which turns lithium_cell and gold_wire into a fabricated component called circuit_board, then uses those materials to produce two sellable items: router and EV_battery. The bill of material for each circuit_board is 5 units of lithium_cell and 3 units of gold_wire. A router requires 1 unit of circuit_board, 1 unit of gold_wire, and 4 units of lithium_cell, while an EV_battery uses 1 unit of circuit_board, 4 units of gold_wire, and 2 units of lithium_cell. On the inbound side, lithium_cell is sourced from both Memory Star and GlobalComponents, and gold_wire comes from ElectroParts, with all three suppliers linked directly to Plant A. Memory Star delivers lithium_cell in exactly 25.0 days at $144.296 per unit, offers payment terms anywhere from 12.0 days to 63.0 days after receipt, and can supply up to 605827 units per order. GlobalComponents also supplies lithium_cell, but with lead time averaging 16.0 days, a lower cost of $48.035 per unit, payment due between 14.0 days and 47.0 days, and a maximum order size of 289266 units. ElectroParts provides gold_wire in a consistent 10.0-day lead time, charges $293.794 per unit, gives 35.0-day payment terms, and is limited to 132709 units per delivery. Internally, lithium_cell is managed with a min-max style replenishment rule that triggers below 577.3 units and restores stock to 2585.56 units, and it carries a holding cost of $2.012 per unit with inventory reviewed every 28 days. Gold_wire, by contrast, is replenished on a regular schedule with shipment quantities centered around 29755.0 units and varying by about 5912.0 units, arriving every somewhere between 10.0 days and 24.0 days. Plant A manages lithium_cell, gold_wire, circuit_board, router, and EV_battery inventory across its manufacturing activities, while Regional Depot acts as the downstream warehouse receiving both finished goods from Plant A. The circuit_board stock starts at 3027438 units and is reviewed every 1 day, with a shortage penalty of $21.405 per unit. Router inventory begins at 3484233 units, has a holding cost of $6.764 per unit, and is checked every 1 day. EV_battery opens with 2326687 units on hand, carries a holding cost of $0.64 per unit, and is also reviewed every 1 day. For production, Plant A runs a bonding step that consumes circuit_board, gold_wire, and lithium_cell to make router, with each cycle taking between 8.0 days and 17.0 days and requiring the CNC Machine. That CNC Machine has capacity for 1 unit at a time, works with service times between 1.15 days and 9.575 days, and costs $14.911 per day to operate. The same Plant A site also performs an inspection step that converts lithium_cell, circuit_board, and gold_wire into EV_battery, and that process runs in a 12.0-day to 22.0-day range without a named resource requirement. On the demand side, HP Enterprise buys router, placing orders every 39.0 days with order size ranging from 2656135.0 units to 3532423.0 units. Customer delivery for HP Enterprise takes between 3.0 days and 10.0 days, the selling price is $3427.482 per unit, and payment comes in between 7.0 days and 30.0 days. If router stock is short, Plant A ships what is available and the remaining demand is lost. Broadcom Limited purchases EV_battery, with orders arriving somewhere between 21.0 days and 39.0 days and demand varying from 974023.0 units to 4682064.0 units. Its delivery lead time runs between 6.0 days and 9.0 days, the sales price is $2846.757 per unit, and payment is received exactly 45.0 days later. Broadcom follows the same partial-fill approach: available units ship immediately and the unmet balance is lost. The modeled network therefore connects Memory Star, ElectroParts, and GlobalComponents to Plant A, and then moves router and EV_battery from Plant A to Regional Depot. The scenario is evaluated over a 100-day horizon, with results collected after a warm-up period of 50 days.

