s with 824878 units of circuit_board on hand, with carrying cost of $8.872 per unit, shortage cost of $29.284 per unit, and a 1-week review cadence. Finished goods begin with 3387403 units of EV_battery and 3325995 units of drone in stock; EV_battery shortages cost $33.418 per unit and that item is also reviewed every 1 week. Manufacturing is centered on the Processing Unit, supported by an Assembly Line with capacity for 2 units, a fixed processing time of 3.896 weeks, batching turned on at 90 units, and a maximum batch wait of 14.627 weeks, with operating expense running at $15.835 per week. At the same Processing Unit, the welding step turns circuit_board, glass_fiber, microprocessor, and lithium_cell into EV_battery units in exactly 28.0 weeks, and that facility manages microprocessor, circuit_board, lithium_cell, glass_fiber, and EV_battery inventory. The same Processing Unit also runs a bonding step for drone output, converting lithium_cell, microprocessor, and circuit_board into drone units in a fixed 21.0 weeks, while managing lithium_cell, microprocessor, circuit_board, and drone stock. Finished EV_battery units and drone units then move from the Processing Unit to the Warehouse. On the demand side, Samsung Electronics buys EV_battery units, with orders arriving exponentially with an average of 15.0 weeks and each order fixed at 2479033.0 units. Customer lead time is exactly 5.0 weeks, the selling price is $759.436 per unit, payment comes 88.0 days after delivery, and if product is unavailable, the sale is lost. Bosch Automotive buys drone units with order arrivals somewhere between 18.0 weeks and 53.0 weeks, demand averaging 1135332.0 units with variability of 250276.0 units, and a customer lead time of 26.0 weeks. Drones sell at $2308.05 per unit, payment is received 62.0 days after delivery, and the fulfillment rule is partial shipment: available units are sent and the remaining demand is lost. Structurally, the network links the three suppliers—MetalFab