ically tablet, GPU, and camera. On the demand side, Dell Technologies buys tablet, placing orders every 21.0 weeks to 56.0 weeks, each order for 1552271.0 units, with customer delivery in 6.0 weeks. Tablets sell at $18.425 per unit, payment arrives between 44.0 weeks and 82.0 weeks, and if stock is unavailable the sale is lost. NXP Semiconductors orders GPU with interarrival averaging 47.0 weeks, demand anywhere from 112789.0 units to 4230566.0 units, and a consistent 12.0-week customer lead time. GPUs sell at $1671.66 per unit, payment is received 11.0 weeks later, and the policy is partial fulfillment—available units ship, and the remainder is lost. Schneider Electric purchases camera every 43.0 weeks, with demand centered around 3446894.0 units and varying by about 722235.0 units, and delivery takes 7.0 weeks. Cameras are priced at $2034.928 per unit, paid 20.0 weeks after shipment, and any shortfall is handled the same way: ship what is on hand and lose the rest. The modeled network includes all 8 suppliers feeding Main Factory, with Main Factory and the Fulfillment Hub as the 2 operating sites, and the study runs over a 500-week horizon with 10 replications.

