ts at a time and processes work with a service time anywhere from 2.278 to 6.021 hours. Batching is enabled, so jobs are released in lots of 95 units or after a maximum wait of 3.576 hours. The line is not perfectly reliable: uptime is a consistent 173.804 hours, followed by downtime averaging 16.705 hours, and it costs $5.204 per hour to run. At the Pilot Line, the cutting step uses copper_wire, power_module, and solder_paste to make FPGA, with each cycle taking between 14.0 and 19.0 hours and requiring the Assembly Line. The same Pilot Line also runs a welding step that converts solder_paste, copper_wire, and power_module into industrial_sensor in exactly 5.0 hours. The Pilot Line manages inventory for power_module, solder_paste, FPGA, and copper_wire in one configuration, and for power_module, solder_paste, industrial_sensor, and copper_wire in the other, with finished goods then moving onward to the Regional Depot. Customer demand is substantial. Infineon Technologies buys FPGA, placing orders every 51.0 hours for 3967114.0 units, expects delivery in exactly 17.0 hours, pays at $4038.081 per unit, and settles invoices between 13.0 and 46.0 days later; if stock is unavailable, the sale is lost. Ross Associates purchases industrial_sensor with order arrivals averaging 46.0 hours, each demand fixed at 4007625.0 units, delivery promised in 2.0 to 29.0 hours, and a selling price of $4582.689 per unit; payment comes 34.0 days after delivery, and available stock ships immediately while the remainder is backordered. The model runs for a 200-hour horizon, with results collected after a 50-hour warm-up period, across 10 replications.

