pper_wire source, with lead time exponentially distributed with mean 10.0 weeks, a unit cost of $125.336, payment somewhere between 8.0 days and 25.0 days after delivery, and capacity capped at 702459 units; shipments from CarbonSupply Co. to the Manufacturing Site take an additional 2.0 to 12.0 weeks to transfer. GlobalComponents supplies copper_wire as well, on a fixed 27.0-week lead time at $229.184 per unit with net 74.0-day payment. Inside the Manufacturing Site, the cutting operation turns display_panel, circuit_board, glass_fiber, and copper_wire into SoC units. That step runs on the Conveyor Belt and takes between 16.0 weeks and 17.0 weeks per cycle. The Conveyor Belt has capacity for 3 units, a consistent 5.03-week service time, average uptime of 324.78 weeks, average downtime of 38.029 weeks, and an operating cost of $17.852 per week. A Laser Cutter is also available with capacity 1, a fixed 7.421-week service time, and batching enabled at 53 units with a maximum wait of 6.351 weeks; it fails after a reliable 306.068 weeks of uptime on average and is then down for about 28.471 weeks, with operating expense of $4.857 per week. On the demand side, TechCorp buys SoC every 8.0 weeks, with order sizes ranging from 1611481.0 units to 4449671.0 units. Customers wait 27.0 weeks for delivery, any unfilled demand is backordered, the selling price is $554.703 per unit, and payment comes between 10.0 days and 87.0 days later. HP Enterprise orders the same product every 36.0 weeks, with demand roughly 511730.0 units and variability of 105476.0 units, also expecting delivery in 27.0 weeks. For that account, if inventory is unavailable, the sale is lost. Those units sell at $4966.497 per unit, and payment is received 64.0 days after delivery. The model runs for a 100-week horizon, with 10 replications.

