actly 22.0 days after receipt, and capped at 901371 units per delivery. ChipCo supplies memory_chip with an exponential lead time averaging 6.0 hours, at $280.281 per unit, with payment due between 47.0 days and 70.0 days. Production is centered at the Manufacturing Site, which manages frame_structure, memory_chip, heat_sink, glass_fiber, and EV_battery inventory. The main conversion step there is called cutting: it takes heat_sink, frame_structure, memory_chip, and glass_fiber as inputs and turns them into EV_battery units on a fixed 3.0-hour cycle, using the Welding Station. That Welding Station has capacity for 4 units and works with a service time ranging from 4.544 hours to 6.588 hours, at an operating cost of $13.089 per hour. There is also an Assembly Line with capacity for 5 units, an exponential processing time averaging 3.883 hours, and an operating cost of $6.85 per hour; it is subject to failures, typically running for 229.488 hours before going down, then staying offline for exactly 31.48 hours. Demand comes from two customers. Ross Associates orders EV_battery units with arrivals anywhere from 8.0 hours to 18.0 hours apart, and each request is for a constant 4051378.0 units. Their delivery expectation is between 5.0 hours and 22.0 hours, and the business follows a partial backorder approach for them, meaning available units ship immediately and the rest waits. Ross Associates pays between 52.0 days and 78.0 days after receipt, and the selling price is $3231.578 per unit. Infineon Technologies also buys EV_battery units, but with order arrivals averaging 20.0 hours; demand is centered on 2157232.0 units with variability of 643718.0 units, customer lead time is exactly 29.0 hours, and if stock is unavailable the sale is lost. Their price point is much lower at $808.161 per unit, with payment made exactly 36.0 days later. The model runs for a horizon of 730 hours with 1 replication.

