or_array, motor_assembly, microprocessor, and aluminum_sheet to produce drone. That operation takes exactly 27.0 days and depends on the Injection Molder, which has capacity for 4 units, a consistent 6.48-day service time, batching turned on at 53 units, a maximum wait of 14.868 days, and an operating cost of $8.724 per day. Starting stock is very large for the semi-finished items: 3285858 units of motor_assembly, with a holding cost of $8.54 per unit and a 1-day review cycle; 3171794 units of sensor_array, with a shortage cost of $6.735 per unit and reviews every 1 day; and 365386 units of display_panel. Finished drone inventory begins at 125165 units, with a shortage penalty of $16.015 per unit and review every 1 day. Customer demand comes from two major accounts. NXP Semiconductors places drone orders somewhere between every 14.0 and 50.0 days, with order size ranging from 92542.0 to 838283.0 units, and expects delivery in 9.0 to 22.0 days. The drone sells to NXP Semiconductors at $4807.102 per unit, and payment arrives between 21.0 and 51.0 days; if stock runs short, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. Sony Electronics orders drone exactly every 12.0 days, with demand varying from 245044.0 to 1740905.0 units, a fixed customer lead time of 4.0 days, a selling price of $2782.238 per unit, and payment 71.0 days after delivery. For Sony Electronics, unmet demand is simply lost. The physical flow is straightforward: all four suppliers feed Assembly Hub, and completed drone units move from Assembly Hub to the Logistics Center.

