ms, and can provide up to 89602 units per order. FastChip Corp. is the other resin source, effectively delivering in exactly 1.0 day, charging $6.927 per unit, allowing 11.0 days for payment, and supporting up to 564525 units per order; shipments from FastChip Corp. to Plant A take between 2.0 days and 4.0 days in transit. Carbon_fiber comes from three suppliers: Memory Star ships with a fixed 27.0-day lead time at $381.53 per unit, payment due between 8.0 days and 41.0 days after receipt, and a maximum order size of 151795 units, with transport to Plant A ranging from 7.0 days to 13.0 days; BatteryTech works on a 14.0-day to 19.0-day lead time, charges $467.704 per unit, and uses payment terms between 46.0 days and 76.0 days; PrecisionParts delivers carbon_fiber in exactly 14.0 days, at $13.983 per unit, with payment due anywhere from 28.0 days to 89.0 days. Manufacturing is split across 2 activities at Plant A. The molding step converts resin, frame_structure, and carbon_fiber into EV_battery in a fixed 6.0-day cycle and requires the Quality Scanner. That scanner has a capacity of 2 units, processes work in exactly 2.127 days, operates at $11.893 per day, stays up for an average of 19.276 days, and when it fails it is down for 42.528 days flat. The polishing step produces router from cooling_unit, display_panel, resin, and carbon_fiber, with cycle times varying between 4.0 days and 13.0 days; this part of the process does not call for an additional named resource. Plant A also works alongside a Storage Facility warehouse, and both EV_battery and router are moved from Plant A into the Storage Facility. There is also a Conveyor Belt resource in the system with capacity for 5 units, a fixed service time of 8.705 days, and an operating cost of $7.217 per day. Demand comes from 2 major customers. Samsung Electronics buys EV_battery, placing orders every 29.0 days to 31.0 days and requesting between 1219305.0 units and 1839130.0 units each time. Customer delivery for Samsung takes exactly 25.0 days, the selling price is $310.709 per unit, payment is received 78.0 days later, and if stock is unavailable the sale is lost. Dell Technologies purchases router on a steady 24.0-day cycle, with demand fixed at 3611234.0 units per order. Its delivery lead time ranges from 3.0 days to 29.0 days, the unit selling price is $187.556, payment arrives after 88.0 days, and this account also follows a lost-sales policy. The modeled network links 5 suppliers—ChipCo, Memory Star, FastChip Corp., BatteryTech, and PrecisionParts—to 2 facilities, Plant A and the Storage Facility, and the scenario is evaluated over a 200-day horizon with 10 replications.

