ks at $384.524 per unit and is paid between 41.0 days and 64.0 days later. Inside the plant, there is already a very large stock of 2226277 units of cooling_unit on hand, with a holding cost of $5.157 per unit, a shortage cost of $4.91 per unit, and review every 1 week. Finished smartphone inventory starts at 87677 units, with a shortage cost of $2.067 per unit and a 1-week review cycle. Production at Advanced Fab runs through a coating operation that uses solder_paste, ceramic_substrate, aluminum_sheet, and cooling_unit to produce smartphones. Each production run takes between 15.0 weeks and 27.0 weeks and requires Test Equipment, which has capacity for 5 units at a time, a service time between 3.63 weeks and 7.528 weeks, and an operating cost of $0.737 per week. On the demand side, Dell Technologies places smartphone orders every 24.0 to 44.0 weeks, with demand centered around 2529006.0 units and varying by about 26529.0 units. Dell expects delivery in exactly 5.0 weeks, pays between 18.0 days and 41.0 days later, and buys at $795.63 per unit; any shortfall is backordered, so customers wait. Sony Electronics orders more frequently, with arrivals averaging 10.0 weeks, and its order size ranges from 1163238.0 units to 2206521.0 units. Sony’s delivery window is between 8.0 weeks and 15.0 weeks, payment is made 30.0 days after receipt, the selling price is $1074.227 per unit, and shortages are also backordered. The supplier group of SiliconWorks, ElectroParts, GlobalComponents, BatteryTech, PrecisionParts, and GlassCore Ltd. feeds Advanced Fab, and completed smartphones then move from Advanced Fab to the Storage Facility. The model runs over a 500-week horizon with 10 replications.

