 and 70.0 days and a limit of 155905 units per delivery. MegaSupply also ships copper_wire, typically in 12.0 to 15.0 weeks, priced at $122.758 per unit, with 51.0 to 80.0 days to pay and capacity of 979812 units; material from MegaSupply also takes 10.0 weeks to transfer into the Manufacturing Site. ElectroParts is the quickest-looking copper_wire option, with lead times ranging from 1.0 to 28.0 weeks, a cost of $83.586 per unit, and payment terms of 15.0 to 18.0 days. For epoxy_compound, PrecisionParts works on an exponential pattern averaging 16.0 weeks, charges $136.32 per unit, and offers payment terms between 29.0 and 70.0 days. NanoMaterials Inc. supplies the same material with an average lead time of 12.0 weeks at $211.755 per unit, payment between 25.0 and 54.0 days, and a 2.0 week transfer into the plant. Steel_rod comes either from Memory Star, with an average lead time of 8.0 weeks, a unit cost of $400.291, payment due 21.0 days after receipt, capacity up to 889240 units, and a 4.0 week transfer, or from FastChip Corp., which runs on a steady 17.0 weeks lead time, costs $471.922 per unit, gives 73.0 day payment terms, can deliver as much as 153609 units, and needs 3.0 weeks for transfer. Production is centered at the Manufacturing Site, which manages drone, display_panel, epoxy_compound, cooling_unit, steel_rod, power_module, and copper_wire inventory. Its main step is called coating, where display_panel, steel_rod, power_module, epoxy_compound, cooling_unit, and copper_wire are combined to produce drone output. That step takes between 4.0 and 23.0 weeks and uses the Injection Molder, which has capacity for 1 unit at a time, a fixed processing time of 1.606 weeks, and an operating cost of $3.558 per week. There is also a Robot Arm resource with capacity 3 units, an exponential service time averaging 8.305 weeks, and operating cost of $5.712 per week; it is subject to failures, staying up for a consistent 110.691 weeks before going down, then remaining unavailable for an average of 42.034 weeks. Once completed, drones move from the Manufacturing Site to the Warehouse. Customer demand comes from Bosch Automotive, which orders drone units with arrivals somewhere between 32.0 and 41.0 weeks, in a constant quantity of 2374433.0 units per order. Customer deliveries take between 16.0 and 29.0 weeks, and the fulfillment rule is partial shipment with backorder for the remainder, so available units go out immediately and any shortfall waits. Each drone sells at $1693.331 per unit, and Bosch Automotive pays between 29.0 and 72.0 days after receipt. The model runs over a 200 week horizon, with results collected after a warm-up period of 30 weeks; 5 replications are included in the setup.

