This network revolves around 2 raw inputs, aluminum_sheet and lithium_cell, which feed 1 built-up sub-assembly, lens_assembly, and ultimately 3 finished items: tablet, GPU, and industrial_sensor. The lens_assembly is made from 4 units of aluminum_sheet and 5 units of lithium_cell, while each tablet consumes 2 units of lens_assembly. The GPU is fabricated directly from 3 units of aluminum_sheet and 3 units of lithium_cell, and each industrial_sensor uses 4 units of aluminum_sheet plus 3 units of lithium_cell. On the sourcing side, aluminum_sheet is bought from GlobalComponents on a demand-driven basis, with replenishment triggered as needed; shortages on that item carry a cost of $41.638 per unit and inventory is reviewed every 19 hours. GlobalComponents delivers aluminum_sheet in a lead-time window of 11.0 to 27.0 hours, charges $418.833 per unit, offers payment terms of 53.0 to 85.0 days after receipt, and can supply up to 193342 units per order. Lithium_cell comes from PrecisionParts and is supplied on a fixed periodic schedule of 59142.0 hours, with deliveries arriving anywhere from 6.0 to 17.0 hours apart in the inbound plan. Its holding cost is $3.564 per unit and it is reviewed every 9 hours. PrecisionParts has an exponential lead time averaging 6.0 hours, charges $253.652 per unit, and is paid exactly 80.0 days after delivery. The route from PrecisionParts to Plant A adds a transfer time of 2.0 to 6.0 hours, while aluminum_sheet also flows from GlobalComponents to Plant A. At Plant A, the company manages tablet and lens_assembly inventory, and the key process is welding: lens_assembly goes in, tablet comes out, and the cycle is a consistent 27.0 hours using the CNC Machine. That CNC Machine has capacity for 4 units at a time, works with a service time ranging from 4.391 to 6.2 hours, runs batches of 83 units, waits up to 14.121 hours to form a batch, and costs $16.15 per hour to operate. Plant A starts with 4945663 units of lens_assembly on hand, where shortages cost $37.08 per unit and reviews happen every 1 hour, and with 258671 units of tablet inventory, held at $7.018 per unit with a 1-hour review cadence. The Fab site handles lithium_cell, aluminum_sheet, GPU, and industrial_sensor production. One Fab fabrication step turns aluminum_sheet and lithium_cell into GPU in anywhere from 1.0 to 28.0 hours, and another Fab polishing step converts the same 2 raw materials into industrial_sensor in 13.0 to 15.0 hours. Starting stock includes 4298890 units of GPU and 2725516 units of industrial_sensor. Finished goods move onward to the Storage Facility, with tablet shipped from Plant A and both GPU and industrial_sensor shipped from Fab. On the customer side, Dell Technologies buys tablet, placing orders every 11.0 to 17.0 hours, with demand centered around 1414452.0 units and varying by about 226911.0 units. Dell expects delivery in 10.0 to 12.0 hours, accepts backorders when stock is unavailable, pays $2517.076 per unit, and settles invoices in 42.0 to 77.0 days. Ross Associates purchases GPU on a steady 15.0-hour cycle, with order quantities between 738503.0 units and 2473657.0 units. GPU shipments reach Ross in 11.0 to 26.0 hours, and if stock is out, the sale is lost. The selling price there is $3637.538 per unit, with customer payment arriving 48.0 to 89.0 days later. Lenovo Group takes industrial_sensor orders every 48.0 hours, with demand ranging from 1680532.0 units to 4388822.0 units. Their promised delivery window is 11.0 to 23.0 hours, shortages are backordered rather than lost, the sales price is $1767.964 per unit, and payment comes exactly 56.0 days after delivery. The modeled network links 2 suppliers—GlobalComponents and PrecisionParts—to 3 operating locations—Plant A, Fab, and the Storage Facility—and the run covers a 100-hour horizon, with results collected after a 50-hour warm-up across 10 replications.

