nel and 1226966 units of drone, and performs a cutting operation that turns display_panel into drone in exactly 6.0 hours. Drone inventory is reviewed every 1 hour, with holding cost of $1.05 per unit and shortage cost of $32.468 per unit. Main Factory manages lithium_cell and camera inventory, begins with 1568337 units of camera, and runs an inspection process that converts lithium_cell into camera in a 6.0-hour to 23.0-hour range. Camera inventory is reviewed every 1 hour and carries a holding cost of $7.697 per unit. Pilot Line starts with 1552513 units of solar_panel on hand; that item is reviewed every 1 hour and has a shortage penalty of $10.46 per unit. There is also a Laser Cutter resource in the system with capacity for 5 units, an exponential service time averaging 3.295 hours, batch processing enabled at 42 units, a maximum wait of 9.086 hours, and an operating cost of $2.842 per hour. Outbound flow is straightforward: solar_panel moves from Pilot Line to the Distribution Center, drone goes from Plant A to the Distribution Center, and camera ships from Main Factory to the Distribution Center. Customer demand comes from three named accounts. NXP Semiconductors buys solar_panel, placing orders with exponentially distributed arrivals averaging 31.0 hours and a constant order size of 354657.0 units. Delivery to that customer takes between 3.0 hours and 22.0 hours, unmet demand is backordered, the selling price is $1321.97 per unit, and payment comes between 8.0 days and 42.0 days later. Dell Technologies buys drone, with orders arriving between 32.0 hours and 38.0 hours and demand centered on 4531175.0 units with variability of 164587.0 units. Dell’s fulfillment lead time is exactly 28.0 hours, available stock ships immediately and the remainder is backordered, the unit price is $2893.76, and payment is received 37.0 days after delivery. HP Enterprise purchases camera, with order arrivals between 26.0 hours and 53.0 hours and fixed demand of 1950192.0 units. 