.0 days. Keeping memory_chip on hand costs $6.581 per unit, shortages cost $49.687 per unit, and inventory is checked every 24 days. Inside the operation, the Production Center manages display_panel, sensor_array, memory_chip, silicon_wafer, lithium_cell, resin, router, and motor_assembly inventory. It starts with 4973640 units of sensor_array, 3509990 units of display_panel, 3417439 units of motor_assembly, and 2663410 units of router already in stock. The in-process inventories are watched closely: sensor_array has a shortage cost of $36.568 per unit with review every 1 day, display_panel has a shortage cost of $27.49 per unit with the same 1-day review, and motor_assembly carries a holding cost of $6.133 per unit and is likewise reviewed every 1 day. Actual router production happens at the Production Center through a cutting step that combines display_panel, sensor_array, lithium_cell, silicon_wafer, motor_assembly, memory_chip, and resin into router output, and each run takes a fixed 19.0 days. That step depends on the Welding Station, which has capacity for 3 units, a constant service time of 1.725 days, and batching turned on at 43 units with a maximum wait of 11.529 days. The station is not perfectly reliable either: uptime is exponentially distributed with an average of 118.825 days, downtime is a fixed 20.321 days, and it costs $15.453 per day to operate. On the demand side, ABB Industrial buys the router. Orders arrive every 14.0 days, with demand centered around 1784075.0 units and varying by about 40368.0 units. Customer lead time runs between 4.0 days and 10.0 days. If stock is short, available units are shipped and the rest of the demand is lost. The router sells at $41.825 per unit, and ABB Industrial pays between 28.0 days and 49.0 days after delivery. The model runs for 365 days total, with the first 30 days excluded as warm-up, and uses 10 replications.

