This network is built around laptop production, with Advanced Fab handling manufacturing and testing, while Regional Depot serves as the downstream warehouse. The supply base includes PrecisionParts, Process Go., MetalFab Inc., and FastChip Corp., and together they feed ceramic_substrate and plastic_pellet into the plant. A heat_sink is also part of the flow as a fabricated component: each one uses 4 units of plastic_pellet and 1 unit of ceramic_substrate. The finished laptop itself is fairly material-intensive, requiring 4 units of ceramic_substrate, 1 unit of plastic_pellet, and 2 units of heat_sink for each unit produced. For raw inputs, ceramic_substrate is sourced on demand and carries a holding cost of $8.696 per unit, a shortage cost of $19.764 per unit, and is reviewed every 4 days. Plastic_pellet is also replenished as needed, with a holding cost of $8.52 per unit and a review cycle of 11 days. Inside the plant, heat_sink starts with 474928 units on hand, with holding running at $0.115 per unit, shortage cost at $36.681 per unit, and inventory checked every 1 day. Laptop stock begins at 2939441 units, has a shortage cost of $9.582 per unit, and is also reviewed every 1 day. Supplier terms vary quite a bit. PrecisionParts provides ceramic_substrate with a fixed 22.0-day lead time at $197.925 per unit, and invoices are paid between 44.0 days and 50.0 days after delivery; shipments from PrecisionParts to Advanced Fab take between 1.0 days and 3.0 days. Process Go. supplies plastic_pellet with exactly 6.0 days of lead time, charging $359.668 per unit, with payment due between 28.0 days and 81.0 days, and transport to Advanced Fab takes 12.0 days. MetalFab Inc. is another ceramic_substrate source, averaging 12.0 days of lead time, priced at $251.834 per unit, with payment terms ranging from 20.0 days to 52.0 days, and it can supply up to 267349 units per order. FastChip Corp. also ships ceramic_substrate, typically around 19.0 days lead time, at $198.526 per unit, with net 9.0-day payment terms and a maximum order size of 382037 units; its transfer to Advanced Fab takes 9.0 days. At Advanced Fab, the key operation is called testing. It consumes ceramic_substrate, plastic_pellet, and heat_sink, and outputs laptop units. The cycle time for that step varies between 1.0 days and 19.0 days, using the Conveyor Belt resource. That Conveyor Belt has a capacity of 5 units, a service time ranging from 0.203 days to 1.619 days, and an operating cost of $5.45 per day. Once completed, laptops move from Advanced Fab to Regional Depot. On the demand side, Schneider Electric buys laptop units with orders arriving between 32.0 days and 41.0 days apart, and each order ranges from 1681347.0 units to 4006860.0 units. Customer lead time is fixed at 17.0 days, the selling price is $3445.13 per unit, and payment is received 11.0 days after delivery. If demand exceeds available stock, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. Bosch Automotive follows a different rhythm, ordering every 56.0 days flat, with demand between 2756653.0 units and 3261780.0 units. Its delivery promise is 15.0 days, the sales price is $2818.041 per unit, and payment comes between 13.0 days and 62.0 days after receipt. It uses the same partial shipment approach, where whatever is available is sent and the shortfall is lost. The model runs for a 500-day horizon with 1 replication.

