This network makes two end items, a medical_device and an EV_battery, using aluminum_sheet and microprocessor supply feeding three in-house component streams: display_panel, circuit_board, and lens_assembly. The display_panel is built from 3 units of microprocessor, the circuit_board uses 4 units of aluminum_sheet plus 3 units of microprocessor, and the lens_assembly has an empty bill of material. At the finished-goods level, each medical_device requires 1 unit of display_panel, 2 units of microprocessor, and 3 units of circuit_board, while each EV_battery consumes 3 units of aluminum_sheet and 2 units of lens_assembly. Aluminum_sheet is sourced on an as-needed basis, with carrying cost of $4.327 per unit, shortage cost of $45.86 per unit, and review every 1 hour. Microprocessor stock follows a min-max style rule, triggering replenishment below 715.16 units and building back up to 1905.42 units; its holding cost is $8.028 per unit, shortage cost is $13.926 per unit, and it is reviewed every 28 hours. On-hand starting stock is 3890627 units of display_panel, 2609257 units of circuit_board, 434358 units of lens_assembly, 1732663 units of medical_device, and 2054130 units of EV_battery. Circuit_board inventory carries $2.306 per unit in holding cost, $37.736 per unit in shortage cost, and a 1 hour review cadence, while lens_assembly has a holding cost of $4.206 per unit and is also checked every 1 hour. EV_battery inventory is held at $1.596 per unit, faces a shortage penalty of $14.775 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 hour. Supplier coverage is split across five sources. Process Go. sends aluminum_sheet to the Integration Center with an exponential lead time averaging 13.0 hours, charges $367.603 per unit, allows payment between 53.0 hours and 74.0 hours after receipt, and can supply up to 267184 units per order. Memory Star provides microprocessor to the Integration Center in exactly 14.0 hours, at $166.417 per unit, with payment due between 39.0 hours and 67.0 hours, and a capacity of 258612 units. NanoMaterials Inc. also supplies aluminum_sheet to the Integration Center, reliably in 5.0 hours, at $183.025 per unit, with 37.0 to 38.0 hours payment terms and a 305691 unit limit. FastChip Corp. ships microprocessor to the Integration Center with an exponential lead time averaging 25.0 hours, a transfer time between 1.0 hours and 11.0 hours, a price of $414.742 per unit, fixed payment at 68.0 hours, and capacity of 43411 units. ElectroParts is another aluminum_sheet source for the Integration Center, taking exactly 20.0 hours plus a 2.0 hour transfer, charging $480.535 per unit, and offering payment between 53.0 hours and 87.0 hours. Manufacturing is split between two plants and supported by a warehouse. The Integration Center manages microprocessor, circuit_board, display_panel, and medical_device inventory, and runs a cutting step that turns display_panel, microprocessor, and circuit_board into medical_device in a fixed 4.0 hour cycle using the Conveyor Belt. That Conveyor Belt has capacity for 5 units, works with service times between 4.998 hours and 5.287 hours, and batches 67 units with a maximum wait of 9.179 hours. It is not perfectly reliable either: uptime is a steady 494.833 hours before a downtime of 21.529 hours, and it costs $2.374 per hour to operate. The Main Factory manages aluminum_sheet, lens_assembly, and EV_battery inventory and performs welding, converting aluminum_sheet and lens_assembly into EV_battery in anywhere from 3.0 hours to 27.0 hours. The other listed shop resource, the Injection Molder, has capacity of 5 units, service time ranging from 3.011 hours to 8.602 hours, batches in lots of 10 units, waits up to 12.104 hours to form a batch, and runs at $11.138 per hour. Finished medical_device units move from the Integration Center to the Storage Facility, and EV_battery output goes from the Main Factory to the Storage Facility. On the demand side, Infineon Technologies buys medical_device with orders arriving exponentially at a mean of 8.0 hours, each order fixed at 2890208.0 units. Delivery to that customer takes between 1.0 hour and 2.0 hours, the selling price is $1344.62 per unit, payment comes exactly 63.0 hours later, and if stock is short, available units ship immediately while the remainder is backordered. Dell Technologies orders EV_battery on a schedule ranging from 17.0 hours to 36.0 hours, with order sizes between 1737401.0 units and 2736435.0 units. Its customer lead time runs from 13.0 hours to 28.0 hours, the sales price is $4160.025 per unit, payment arrives between 14.0 hours and 62.0 hours, and any unmet demand is fully backordered. Overall, the model includes Process Go., Memory Star, NanoMaterials Inc., FastChip Corp., and ElectroParts connected to the Integration Center, alongside the Integration Center, Main Factory, and Storage Facility, and it runs over a 730 hour horizon with 10 replications.

