an supply up to 330627 units per order. ChipCo is another silicon_wafer supplier, averaging 5.0 hours of lead time, charging $325.753 per unit, with payment terms between 16.0 days and 57.0 days. Copper_wire is also managed with a reorder point system: when stock drops below 916.64 units, it is replenished up to 2900.79 units. Its shortage cost is $9.811 per unit and review happens every 22 hours. PrecisionParts sends copper_wire to Plant A with a lead time between 12.0 hours and 21.0 hours, at $187.884 per unit, on 68.0-day payment terms, and the transfer leg takes between 2.0 hours and 9.0 hours. Plant A carries the inventories for display_panel, cooling_unit, silicon_wafer, camera, and copper_wire in one manufacturing stream, and for lithium_cell, laptop, memory_chip, and heat_sink in another. It starts with 3176341 units of cooling_unit, 993121 units of display_panel, 1517827 units of heat_sink, 1807138 units of camera, and 154234 units of laptop. Display_panel has a shortage cost of $44.516 per unit and is reviewed every 1 hour, heat_sink carries a shortage cost of $19.337 per unit with a 1-hour review cycle, camera has a shortage cost of $32.894 per unit and is checked every 1 hour, and laptop has a shortage cost of $15.163 per unit with the same 1-hour review. For production, Plant A runs a cutting step that turns copper_wire, cooling_unit, display_panel, and silicon_wafer into camera, with each cycle taking 23.0 hours and requiring the Robot Arm. That Robot Arm has capacity for 4 units, works at a consistent 1.199-hour service time, and costs $18.044 per hour to operate. The laptop side uses a molding step that converts lithium_cell, heat_sink, and memory_chip into laptop in 21.0 hours per cycle. The site also has an Assembly Line resource with capacity of 5 units, a fixed service time of 8.746 hours, and an operating cost of $10.548 per hour. Demand comes from Broadcom Limited for camera and Lenovo Group for laptop. Broadcom places camera orders every 28.0 