p to 106083 units per order. BatteryTech is the second carbon_fiber source, shipping in 6.0 to 29.0 days, charging $311.865 per unit, and requiring payment 51.0 days after delivery; its capacity reaches 876895 units, and that route into Plant A adds a fixed 12.0-day transfer. For solder_paste, NanoMaterials Inc. delivers in exactly 20.0 days at $412.592 per unit with net 22.0-day payment and a maximum order size of 274388 units. CarbonSupply Co. is the alternate solder_paste supplier, with exponential lead time averaging 9.0 days, a cost of $174.746 per unit, payment due in 34.0 to 87.0 days, and capacity of 663899 units; its transfer to Plant A is a consistent 4.0-day move. Lithium_cell comes from ChipCo or SiliconWorks. ChipCo ships in anywhere from 1.0 to 25.0 days, costs $134.204 per unit, and is paid 15.0 days after delivery. SiliconWorks is somewhat tighter on timing at 2.0 to 15.0 days, but more expensive at $254.525 per unit, with 41.0-day payment terms. Manufacturing is concentrated at Plant A. One operation there, welding, takes solder_paste, lithium_cell, carbon_fiber, and lens_assembly and turns them into solar_panel, with a cycle time between 6.0 and 8.0 days and a Laser Cutter required. The Laser Cutter has capacity for 5 units, processes work in 5.634 to 9.195 days, costs $7.059 per day to run, stays up for a fixed 181.69 days at a time, and when it fails, downtime averages 27.259 days. Plant A also performs testing to convert heat_sink and lens_assembly into camera, and that step takes exactly 13.0 days. The site also manages lithium_cell, solar_panel, solder_paste, carbon_fiber, lens_assembly, heat_sink, and camera inventory before finished solar_panel and camera units move on to the Warehouse. On the demand side, Infineon Technologies buys solar_panel with order arrivals between 25.0 and 41.0 days and demand volumes from 1497944.0 units to 3004411.0 units. Customer lead time is exactly 27.0 days, the selling price is $3537.29 per unit, payment comes 71.0 days later, and if stock is short, available units are shipped while the remainder is lost. Sony Electronics purchases camera on an exponential order pattern averaging 28.0 days, with a fixed order size of 1385851.0 units. Camera orders are promised in 15.0 days, sold at $4292.752 per unit, and paid 69.0 days after delivery, again with partial fulfillment and lost remainder if inventory is insufficient. Plant A also uses a Robot Arm resource in the operation environment, with capacity of 1 unit, a fixed service time of 8.076 days, operating cost of $2.695 per day, uptime of 131.128 days, and breakdown periods averaging 13.502 days. The model runs for a 500-day horizon, and results are taken after a 30-day warm-up period.

