d, Memory Star operates with demand-triggered replenishment, lead time averaging 29.0 weeks, a unit cost of $7.776, payment due between 45.0 and 59.0 days, and a delivery limit of 543906 units. MetalFab Inc. supplies the same item in 4.0 to 9.0 weeks at $369.999 per unit, with 84.0-week payment terms, a capacity of 843834 units, and transfer to the Pilot Line ranging from 4.0 to 13.0 weeks. Manufacturing is centered at the Pilot Line. One polishing step converts steel_rod, resin, and frame_structure into SoC in exactly 6.0 weeks, and that step requires the Quality Scanner. The scanner has capacity for 5 units at a time, works with service times between 5.872 and 6.925 weeks, tends to run for an average of 281.272 weeks before failure, then stays down for an average of 38.072 weeks, and costs $9.144 per week to operate. A second polishing activity turns frame_structure, battery_pack, and resin into solar_panel, with cycle times varying from 4.0 to 21.0 weeks. A separate assembly operation converts battery_pack into tablet in 11.0 to 22.0 weeks. The Conveyor Belt supports flow with capacity for 2 units, service time averaging 1.749 weeks, batching enabled at 18 units with a maximum wait of 2.037 weeks, and an operating cost of $6.199 per week. Customer demand is spread across three major accounts. Dell Technologies buys SoC, placing orders every 35.0 to 44.0 weeks for between 852641.0 and 4183164.0 units, expects delivery in exactly 14.0 weeks, and pays 22.0 weeks after receipt. The SoC sells at $177.963 per unit, and if inventory falls short, available units ship immediately while the remainder is backordered. Ross Associates orders solar_panel anywhere from every 28.0 to 44.0 weeks, with demand centered around 997360.0 units and varying by about 237638.0 units. Their requested lead time ranges from 5.0 to 20.0 weeks, the selling price is $70.916 per unit, payment comes in 14.0 to 35.0 weeks, and any unmet demand is fully backordered. HP Enterprise purchases tablet u