ry 25 hours. At Fab, the molding step turns glass_fiber, solder_paste, copper_wire, and display_panel into medical_device in exactly 4.0 hours per cycle. That operation depends on a single Quality Scanner with capacity for 1 unit at a time; the scanner works in batches of 35 units, can wait up to 11.533 hours to form a batch, and its service time ranges from 3.269 to 8.929 hours, with an operating cost of $17.019 per hour. Main Factory handles router production through its own molding activity, consuming gold_wire and display_panel and completing each run in anywhere from 5.0 to 29.0 hours. Fab manages display_panel, copper_wire, solder_paste, glass_fiber, and medical_device inventory, while Main Factory manages router, gold_wire, and display_panel, and both finished goods move onward to the Warehouse. On the demand side, Infineon Technologies buys medical_device, placing orders with exponentially spaced arrivals averaging 23.0 hours and requesting between 2526000.0 and 4015460.0 units per order. Medical_device shipments to this customer take between 1.0 and 15.0 hours, sell at $4857.507 per unit, and follow a partial backorder approach where available units ship right away and the rest wait; customer payment comes between 27.0 and 54.0 days later. Dell Technologies orders router every 47.0 hours, with order sizes between 2925097.0 and 3070741.0 units, delivery in 5.0 to 8.0 hours, a selling price of $4374.111 per unit, and payment arriving in 43.0 to 50.0 days, again with partial fulfillment and the remainder backordered. Lenovo Group also buys router, ordering every 40.0 hours for exactly 1676752.0 units at a time, expecting delivery in 14.0 hours and paying $1585.095 per unit with 61.0-day payment terms; in this case, if stock is short, available units are shipped and the balance is lost. The medical_device inventory is reviewed every 1 hour and carries a shortage cost of $32.712 per unit.

