entory. The polishing step converts microprocessor, silicon_wafer, frame_structure, and copper_wire into router output, and each run takes between 8.0 days and 13.0 days using the CNC Machine. That CNC Machine has capacity for 4 units, runs at a fixed 4.098 days per service cycle, and costs $12.157 per day to operate. A Conveyor Belt is also part of the setup, with capacity for 2 units, a consistent 2.031-day service time, and an operating cost of $8.78 per day; it stays up for 160.306 days at a time and, when it fails, downtime averages 40.907 days. On the inventory side, frame_structure starts with 195913 units on hand, with a holding cost of $1.206 per unit, a shortage penalty of $15.46 per unit, and daily review every 1 day. Finished router stock begins at 3441796 units, with shortage cost running at $16.244 per unit and review every 1 day. Customer demand comes from Apple Supply, which places orders for router every 13.0 days, each for exactly 2813564.0 units. Customer lead time is 17.0 days, the selling price is $4725.237 per unit, and cash is collected 64.0 days after delivery. If stock is unavailable, the sale is lost rather than backordered. The model runs over a 365-day horizon with 10 replications.

