r, and gold_wire to produce router, again on a fixed 13.0-day cycle, but without any named resource requirement. Both camera and router then move from the Pilot Line to the Distribution Center. On the demand side, TechCorp buys camera, placing orders every 17.0 to 18.0 days for a constant 2107451.0 units. Customer delivery for those orders is promised in 11.0 days, unmet demand is backordered, the selling price is $4323.007 per unit, and payment is collected 13.0 to 53.0 days later. Lenovo Group purchases router on a steadier schedule, every 37.0 days, with demand fixed at 580804.0 units per order. Router shipments take 17.0 to 26.0 days to reach the customer, the business ships what is available and loses the remainder, the selling price is $1279.463 per unit, and Lenovo pays 70.0 days after receipt. The modeled network explicitly links 8 suppliers—ChipCo, CarbonSupply Co., PrecisionParts, Process Go., Memory Star, GlobalComponents, PolymerSource, and MegaSupply—to 2 facilities, the Pilot Line and the Distribution Center. The run itself covers 500 days, with results collected after a 30-day warm-up period.

