This network revolves around a Processing Unit and a Warehouse, supplied by Memory Star, PrecisionParts, PolymerSource, MetalFab Inc., FastChip Corp., Process Go., and GlobalComponents. The basic inbound materials are microprocessor and ceramic_substrate, and those inputs support three sellable lines: SoC, drone, and GPU, plus an in-house built sub-assembly called display_panel. Each display_panel consumes 3 units of ceramic_substrate and 3 units of microprocessor. The SoC line uses 2 units of microprocessor per unit, the drone requires 2 units of display_panel, and the GPU is the most material-intensive item, needing 4 units of ceramic_substrate, 1 unit of display_panel, and 4 units of microprocessor. Microprocessor supply is brought in on a periodic rhythm, with replenishment volumes typically around 79005.0 units and varying by about 8931.0 units, and those deliveries are scheduled every 18.0 weeks. Ceramic_substrate is managed with a min-max style trigger: when stock drops below 1019.89 units, an order is placed to bring inventory back up to 3120.71 units. For ceramic_substrate inventory, holding runs at $9.691 per unit, shortage exposure is $14.842 per unit, and the stock position is reviewed every 14 weeks. Display_panel starts with 3964181 units on hand, carries a shortage cost of $10.242 per unit, and is checked every 1 week. Finished goods also begin with large buffers: 4423710 units of SoC, 2773959 units of drone, and 496381 units of GPU. The SoC shortage penalty is $27.91 per unit with a 1 week review cycle, drone shortage is $17.48 per unit with a 1 week review cycle, and GPU carries $0.587 per unit in holding cost plus $12.656 per unit in shortage cost, also reviewed every 1 week. On the sourcing side, Memory Star sends microprocessor shipments to the Processing Unit with lead times between 11.0 weeks and 25.0 weeks, at $319.34 per unit, and payment is due between 10.0 weeks and 63.0 weeks after receipt. PolymerSource also supplies microprocessor, reliably in 10.0 weeks, at $492.079 per unit, with 7.0-week payment terms and a maximum order size of 257300 units. FastChip Corp. provides the same material in exactly 16.0 weeks at $280.082 per unit, with payment due 23.0 weeks after delivery. GlobalComponents is another microprocessor source, shipping in 3.0 weeks at $479.604 per unit with net 42.0 weeks, and its lane to the Processing Unit adds an 11.0-week transfer. Ceramic_substrate comes from PrecisionParts, MetalFab Inc., and Process Go. PrecisionParts averages 27.0 weeks of lead time, charges $362.99 per unit, offers payment in 46.0 to 54.0 weeks, and can supply up to 34046 units per order. MetalFab Inc. averages 17.0 weeks, charges $236.134 per unit, gives 39.0 to 51.0 weeks to pay, and is capped at 611889 units per delivery. Process Go. ships ceramic_substrate in a fixed 14.0 weeks, at $39.086 per unit, with payment terms ranging from 12.0 weeks to 21.0 weeks. Inside the Processing Unit, one flow inspects microprocessor into SoC in a consistent 13.0-week cycle and uses the Welding Station. That Welding Station has capacity for 3 units, works with an exponential service profile averaging 1.259 weeks, runs batch processing with batch size 83 units, waits up to 16.544 weeks to fill a batch, and costs $13.651 per week to operate. A second flow assembles display_panel into drone in 15.0 weeks flat. A third flow fabricates GPU from ceramic_substrate, display_panel, and microprocessor, with cycle times ranging from 6.0 weeks to 28.0 weeks. The other listed shop-floor asset is the Robot Arm, which has capacity of 4 units, a fixed 5.396-week service time, and an operating cost of $2.497 per week. Outbound finished goods move from the Processing Unit to the Warehouse for SoC, drone, and GPU storage and fulfillment. Customer demand is substantial. TechCorp buys SoC, placing orders every 16.0 weeks, with demand roughly 2244147.0 units and variability of 471912.0 units. The promised lead time to that customer is 18.0 weeks, the selling price is $1893.387 per unit, and payment comes between 10.0 weeks and 27.0 weeks. If stock is short, available units are shipped and the rest of the demand is lost. HP Enterprise purchases drone every 53.0 weeks in fixed lots of 3596017.0 units, expects delivery in 6.0 to 16.0 weeks, pays $4638.243 per unit, and settles invoices in 27.0 to 68.0 weeks; any available quantity ships immediately and the balance is backordered. Lenovo Group orders GPU somewhere between every 15.0 weeks and 40.0 weeks, with demand centered around 2489589.0 units and varying by about 116413.0 units. GPU orders are delivered in 7.0 to 23.0 weeks, sold at $69.177 per unit, and paid in 48.0 to 78.0 weeks, with unmet demand fully backordered until supply is available. The model runs for a 730-week horizon, and 5 replications are used.

