s a shortage cost of $26.774 per unit. Supplier terms vary a lot. GlobalComponents supplies resin with a consistent 6.0-week lead time at $425.678 per unit and is paid 21.0 days after delivery. ElectroParts is the alternate resin source, with an exponential lead time averaging 9.0 weeks, a lower price of $281.577 per unit, and payment due anywhere from 17.0 days to 55.0 days after receipt. MegaSupply provides memory_chip with lead times between 6.0 weeks and 14.0 weeks, charges $66.693 per unit, allows 53.0 to 59.0 days to pay, and can supply up to 889487 units per order; shipments from MegaSupply to the Pilot Line take 12.0 weeks to transfer. ChipCo handles carbon_fiber with a fixed 21.0-week lead time, a cost of $367.365 per unit, 11.0-day payment terms, and a maximum order size of 464695 units. GlassCore Ltd. supplies copper_wire in 15.0 to 18.0 weeks at $12.527 per unit, with payment due between 52.0 days and 63.0 days. Manufacturing takes place at the Pilot Line through a welding step that consumes power_module, motor_assembly, frame_structure, resin, carbon_fiber, memory_chip, and copper_wire to produce GPU. That step runs in a 13.0-week to 14.0-week cycle and requires the Quality Scanner, which has capacity for 2 units at a time. The scanner’s service time ranges from 2.425 weeks to 9.244 weeks, it operates for a steady 326.418 weeks before failing on average, then stays down for an exponentially distributed 43.438 weeks, and it costs $4.596 per week to run. On the demand side, NXP Semiconductors places GPU orders every 18.0 weeks, with demand centered around 3185043.0 units and varying by 699858.0 units. Their delivery expectation is 28.0 weeks, the selling price is $3350.724 per unit, and under short supply the business ships what it can and loses the rest; cash comes in 17.0 to 77.0 days later. Bosch Automotive orders somewhere between every 29.0 weeks and 56.0 weeks, always for 910036.0 units, accepts a 9.0-week to 20.0-week customer lead time, pays $3655.731 per unit, and follows a partial-backorder approach where available units go out first and the balance waits; payment arrives 70.0 days later. Samsung Electronics orders every 49.0 weeks, with demand around 2026267.0 units and variability of 306444.0 units, expects delivery in 21.0 weeks, pays $1886.385 per unit, and if stock is unavailable the missed sale is simply lost; payment is received 68.0 days after delivery. The model runs in weekly time buckets over a 100-week horizon, with 10 replications.

