l_rod, CarbonSupply Co. delivers in 3.0 hours to 12.0 hours at $385.49 per unit with payment terms of 36.0 hours to 79.0 hours, while ChipCo operates in a 4.0-hour to 13.0-hour lead-time window, charges $320.199 per unit, offers net 15.0 hours payment, can deliver up to 927766 units, and its transfer leg to the plant takes 12.0 hours. Solder_paste comes from ElectroParts, GlobalComponents, and PrecisionParts: ElectroParts runs at 11.0 hours to 20.0 hours lead time for $316.511 per unit with 44.0-hour to 75.0-hour payment terms; GlobalComponents delivers in 10.0 hours to 21.0 hours at $479.035 per unit with payment due between 13.0 hours and 38.0 hours; and PrecisionParts has an exponential lead time with a mean of 15.0 hours, a price of $42.127 per unit, payment in 51.0 hours to 54.0 hours, and a maximum order size of 113684 units. Inside the Manufacturing Site, assembly is done using frame_structure, solder_paste, silicon_wafer, and steel_rod to produce solar_panel, and each production cycle takes between 12.0 hours and 14.0 hours using the Conveyor Belt. That Conveyor Belt has a capacity of 4 units, a service time averaging 5.267 hours, and an operating cost of $8.891 per hour. There is also a CNC Machine with capacity for 4 units, service time averaging 3.301 hours, and operating cost of $14.161 per hour; it works in batches of 62 units, waiting up to 14.31 hours to form a batch, and it is subject to failures with uptime fixed at 169.714 hours and downtime averaging 36.785 hours. Customer demand comes from Lenovo Group for solar_panel, with orders arriving every 44.0 hours and each order fixed at 2830280.0 units. Lenovo accepts delivery in 9.0 hours to 22.0 hours, pays $1410.951 per unit, and settles invoices 28.0 hours later; if stock is short, demand is backordered rather than lost. The model runs in hours over a 200-hour horizon, with results collected after a 50-hour warm-up period and 1 replication.

