This setup revolves around making 2 finished goods, drone units and medical_device units, using 4 incoming materials: silicon_wafer, lithium_cell, aluminum_sheet, and glass_fiber. A few in-house built sub-assemblies sit in the middle of the flow. Each battery_pack uses 2 units of lithium_cell and 5 units of silicon_wafer, each lens_assembly consumes 3 units of aluminum_sheet, and each cooling_unit requires 2 units of glass_fiber, 2 units of silicon_wafer, and 3 units of aluminum_sheet. On the finished side, every drone is assembled from 3 units of cooling_unit, 3 units of glass_fiber, 4 units of aluminum_sheet, and 3 units of silicon_wafer, while each medical_device needs 1 unit of lithium_cell, 4 units of lens_assembly, 2 units of battery_pack, and 2 units of cooling_unit. Silicon_wafer is sourced on an as-needed basis and carries a holding cost of $1.677 per unit, with stock reviewed every 29 days. It comes from Memory Star with an exponential lead time averaging 26 days at $28.735 per unit and payment due 81 days after delivery, or from PrecisionParts in exactly 15 days at $380.572 per unit with 12-day payment terms and a maximum order size of 909811 units; shipments from PrecisionParts also take 9 days to transfer to the Integration Center. Lithium_cell follows a min-max style replenishment approach, reordering when stock drops below 512.63 units and restoring up to 950.51 units, with a holding cost of $4.917 per unit, a shortage penalty of $35.737 per unit, and a 26-day review cycle. Supply can come from GlassCore Ltd. in a 6-day to 7-day range at $116.369 per unit with payment 83 days after receipt and capacity up to 662505 units per order, or from MetalFab Inc. with lead times between 7 days and 28 days at $192.457 per unit, payment due between 18 days and 48 days, and capacity of 705607 units. Aluminum_sheet is also managed by reorder levels, with a trigger at 1386.3 units and replenishment up to 3007.13 units; it has a shortage cost of $2.358 per unit and is reviewed every 16 days. CarbonSupply Co. supplies it in exactly 21 days at $456.826 per unit, offers payment terms between 29 days and 85 days, is limited to 108742 units per delivery, and requires 2 days to transfer to the Integration Center. MegaSupply is the alternate source, delivering in exactly 8 days at $373.075 per unit with net 35 days and capacity of 857207 units. Glass_fiber arrives on a periodic schedule in batch sizes ranging from 647 units to 52799 units, with deliveries every 5 days, a shortage cost of $32.531 per unit, and daily review every 1 day. GlobalComponents supplies this material with lead times between 1 day and 9 days at $214.063 per unit, 61-day payment terms, and up to 955335 units per order. Inventory is substantial in several parts of the network: battery_pack starts at 200289 units and carries $4.75 per unit in holding cost with a 1-day review interval, lens_assembly begins at 3505156 units, cooling_unit opens at 2076112 units with holding cost of $1.4 per unit, shortage cost of $8.03 per unit, and review every 1 day, drone inventory starts at 189440 units with shortage cost of $40.31 per unit and daily review, and medical_device begins with 4535817 units on hand. Manufacturing is split across 2 operating sites plus 1 warehouse. The Integration Center manages drone, aluminum_sheet, cooling_unit, silicon_wafer, and glass_fiber inventory, and runs the assembly step that turns cooling_unit, glass_fiber, aluminum_sheet, and silicon_wafer into drone units. That cycle varies uniformly between 14 days and 17 days and uses the Conveyor Belt resource. The Production Center manages battery_pack, cooling_unit, lithium_cell, lens_assembly, and medical_device inventory, and performs an inspection-stage build that converts lithium_cell, lens_assembly, battery_pack, and cooling_unit into medical_device units in a fixed 19 days, with no named machine required for that step. The Conveyor Belt has capacity for 1 unit at a time, works with service times between 2.75 days and 6.026 days, costs $1.235 per day to operate, and is not perfectly reliable: uptime is exponential with a mean of 144.058 days, followed by a fixed 43.18-day downtime. The CNC Machine is available with capacity 5 units, a consistent 0.305-day service time, and an operating cost of $10.537 per day; it runs for 133.521 days between failures and then is down for an exponential average of 33.734 days. Outbound flow is straightforward: drone units move from the Integration Center to the Storage Facility, and medical_device units move from the Production Center to the Storage Facility. Customer demand comes from 2 accounts. Ross Associates buys drone units, placing orders with exponentially spaced arrivals averaging 14 days, each order for 2400745 units, and expects delivery in 5 days to 29 days. The selling price is $3957.354 per unit, payment is received 86 days later, and if stock is unavailable the sale is lost. NXP Semiconductors purchases medical_device units, with orders arriving every 8 days to 9 days, demand centered around 3212953 units with variability of 168184 units, and a fixed 12-day customer lead time. These sell for $2430.373 per unit, cash comes in between 42 days and 82 days, and the business ships whatever is available while the remaining demand is lost. The network itself includes 7 suppliers, 3 facilities, a 365-day planning horizon, and results are measured after a 30-day warm-up period; the model is run for 10 replications.

