ceipt, and a maximum order size of 520525 units. Ceramic_substrate is also supplied periodically, with shipment sizes typically 58367.0 units with some variability of 11597.0 units, and deliveries occur every 7.0 hours flat. It is reviewed every 17 hours and carries a shortage cost of $40.828 per unit. NanoMaterials Inc. provides ceramic_substrate with lead times between 1.0 hours and 12.0 hours, charging $124.952 per unit, offering 62.0-day payment terms, and limiting supply to 331348 units per order. FastChip Corp. is the alternate source, with lead time averaging 16.0 hours, a cost of $495.266 per unit, payment due 25.0 days after delivery, capacity capped at 718650 units, and transfer to the Processing Unit taking between 4.0 hours and 10.0 hours. On the internal inventory side, the business starts with 3598192 units of motor_assembly, 4601019 units of circuit_board, and 143476 units of cooling_unit. Motor_assembly carries $1.853 per unit in holding cost, $29.983 per unit in shortage cost, and is reviewed every 1 hour. Circuit_board has a holding cost of $6.529 per unit and is also checked every 1 hour. Cooling_unit is reviewed every 1 hour as well, with holding at $1.714 per unit and shortage at $34.541 per unit. Finished goods are stocked too: laptop begins with 558481 units and has a holding cost of $2.76 per unit with a 1-hour review cycle; FPGA starts at 1736131 units with $0.944 per unit holding cost and review every 1 hour; drone opens at 721401 units, costs $7.453 per unit to hold, faces a $32.943 per unit shortage penalty, and is reviewed every 1 hour. Production itself is split across three manufacturing activities, all housed in the Processing Unit. The cutting step converts circuit_board, motor_assembly, and gold_wire into laptop, taking a consistent 21.0 hours and requiring Test Equipment. That Test Equipment has capacity for 2 units at a time, works with service times averaging 0.462 hours, and costs $18.061 per hour to operate. FPGA output comes from an inspection step that combines cooling_unit, gold_wire, circuit_board, and ceramic_substrate, with each run taking between 13.0 hours and 19.0 hours. Drone production uses a bonding step that consumes ceramic_substrate, carbon_fiber, circuit_board, and motor_assembly, and the cycle time ranges from 17.0 hours to 21.0 hours. The site also has a Robot Arm resource with capacity of 3 units, service time between 5.175 hours and 5.585 hours, operating cost of $19.445 per hour, uptime fixed at 41.123 hours, and downtime fixed at 16.921 hours. Customer demand is sizable and varied. ABB Industrial buys laptop, placing orders every 29.0 hours, with demand around 4700662.0 units on average and variability of 570189.0 units. Shipments to ABB Industrial take 4.0 hours, the selling price is $1343.548 per unit, payment arrives between 31.0 days and 58.0 days, and the policy is partial fulfillment—available units are shipped and the rest is lost. Bosch Automotive orders FPGA with arrivals anywhere from 7.0 hours to 25.0 hours apart and demand between 2599643.0 units and 3915068.0 units. Customer lead time is 7.0 hours, the sales price is $1853.73 per unit, payment comes in 38.0 to 58.0 days, and any shortfall is backordered. HP Enterprise purchases drone with order arrivals averaging 34.0 hours, demand ranging from 822631.0 units to 2867973.0 units, delivery in 12.0 hours, a selling price of $813.933 per unit, and payment due 65.0 days later; here too, available stock is shipped and the remaining demand is lost. Structurally, all 6 suppliers—ElectroParts, SiliconWorks, NanoMaterials Inc., Memory Star, GlobalComponents, and FastChip Corp.—feed the Processing Unit, and the Processing Unit sends laptop, FPGA, and drone to the Regional Depot. The model runs in hours over a horizon of 365 hours, with 10 replications.

