e. The Pilot Line handles coating for SoC production, taking microprocessor and motor_assembly as inputs and finishing each run somewhere between 13.0 and 21.0 hours. The second shared asset, Test Equipment, has capacity of 3 units, a service time ranging from 5.646 to 6.744 hours, a consistent 296.236-hour uptime, a 45.308-hour downtime when it fails, and an operating cost of $2.643 per hour. Material flow is straightforward: SiliconWorks ships copper_wire to Processing Unit, PrecisionParts sends microprocessor to Processing Unit, GPU moves from Processing Unit to Storage Facility, and SoC moves from Pilot Line to Storage Facility. On the demand side, Lenovo Group buys GPU, placing orders every 29.0 to 55.0 hours, with demand roughly 2420373.0 units and variability of 341094.0 units; customer lead time is exactly 21.0 hours, the selling price is $2783.774 per unit, payment arrives in 33.0 to 80.0 days, and if stock is unavailable the sale is lost. Apple Supply purchases SoC with order arrivals averaging 59.0 hours, a fixed demand of 3426672.0 units, delivery promised in 2.0 to 10.0 hours, a selling price of $1559.639 per unit, and payment received 62.0 days later; in this case, unfilled demand is backordered. The model runs for a 100-hour horizon, with results collected after a 50-hour warm-up period, and 1 replication is used.

