This network is set up to produce tablet devices through a fairly concentrated operation: 2 facilities, with the Pilot Line handling manufacturing and the Fulfillment Hub serving as the warehouse, supported by 8 suppliers feeding in silicon_wafer and microprocessor inputs. The tablet itself is built from several in-house sub-components and direct materials, requiring 2 units of lens_assembly, 1 unit of silicon_wafer, 2 units of microprocessor, 2 units of cooling_unit, and 3 units of circuit_board for each finished tablet. Upstream, each circuit_board consumes 1 unit of silicon_wafer and 5 units of microprocessor, each lens_assembly uses 4 units of microprocessor, and the cooling_unit is stocked as a fabricated component with an empty bill of materials. On the raw material side, silicon_wafer is managed with a min-max policy: when stock drops below 3950.61 units, it is replenished up to 9937.34 units. Its carrying cost is $7.696 per unit per week, shortage cost is $30.525 per unit, and inventory is reviewed every 3 weeks. Microprocessor supply is demand-driven, with no preset reorder point; its holding cost runs at $4.1 per unit per week and the stock position is reviewed every 8 weeks. The starting on-hand balances are very large for the in-process and finished goods stages, with 2654012 units of circuit_board, 2253765 units of lens_assembly, 4680926 units of cooling_unit, and 3785516 units of tablet already in inventory. Circuit_board holding costs are $7.078 per unit per week with review every 1 week, lens_assembly carries a shortage penalty of $12.471 per unit and is also checked every 1 week, cooling_unit has a holding cost of $6.778 per unit per week plus a shortage cost of $11.525 per unit with 1-week reviews, and finished tablet inventory costs $0.652 per unit per week with review every 1 week. Supplier relationships are quite mixed. For silicon_wafer, FastChip Corp. delivers to the Pilot Line in exactly 20.0 weeks at $432.979 per unit, offers payment terms between 24.0 and 39.0 days after receipt, and can supply up to 867401 units per order. BatteryTech is another source at $400.436 per unit, with lead times ranging from 1.0 to 16.0 weeks, fixed 14.0-day payment terms, and a maximum order size of 621453 units. Process Go. supplies the same material at $370.447 per unit, taking between 9.0 and 29.0 weeks, with invoices due between 24.0 and 65.0 days, and a capacity of 239069 units. GlassCore Ltd. is the quickest steady source for silicon_wafer, taking 7.0 weeks flat at $265.454 per unit, with payment due between 24.0 and 52.0 days. For microprocessor purchases, GlobalComponents ships in anywhere from 3.0 to 4.0 weeks at $338.154 per unit, with payment due between 51.0 and 63.0 days, and the transfer from supplier to Pilot Line takes between 3.0 and 12.0 weeks. Memory Star is priced at $195.386 per unit, with supplier lead time between 1.0 and 14.0 weeks, net 73.0 days for payment, and a 4.0-week transfer to the plant. MetalFab Inc. is slower and more expensive, taking 17.0 to 22.0 weeks at $393.224 per unit with 80.0-day payment terms. ElectroParts offers microprocessor supply at $181.775 per unit, arrives in exactly 26.0 weeks, gives 49.0-day payment terms, can supply up to 799880 units per delivery, and its transfer to Pilot Line ranges from 5.0 to 13.0 weeks. Manufacturing is done at the Pilot Line through a bonding step that consumes lens_assembly, silicon_wafer, microprocessor, cooling_unit, and circuit_board to produce tablet output, and every production cycle takes exactly 1.0 week. That operation depends on 1 Assembly Line, so there is a single effective processing resource. The line’s service time averages 0.724 weeks, it runs with an uptime averaging 123.085 weeks before failure, and when it goes down the outage lasts 10.091 weeks flat. Operating the line costs $5.924 per week. Once completed, tablets move from Pilot Line to Fulfillment Hub for downstream handling. Demand comes from Apple Supply, which places tablet orders with interarrival times averaging 54.0 weeks, and each order is for a fixed 3192154.0 units. Customer delivery lead time is 27.0 weeks, the selling price is $2866.983 per unit, and payment is received 53.0 days later. The service rule is partial fulfillment with backorder for the remainder, so available units ship immediately and any shortfall waits until inventory is replenished. The model runs in weeks over a 730-week horizon, with 10 replications.

