_fiber, with an exponential lead time averaging 10.0 weeks, a unit cost of $79.385, payment in 51.0 to 78.0 days, and a 5.0-week transfer. Steel_rod follows a min-max arrangement: when stock drops below 3984.66 units, it is replenished up to 7841.21 units. It has a holding cost of $9.128 per unit, a shortage cost of $40.413 per unit, and a 1-week review cadence. FastChip Corp. supplies steel_rod in exactly 13.0 weeks, charges $401.061 per unit, requires payment 21.0 days after receipt, and the move into the plant takes between 4.0 and 8.0 weeks. Inside the operation, some materials are first turned into sub-assemblies. A circuit_board uses 4 units of glass_fiber and 2 units of lithium_cell, while a frame_structure consumes 2 units of steel_rod, 4 units of silicon_wafer, 5 units of glass_fiber, and 2 units of lithium_cell. Inventory is already substantial for these in-process parts: 91069 units of circuit_board on hand and 2634957 units of frame_structure. Circuit_board is reviewed every 1 week and faces a shortage cost of $45.063 per unit, while frame_structure is also reviewed every 1 week and carries a holding cost of $8.139 per unit. Router assembly at the Manufacturing Site is tied to an operation called testing. Each router requires 1 unit of frame_structure, 3 units of steel_rod, 4 units of circuit_board, and 3 units of silicon_wafer. The cycle time ranges from 1.0 to 26.0 weeks, and it uses the Welding Station. That Welding Station has capacity for 2 units, works with an exponential service time averaging 8.269 weeks, and costs $9.483 per week to operate. The router stock position starts at 2251124 units, with a holding cost of $8.057 per unit, a shortage cost of $0.67 per unit, and review every 1 week. EV_battery production is also run at the Manufacturing Site, through a fabrication step that takes exactly 29.0 weeks per cycle and does not call for a named resource. Each EV_battery needs 4 units of silicon_wafer, 4 units of lithium_cell, 2 units of glass_fiber, 1 unit of circuit_board, and 3 units of frame_structure. Beginning inventory is 355809 units, holding cost is $1.728 per unit, shortage cost is $40.703 per unit, and reviews happen every 1 week. There is also Test Equipment in the system with capacity of 4 units, an exponential service time averaging 8.402 weeks, and an operating cost of $17.756 per week. It is not always available: uptime runs a consistent 495.854 weeks before a failure, and downtime then averages 18.577 weeks. Finished router units and EV_battery units move from the Manufacturing Site to the Storage Facility for downstream fulfillment. On the demand side, Broadcom Limited buys router units every 9.0 weeks, with demand centered around 3620714.0 units and varying by about 5487.0 units per order cycle. Customers wait if product is not available, so unmet router demand is backordered. Broadcom expects delivery in 16.0 to 17.0 weeks, pays $3701.021 per unit, and settles invoices 63.0 days later. Bosch Automotive purchases EV_battery units with arrivals somewhere between 17.0 and 33.0 weeks, and order size is roughly 3332310.0 units with variability of 20218.0 units. Its orders are also backordered when stock is short, customer lead time falls between 9.0 and 24.0 weeks, the selling price is $3444.927 per unit, and payment comes 16.0 days after delivery. Dell Technologies is the other EV_battery customer, placing orders between 10.0 and 46.0 weeks apart, with demand anywhere from 2014109.0 to 3072460.0 units. Dell’s delivery promise is a fixed 25.0 weeks, the product sells at $557.488 per unit, and payment arrives 26.0 days later. If EV_battery inventory is unavailable for Dell, the sale is lost rather than queued. The model runs in weeks over a 730-week horizon, with the first 30 weeks excluded from results.

