 unit, and is paid in 42.0 to 63.0 weeks. Inside Fab, starting stocks are quite large for the semi-finished items: 3158920 units of power_module, 3496233 units of display_panel, and 4107505 units of battery_pack, while router inventory begins at 2609529 units. Holding power_module costs $9.215 per unit with a 1-week review cycle. Display_panel carries at $7.901 per unit, has a shortage penalty of $24.668 per unit, and is also checked every 1 week. Battery_pack is much cheaper to hold at $0.509 per unit, again reviewed every 1 week. Fab manages display_panel, battery_pack, epoxy_compound, silicon_wafer, steel_rod, power_module, router, and plastic_pellet inventory, and its key step for the finished item is a testing operation that converts plastic_pellet, power_module, steel_rod, silicon_wafer, display_panel, battery_pack, and epoxy_compound into router output. That step takes between 11.0 and 15.0 weeks per cycle and requires the Packaging Machine, which has capacity for 1 unit at a time, runs at exactly 1.367 weeks of service time, and costs $12.117 per week to operate. Another available resource is the Conveyor Belt, also with capacity of 1 unit, with service averaging 2.071 weeks and an operating cost of $6.162 per week. Finished routers move from Fab to the Distribution Center. Customer demand comes from Samsung Electronics and Schneider Electric. Samsung Electronics places router orders with exponentially spaced arrivals averaging 17.0 weeks, and each order is somewhere between 720028.0 units and 2618099.0 units. The promised customer lead time is exactly 24.0 weeks, the sale is lost if stock is unavailable, the selling price is $659.081 per unit, and payment comes in 7.0 to 25.0 weeks. Schneider Electric orders on a steady 9.0-week cycle, asking for exactly 3341859.0 units each time. Its delivery expectation ranges from 17.0 to 20.0 weeks, and the policy is partial fulfillment: available units are shipped and any remainder is lost. Those routers sell at $1029.922 per unit, with payment received 85.0 weeks later. The model runs in weeks over a 200-week horizon, with the first 30 weeks treated as warm-up, and 10 replications are used.

