ion Center converts epoxy_compound and plastic_pellet into finished laptops in 10.0 to 12.0 days. Drone work is handled through an inspection-stage operation that consumes cooling_unit, plastic_pellet, and aluminum_sheet and takes exactly 7.0 days per cycle. Supporting this flow is an Assembly Line with capacity 5 units, exponentially distributed service time averaging 5.759 days, and an operating cost of $10.349 per day. Finished products then move from the Production Center to the Logistics Center for distribution. On the demand side, Siemens Digital buys FPGA with order arrivals averaging every 9.0 days, and each order falls between 971995.0 units and 3833537.0 units. Customer lead time is exactly 17.0 days, the selling price is $1331.472 per unit, payment comes in 36.0 to 39.0 days, and if inventory is unavailable, the sale is lost. Sony Electronics orders laptops anywhere from every 23.0 to 42.0 days, always for 765251.0 units, expects delivery in 19.0 days, pays $2174.002 per unit, settles invoices in 22.0 to 76.0 days, and also follows a lost-sales arrangement. Dell Technologies purchases drones with arrivals between 29.0 and 35.0 days apart, demand ranging from 979555.0 units to 2918123.0 units, a fixed 19.0-day customer lead time, and a selling price of $3880.835 per unit; cash is collected 37.0 to 40.0 days later, and unmet demand is lost here as well. The supplier group of GlassCore Ltd., NanoMaterials Inc., BatteryTech, SiliconWorks, ChipCo, MetalFab Inc., and PrecisionParts all feed the Production Center, while FPGA, laptop, and drone shipments flow onward to the Logistics Center. The scenario is evaluated over a 730-day horizon, with results counted after a 30-day warm-up period, using 1 replication.

