This network is set up to build routers, with the Integration Center acting as the manufacturing hub and the Logistics Center handling downstream storage and movement. The plant manages gold_wire, ceramic_substrate, display_panel, heat_sink, lens_assembly, and finished router inventory in one place. The router itself is a fairly component-heavy item: each router uses 4 units of heat_sink, 2 units of display_panel, 3 units of lens_assembly, 1 unit of ceramic_substrate, and 4 units of gold_wire. Upstream, those sub-assemblies are also material-intensive. A display_panel consumes 4 units of gold_wire, a heat_sink uses 5 units of ceramic_substrate, and a lens_assembly requires 4 units of gold_wire plus 1 unit of ceramic_substrate. For raw inputs, ceramic_substrate follows a min-max replenishment logic, with orders triggered when stock drops below 2044.79 units and raised back to 2280.47 units; it carries a holding cost of $5.843 per unit, a shortage penalty of $10.1 per unit, and is reviewed every 24 days. Gold_wire is handled the same way operationally, reordered below 3134.16 units and topped up to 7613.84 units, with a shortage cost of $8.229 per unit and a 9-day review cadence. On-hand balances are very large for the semi-finished and finished stock: 4311152 units of display_panel, 3217721 units of heat_sink, 72499 units of lens_assembly, and 721131 units of router. Display_panel inventory is reviewed every 1 day and costs $3.125 per unit to hold, while heat_sink is also checked every 1 day and carries a shortage cost of $46.682 per unit. Router stock is reviewed every 1 day with a holding cost of $4.835 per unit. Supply is diversified. SiliconWorks provides ceramic_substrate in exactly 19.0 days at $314.402 per unit, with payment due 25.0 days after delivery. GlassCore Ltd. also supplies ceramic_substrate, but faster at 14.0 days, charging $216.913 per unit; its freight to the Integration Center takes between 4.0 and 12.0 days, and invoices are paid between 26.0 and 83.0 days after receipt. PrecisionParts is another ceramic_substrate source with a consistent 29.0-day lead time, a unit cost of $493.636, and net 67.0-day payment. BatteryTech ships the same material in anywhere from 8.0 to 14.0 days for $290.142 per unit, with payment terms ranging from 47.0 to 86.0 days. CarbonSupply Co. adds another ceramic_substrate option, with lead time between 9.0 and 26.0 days, a cost of $471.443 per unit, fixed 66.0-day payment terms, and a maximum order size of 634537 units; transfer into the plant is effectively 1.0 day flat. For gold_wire, Memory Star delivers in 9.0 to 11.0 days at $380.929 per unit, then needs another 13.0 days to move the material into the Integration Center, and payment falls between 16.0 and 49.0 days. GlobalComponents is much quicker, with an exponential lead time averaging 1.0 day, though it is capped at 165946 units per order; its price is $472.48 per unit and payment runs between 44.0 and 45.0 days. ElectroParts offers a steady 12.0-day lead time, charges $192.901 per unit, allows 48.0 days to pay, and can supply up to 244751 units per delivery. Inside the plant, the key manufacturing step is bonding at the Integration Center, where heat_sink, display_panel, lens_assembly, ceramic_substrate, and gold_wire are combined into router output. That operation runs in a 10.0- to 17.0-day cycle and depends on the Assembly Line. The Assembly Line has capacity for 4 units at a time, works with service times between 0.98 and 6.739 days, and batches jobs in lots of 12 units with a maximum wait of 10.39 days before release. It is not perfectly reliable: uptime is exponentially distributed with a mean of 198.88 days, and when it goes down, repairs take 39.011 days. Running it costs $18.252 per day. A Quality Scanner supports the process with capacity for 3 units, an exponential service time averaging 8.818 days, uptime fixed at 481.57 days, downtime averaging 35.644 days, and an operating cost of $11.463 per day. On the demand side, all three customers buy routers. Dell Technologies places orders at intervals between 9.0 and 34.0 days, each for a constant 4785015.0 units, expects delivery in 5.0 to 21.0 days, pays $2129.291 per unit, and settles invoices 64.0 days later; if stock is unavailable, the sale is lost. NXP Semiconductors orders every 22.0 days, with demand centered around 3840756.0 units and varying by about 686136.0 units, accepts delivery in 11.0 to 29.0 days, pays $1791.115 per unit, and pays between 32.0 and 40.0 days after receipt; under shortages, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. Bosch Automotive arrives every 32.0 days with demand roughly 2310347.0 units and variability of 612645.0 units, expects a fixed 12.0-day customer lead time, pays $273.796 per unit, and remits payment after 34.0 days; whatever is available ships immediately and the balance is backordered. The network links all 8 suppliers to the Integration Center, and finished router output then moves from the Integration Center to the Logistics Center. The model runs for a 365-day horizon, with results collected after a 50-day warm-up period, across 5 replications.

