This network makes three end products—smartphone, GPU, and solar_panel—using raw inputs plastic_pellet, glass_fiber, and epoxy_compound, plus a few in-house built sub-components: sensor_array, display_panel, and motor_assembly. The build logic is pretty layered. A sensor_array consumes 3 units of plastic_pellet, a display_panel uses 1 unit of epoxy_compound, and a motor_assembly requires 4 units of glass_fiber, 5 units of epoxy_compound, and 1 unit of plastic_pellet. On the finished goods side, each smartphone uses 2 units of plastic_pellet and 1 unit of epoxy_compound, each GPU needs 3 units of display_panel, 4 units of motor_assembly, 1 unit of epoxy_compound, and 2 units of sensor_array, while each solar_panel takes 3 units of glass_fiber, 3 units of sensor_array, and 2 units of plastic_pellet. Plastic_pellet is controlled with a min-max style replenishment rule: once stock falls below 4358.74 units, it is brought back up to 10866.11 units. Its carrying cost is $5.862 per unit, with inventory checked every 24 days. Glass_fiber arrives on a periodic schedule in quantities ranging from 36210.0 units to 80672.0 units, with deliveries coming somewhere between 7.0 days and 17.0 days apart; it carries a holding cost of $3.088 per unit, a shortage penalty of $41.991 per unit, and a 10-day review rhythm. Epoxy_compound is also replenished periodically, typically 25900.0 units with variability of about 3446.0 units, and it arrives exactly 6.0 days after release; its holding cost is $8.506 per unit, its shortage cost is $25.713 per unit, and it is reviewed every 26 days. The business starts with 2132966 units of sensor_array on hand, with holding at $9.241 per unit, shortage cost at $39.659 per unit, and daily review every 1 day. It also carries 3672527 units of display_panel initially, 2442046 units of motor_assembly with $3.089 per unit in holding cost, $12.63 per unit in shortage cost, and a 1-day review cycle, plus finished stock of 3816834 units of smartphone, 3435608 units of GPU with $1.663 per unit holding cost and review every 1 day, and 1754145 units of solar_panel with $8.786 per unit holding cost and the same 1-day review cadence. Supply comes from four vendors tied into the network. PolymerSource provides plastic_pellet with a lead time between 15.0 days and 16.0 days, charges $132.061 per unit, offers net 23.0 days after delivery, and can ship up to 22626 units per order. Memory Star supplies glass_fiber with lead times ranging from 12.0 days to 26.0 days, charges $283.295 per unit, gives 43.0 to 54.0 days to pay, and can supply as much as 757521 units per delivery. MegaSupply handles epoxy_compound, usually arriving in 14.0 to 25.0 days, at $218.773 per unit, with payment due between 14.0 days and 53.0 days after receipt. MetalFab Inc. is the second plastic_pellet source, with an exponential lead time averaging 24.0 days, a much higher price of $344.42 per unit, payment due 48.0 days after delivery, and a maximum order size of 552289 units. Production is spread across three manufacturing sites and one warehouse. At Fab, the cutting step turns plastic_pellet and epoxy_compound into smartphone, using the Conveyor Belt resource; that operation runs in a 5.0-day to 8.0-day range. The Conveyor Belt itself has capacity for 5 units at a time, an exponential service time averaging 9.438 days, and operating expense of $16.665 per day. Assembly Hub manages GPU, display_panel, epoxy_compound, sensor_array, and motor_assembly, where the testing process converts display_panel, motor_assembly, epoxy_compound, and sensor_array into GPU in anywhere from 6.0 days to 18.0 days. Main Factory handles solar_panel along with glass_fiber, plastic_pellet, and sensor_array; its fabrication step takes those three inputs and produces solar_panel in a fixed 15.0 days. Storage Facility serves as the warehouse in the network. In terms of physical flow, PolymerSource sends plastic_pellet to Fab, Memory Star sends glass_fiber to Fab with a 3.0-day transfer, MegaSupply sends epoxy_compound to Fab, and MetalFab Inc. also feeds plastic_pellet into Fab. Finished smartphones move from Fab to Storage Facility, GPUs go from Assembly Hub to Storage Facility, and solar_panels move from Main Factory to Storage Facility. On the demand side, Schneider Electric buys smartphone, with orders arriving exponentially with a mean of 52.0 days and order size varying from 2817940.0 units to 4600715.0 units. Customers are promised shipment in exactly 26.0 days, the selling price is $4051.594 per unit, payment comes 28.0 days after delivery, and the fulfillment rule is partial shipment—available units go out and the rest is lost. Dell Technologies purchases GPU, with demand arrivals averaging 31.0 days and quantities between 1020356.0 units and 3616820.0 units. Delivery to Dell takes between 17.0 days and 28.0 days, the item sells at $361.01 per unit, payment is received in 39.0 to 83.0 days, and any shortfall is partially filled immediately with the remainder backordered. Sony Electronics orders solar_panel, with arrivals averaging 24.0 days and demand from 1173396.0 units to 1902171.0 units. Lead time to the customer is a fixed 21.0 days, the price is $3561.874 per unit, payment lands between 49.0 days and 80.0 days, and unmet demand is simply lost. Overall, the modeled network links 4 suppliers to 4 facilities, runs over a 100-day horizon, and is evaluated in day units.

