tic_pellet inventory and runs a fabrication step that takes 4.0 days flat per cycle, using the Laser Cutter. That Laser Cutter has capacity for 1 unit at a time, works with an exponential service time averaging 5.798 days, operates at $13.589 per day, stays up for 323.117 days at a stretch, and when it fails it is down for 41.38 days. A Quality Scanner is also in the system with capacity of 1 unit, an exponential service time with a mean of 8.483 days, operating cost of $18.929 per day, uptime averaging 353.148 days, and downtime of exactly 29.133 days. Starting inventories are very large: 942430 units of motor_assembly, 2713214 units of heat_sink, and 3317896 units of CPU. Motor_assembly is reviewed every 1 day, with holding cost of $2.794 per unit and shortage cost of $19.047 per unit. Heat_sink is also reviewed every 1 day and held at $2.283 per unit. Finished CPU inventory is reviewed every 1 day as well, with holding cost of $1.689 per unit and shortage cost of $30.251 per unit. On the demand side, Bosch Automotive places CPU orders with exponentially distributed arrivals averaging 41.0 days, each for 706514.0 units, and expects delivery in between 6.0 days and 28.0 days. Bosch pays between 10.0 days and 28.0 days after receipt, and the selling price is $138.828 per unit. If stock is short, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. NXP Semiconductors, by contrast, orders every 17.0 days exactly for 1462871.0 units, accepts lead times between 3.0 days and 11.0 days, pays in 81.0 days, and buys at $4195.341 per unit. For NXP, whatever is available ships immediately and the balance is backordered. The model runs for 365 days, and results are measured after a 50-day warm-up period.

