ss_fiber, and lens_assembly into FPGA, runs on the Assembly Line, and takes exactly 21.0 hours per cycle. The Assembly Line has capacity for 1 unit at a time, a consistent 4.806-hour service time, and an operating cost of $1.514 per hour. The testing step turns lens_assembly into SoC in exactly 15.0 hours per cycle. In parallel, there is also a Conveyor Belt resource with capacity of 1 unit, service times averaging 5.075 hours, and an operating cost of $6.318 per hour. Once completed, both FPGA and SoC move from Plant A to the Storage Facility. On the demand side, ABB Industrial buys FPGA, placing orders somewhere between every 28.0 hours and 29.0 hours for a fixed 2770412.0 units at a time. Customer deliveries take 25.0 hours, the selling price is $3206.136 per unit, payment comes between 28.0 hours and 35.0 hours later, and any shortfall is backordered until stock becomes available. Siemens Digital purchases SoC with order arrivals averaging 21.0 hours, each request fixed at 1498553.0 units. SoC shipments take between 11.0 hours and 17.0 hours, sell at $1739.022 per unit, and are paid 27.0 hours after delivery. If SoC inventory is short, Plant A ships whatever is available and loses the rest of the demand. The modeled network explicitly links SiliconWorks and ElectroParts as suppliers, Plant A and the Storage Facility as operating nodes, with raw materials flowing into Plant A and finished FPGA and SoC moving onward to storage. The scenario is evaluated over a 500-hour horizon with 10 replications.

