r unit, and payment is due 75.0 days after delivery. SiliconWorks is the second ceramic_substrate source, shipping in anywhere from 12.0 to 17.0 hours at $297.631 per unit with 57.0-day payment terms, and its route to the Integration Center takes another 7.0 hours. Aluminum_sheet can come from BatteryTech, which delivers in exactly 10.0 hours, charges $32.125 per unit, allows payment in 31.0 to 64.0 days, and can supply up to 522565 units per order; the transfer to the plant takes between 7.0 and 10.0 hours. The alternate aluminum_sheet supplier is GlassCore Ltd., with a fixed 4.0-hour lead time, a much higher cost of $479.67 per unit, and payment due 12.0 days after receipt. Carbon_fiber is available from MegaSupply in exactly 29.0 hours at $156.016 per unit with net 28.0 days and a delivery cap of 55171 units, or from MetalFab Inc., whose lead time is exponential with a mean of 4.0 hours, price is $85.361 per unit, payment falls between 20.0 and 54.0 days, and capacity reaches 306560 units per order. Lithium_cell comes from PolymerSource with lead time averaging 22.0 hours, a purchase cost of $70.937 per unit, payment 50.0 days after delivery, and a 9.0-hour transfer into the plant. Manufacturing is concentrated at the Integration Center. The coating step converts circuit_board and ceramic_substrate into camera in a fixed 17.0 hours and requires the Quality Scanner. That scanner has capacity for 5 units, processes work with an exponential service time averaging 4.104 hours, and runs in batches of 70 units or after 18.104 hours of waiting, whichever comes first. It also breaks down: uptime averages 337.177 hours, downtime averages 45.657 hours, and operating expense is $4.184 per hour. The molding step produces SoC from aluminum_sheet and carbon_fiber in exactly 20.0 hours with no named machine requirement. The testing step converts lithium_cell into laptop, with cycle time varying uniformly between 1.0 and 26.0 hours. A Welding Station is also part of the operation, offering capacity for 2 units, a fixed 4.046-hour service time, batching of 18 units with a maximum wait of 16.549 hours, average uptime of 390.162 hours, average downtime of 46.027 hours, and an operating cost of $9.111 per hour. Finished cameras, SoC units, and laptops then move from the Integration Center to the Warehouse. On the demand side, Apple Supply buys camera on a steady pattern every 13.0 hours, requesting exactly 2692021.0 units each time. Orders are promised in 26.0 hours, sold at $633.888 per unit, and paid in 17.0 to 87.0 days; if inventory is short, available units ship and the remainder is lost. HP Enterprise orders SoC with arrivals averaging 55.0 hours apart, demand centered around 4659194.0 units with variability of 243625.0 units, a customer lead time of 15.0 hours, and a selling price of $1153.53 per unit. Payment comes in 36.0 to 41.0 days, and any unmet demand is simply lost. Ross Associates purchases laptop with order arrivals averaging 37.0 hours, order size ranging from 294682.0 units to 4073976.0 units, and delivery promised in 11.0 to 24.0 hours. Those sales bring in $1839.269 per unit, payment is received 76.0 days later, and shortages are backordered rather than abandoned. The model runs over a 365-hour horizon, with results collected after a warm-up period of 30 hours.

