 per unit, allows payment between 41.0 hours and 58.0 hours, and can supply up to 295940 units per order. PrecisionParts ships gold_wire in a 3.0-hour to 17.0-hour range, bills $14.014 per unit, and has payment terms between 35.0 hours and 61.0 hours; its lane into the Integration Center also has a fixed 3.0-hour transfer time. Memory Star is the expensive backup for gold_wire, with an exponential lead time averaging 14.0 hours, a cost of $414.806 per unit, payment due in 22.0 hours to 79.0 hours, and capacity capped at 310463 units per delivery. Production at the Integration Center runs through an inspection step that consumes gold_wire, battery_pack, and steel_rod and outputs smartphone units. That activity uses the Robot Arm resource, which has capacity for 2 units at a time and a fixed service time of 4.923 hours. The Robot Arm is not perfectly reliable either: uptime averages 275.513 hours before a failure, downtime averages 15.651 hours when it goes down, and it costs $7.987 per hour to operate. Customer demand comes from 2 accounts. Ross Associates places smartphone orders with interarrival times averaging 19.0 hours, and order size is normally centered on 356345.0 units with variability of 86799.0 units. Their delivery expectation is exactly 22.0 hours, smartphones are sold to them at $1268.268 per unit, and payment arrives between 50.0 hours and 73.0 hours. If stock is short, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. Schneider Electric orders smartphone units every 27.0 hours, with demand ranging from 256925.0 units to 858492.0 units. Their requested lead time varies from 14.0 hours to 28.0 hours, the selling price is $670.257 per unit, and they pay between 19.0 hours and 89.0 hours after delivery. In their case, whatever is available ships immediately and the balance is backordered. The model runs in hours over a 730-hour horizon with 10 replications.

