er hour. A laptop needs 2 units of frame_structure and 1 unit of resin. Smartphone production uses a welding step that consumes memory_chip and runs in a uniform 11.0 to 23.0 hours, with each smartphone requiring 2 units of memory_chip. EV_battery output comes from a coating step using motor_assembly, taking between 6.0 and 19.0 hours, and each EV_battery needs 4 units of motor_assembly. The CNC Machine is also part of the resource base, with capacity for 3 units, an average service time of 3.507 hours, and an operating cost of $18.98 per hour. Inventory positions are hefty: laptop opens at 2135935 units with a holding cost of $3.194 per unit and a 1-hour review time; smartphone starts at 2802929 units with holding at $1.273 per unit, shortage at $22.129 per unit, and 1-hour reviews; EV_battery begins at 2199913 units with a holding charge of $6.685 per unit, shortage cost of $22.12 per unit, and the same 1-hour review frequency. Customer demand is also significant. ABB Industrial buys laptop, placing orders every 39.0 hours for 4070685.0 units, expecting delivery in exactly 16.0 hours; if stock is short, available units ship right away and the remainder is backordered. The selling price is $4100.062 per unit, and ABB pays 47.0 days after delivery. Lenovo Group purchases smartphone with order arrivals averaging 48.0 hours, a fixed demand of 1874106.0 units per order, and delivery promised in 10.0 to 15.0 hours; unmet demand is fully backordered. Smartphones sell at $1187.954 per unit, with customer payment received in 42.0 to 85.0 days. Schneider Electric takes EV_battery orders every 50.0 hours, with demand centered around 2930283.0 units and varying by about 253934.0 units, and customer lead time ranges from 17.0 to 18.0 hours; these orders are also backordered if inventory is not available. The EV_battery sells at $256.579 per unit, and payment comes in 38.0 to 60.0 days. Finished laptop, smartphone, and EV_battery units all move from the Production Center to the