ck of 3334532 units of motor_assembly, with holding cost of $2.848 per unit, shortage cost of $41.01 per unit, and a 1 hour review cycle, plus 1211375 units of sensor_array on hand initially, held at $1.574 per unit with a $22.62 per unit shortage penalty and reviewed every 1 hour. Finished goods begin with 4156171 units of medical_device in inventory, and holding that stock costs $4.008 per unit with review every 1 hour. Production is centered at Main Factory, a manufacturing site managing microprocessor, sensor_array, lithium_cell, glass_fiber, motor_assembly, and medical_device, while the Logistics Center serves as a warehouse downstream. The key shop-floor step is a cutting operation that consumes lithium_cell, sensor_array, motor_assembly, glass_fiber, and microprocessor to produce medical_device, and each run takes a fixed 13.0 hours using the Conveyor Belt. That Conveyor Belt has capacity for 2 units at a time, works with an exponential service time averaging 4.746 hours, and costs $14.352 per hour to operate. A Quality Scanner is also available with capacity of 3 units, an exponential service time with a mean of 5.708 hours, and an operating cost of $11.162 per hour. Demand comes from Schneider Electric, which orders medical_device with customer arrivals somewhere between 34.0 hours and 52.0 hours and a constant requirement of 3569605.0 units per order. Customer lead time is fixed at 24.0 hours, the selling price is $540.244 per unit, and payment is received 42.0 days later. If stock is short, available units ship right away and the balance is backordered. The model runs for 100 hours total, with the first 30 hours treated as warm-up, and it includes 10 replications.

