This network revolves around a Pilot Line supported by a Warehouse, with inbound supply coming from SiliconWorks, PrecisionParts, CarbonSupply Co., and MegaSupply. The plant works with 4 raw inputs—glass_fiber, silicon_wafer, gold_wire, and resin—and first builds a sub-assembly called motor_assembly using 1 unit of gold_wire, 3 units of resin, 4 units of silicon_wafer, and 5 units of glass_fiber. From there, the business turns out 3 finished items: each EV_battery needs 4 units of motor_assembly plus 1 unit of resin, each medical_device requires 4 units of silicon_wafer, 3 units of gold_wire, 2 units of glass_fiber, and 4 units of motor_assembly, and each industrial_sensor uses 1 unit of glass_fiber, 3 units of gold_wire, and 1 unit of motor_assembly. On the purchasing side, glass_fiber follows a min-max style replenishment rule, with orders triggered below 4340.74 units and raised to 11709.18 units. Silicon_wafer is supplied on a fixed schedule of 79717.0 units every 4.0 hours, with a holding cost of $4.688 per unit and a shortage penalty of $9.111 per unit, reviewed every 25 hours. Gold_wire is sourced on demand. Resin also uses a threshold-based restocking approach, reordered below 3403.96 units and topped up to 6791.07 units, with a shortage cost of $29.267 per unit and a 9-hour review cycle. Supplier terms vary quite a bit: SiliconWorks delivers glass_fiber in exactly 26.0 hours at a cost of $480.732 per unit, with payment due 52.0 days after delivery, and the transfer from SiliconWorks to the Pilot Line takes 2.0 hours. PrecisionParts provides silicon_wafer with an exponential lead time averaging 14.0 hours, charges $65.723 per unit, allows payment anywhere from 28.0 days to 74.0 days after receipt, and can ship up to 731125 units per order; moving silicon_wafer into the Pilot Line takes 7.0 hours. CarbonSupply Co. handles gold_wire with a steady 29.0-hour lead time at $202.599 per unit, and its invoices are paid between 46.0 days and 61.0 days later. MegaSupply ships resin in 14.0 hours flat, charges $456.91 per unit, offers 24.0-day payment terms, and is capped at 115362 units per delivery. Inside the plant, the starting stock is substantial: 1233843 units of motor_assembly, 4588318 units of EV_battery, 1037923 units of medical_device, and 1115121 units of industrial_sensor. Motor_assembly carries a holding cost of $6.192 per unit and is checked every 1 hour. EV_battery inventory is also reviewed every 1 hour, with holding at $6.8 per unit and shortage cost at $39.121 per unit. Industrial_sensor is reviewed every 1 hour as well, with a holding cost of $8.573 per unit. Production on the Pilot Line is split across 3 activities. The bonding step for EV_battery takes motor_assembly and resin and runs in a constant 29.0 hours, using the Injection Molder. That Injection Molder has capacity for 1 unit at a time, works with an exponential service time averaging 6.305 hours, and costs $2.951 per hour to operate. Medical_device is also made through a bonding operation at the same Pilot Line, consuming silicon_wafer, gold_wire, glass_fiber, and motor_assembly, with processing time varying uniformly between 17.0 hours and 24.0 hours. Industrial_sensor goes through a testing step that uses glass_fiber, gold_wire, and motor_assembly, and that cycle ranges from 1.0 hour to 15.0 hours. The second resource in the system is a Robot Arm with capacity for 4 units, a fixed service time of 9.945 hours, and an operating cost of $0.731 per hour. Finished goods from the Pilot Line move on to the Warehouse for EV_battery, medical_device, and industrial_sensor storage and fulfillment. Demand is driven by 3 major customers. Sony Electronics buys EV_battery, placing orders every 27.0 hours, with demand centered around 2505261.0 units and varying by about 562842.0 units. Customer lead time is 11.0 hours, the selling price is $2232.864 per unit, payment comes between 18.0 days and 56.0 days later, and if stock is unavailable the sale is lost. Bosch Automotive purchases medical_device with order arrivals ranging from 33.0 hours to 54.0 hours, demand averaging 198939.0 units with variability of 20930.0 units, and delivery promised in 7.0 to 23.0 hours. These units sell for $2067.368 each, payment arrives in 25.0 to 50.0 days, and available stock ships immediately while the remainder is backordered. Broadcom Limited takes industrial_sensor orders with exponentially distributed arrivals averaging 24.0 hours, and demand can land anywhere from 2028978.0 units to 4045920.0 units. The customer lead time is a fixed 26.0 hours, the unit price is $1473.09, payment is received between 41.0 days and 49.0 days, and the business ships what it has while any shortfall is lost. The model runs in hours over a 730-hour horizon, with 10 replications.

